SYNTH-0001♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donald♔Martin♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔RapidAI♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Milwaukee in February 2023. I watched nurses make judgment calls that no algorithm could have made. One of them noticed something in the way my mother was breathing that wasn't in any chart, wasn't a data point, and she flagged it to the attending before a secondary infection took hold. That's what I keep thinking about when I read through what HHS is proposing here.⏎⏎The question the RFI seems to be asking is essentially "how do we move faster" and I think that's the wrong starting point entirely. The assumption baked into this whole document is that speed of adoption is the goal and that regulatory friction is the problem. But what if the friction is doing something useful?⏎⏎I'm not opposed to technology in medicine. I use apps, I get the appeal. But there's a difference between a scheduling tool and a system that is feeding recommendations into a clinical decision at 2am when a hospitalist is covering forty patients. The accountability question alone should give HHS pause before issuing anything that looks like a green light to the industry.⏎⏎Who is responsible when an AI-assisted diagnostic tool misses something, or worse, confidently flags a false positive that leads to an unnecessary procedure? The vendor? The hospital? The physician who clicked approve on a recommendation generated by a model they don't fully understand and have no practical way to audit? I genuinely don't know the answer to that and I'm not sure HHS does either, which is exactly why accelerating adoption right now seems backwards.⏎⏎There's also the bias problem which I don't think can be waved away with language about "ongoing monitoring." Pulse oximeters were used for decades before researchers documented in late 2020 and 2021 that they were systematically less accurate for patients with darker skin. Medical devices can carry bias for a long, long time before anyone catches it, especially when the harm is distributed across a population that doesn't have strong advocates in the room when these systems get approved. AI models trained on historical clinical data will reproduce historical disparities. This is not a hypothetical concern.⏎⏎I work as a social worker and I've seen what happens when systems meant to be neutral end up encoding old assumptions. It's not dramatic. It's just a slow accumulation of worse outcomes for people who were already getting worse outcomes.⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to adoption and I want to register that some barriers exist for good reason. Physician resistance to certain AI tools isn't purely technophobia. Some of it is clinicians trying to protect the patient relationship from being intermediated by a black box system their institution licensed to cut costs. Reimbursement pressure is real and if HHS creates incentive structures that reward AI-assisted care without robust outcome tracking, hospitals will adopt these tools for financial reasons, not because they improve care.⏎⏎I don't have a detailed regulatory proposal to offer. I'm not a lawyer or a health policy expert. But I'd ask HHS to think seriously about whether the framing of this whole effort, which is oriented around removing barriers and accelerating timelines, is the right frame for a domain where errors cost lives. The people who will be harmed by a rushed rollout are not the people who will be at the table when the rollout gets decided.⏎⏎There should at minimum be independent post-market surveillance requirements with real teeth before any of the reimbursement changes the RFI gestures toward. And I mean independent, not self-reported by vendors or institutions with financial interest in showing positive results.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0002♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Moore♔♔Delaware♔♔United States♔National Alliance on Mental Illness♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in February of 2023, and I watched her doctors make decisions I still don't fully understand. Not because they were hiding anything, but because medicine is genuinely complicated and they were weighing things I couldn't see. They knew her. They had talked to her, examined her, read between the lines of what she told them. That's what I keep thinking about when I read proposals like this one.⏎⏎The question I want to address is the one about what actions HHS can take to accelerate AI adoption in clinical decision-making. My answer is: please don't. Or at least, please stop and think much more carefully than this RFI suggests you have.⏎⏎There is a real problem with framing this as "acceleration." The word itself assumes the destination is good and the only question is speed. But nobody has established that AI integration into clinical care at scale is safe. The studies I've read — and I'll admit I'm not a researcher, I'm a social worker with a master's degree, so I'm reading summaries and journal abstracts more than the raw data — suggest pretty mixed results. The tools work reasonably well on populations that look like the populations they were trained on, and they perform worse on everyone else. There was a study from 2019, I believe it was published in Science, where a widely-used algorithm was shown to systematically underestimate how sick Black patients were because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs. The company involved was Optum. That kind of bias doesn't announce itself. A doctor using that tool doesn't get a warning that says "this output may be racially skewed." They just get a number, and they trust it.⏎⏎What worries me is that acceleration means deploying these tools before we understand the failure modes. And when something goes wrong, who is responsible? The hospital? The vendor? The physician who relied on the recommendation? This diffusion of accountability is not a minor administrative concern. It is the thing that makes me most opposed to what HHS is describing here.⏎⏎I also want to say something about what medicine is actually for. I don't think efficiency is the right goal. I think care is the right goal. When my mother's pulmonologist sat down and explained to us what she was seeing and why she was choosing one approach over another, that conversation was medicine. It was not inefficient. It was the point. I worry that framing AI as a solution to physician burnout and administrative burden ends up treating the symptoms while making the underlying problem worse, because if we automate away the parts that feel burdensome, we also risk automating away the judgment and presence that patients actually need.⏎⏎I understand there are administrative uses of AI that seem lower-risk, like scheduling or coding or prior authorization processing, and I'd be more open to those, though honestly even prior authorization AI makes me nervous given how often those systems already deny care inappropriately.⏎⏎There's also something I haven't seen addressed anywhere in this RFI, which is what happens to the nurses, medical assistants, and support staff whose jobs get restructured or eliminated because of these tools. The RFI talks a lot about physician time but the people with the most direct patient contact are often not physicians, and they're the ones most economically vulnerable to automation. I don't see a single question in this RFI that asks about workforce impacts on non-physician healthcare workers.⏎⏎I'd ask HHS to at minimum require independent pre-deployment safety evaluations for any AI tool used in clinical decisions, conducted by parties with no financial relationship to the vendor, before any federal funding or reimbursement pathway is tied to AI adoption. That seems like a baseline that should not be controversial but somehow isn't currently required.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0003♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Clark♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔UnidosUS♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Des Moines in February of 2023, and I watched a team of nurses and physicians make dozens of judgment calls that no algorithm could have navigated. Small things. The way her attending noticed she was holding her left arm differently at 2am and ordered an additional scan that caught a secondary clot. That wasn't a data point. That was a person paying attention to another person.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS wants to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, my stomach drops a little. Not because I think technology is evil or that computers can't do useful things. I use computers. I understand that administrative burden in hospitals is real and that physicians are drowning in paperwork. I get it. But there's a difference between using software to schedule appointments and using an algorithm to inform whether a patient gets a procedure approved or what treatment pathway gets recommended. That difference seems to be getting blurred pretty fast in this conversation.⏎⏎What concerns me most, honestly, is the insurance side. The RFI asks about accelerating AI in clinical care, but clinical care doesn't happen in a vacuum separate from coverage decisions. UnitedHealth Group was sued in 2023 specifically over its nH Predict algorithm allegedly being used to deny post-acute care to elderly patients at rates that human reviewers would not have reached. That case is still working through courts. We don't even have a verdict yet and HHS wants to speed up adoption. That feels backwards to me.⏎⏎I'm not a doctor or a lawyer, and I'll be honest that I don't know exactly how federal certification rules work or which specific regulations govern AI tools in clinical settings. But I do know that when something goes wrong with an algorithm, nobody seems to be clearly responsible. The hospital says the vendor built the tool. The vendor says the hospital implemented it wrong. The insurer says the algorithm only flagged the case and a human made the final call. Meanwhile the patient or their family is left trying to figure out who to even talk to.⏎⏎The efficiency argument also makes me nervous because of where it leads. If an AI system can process prior authorization requests faster, that's good for administrative throughput. But if the system is trained on historical data that underrepresents certain populations, those patients are going to get systematically worse recommendations and nobody may notice for years. There was a study published in Science in October 2019 by Obermeyer and colleagues showing that a widely used health risk algorithm was significantly biased against Black patients. That study was about an existing tool, not a hypothetical future one. Tools like that are already in use.⏎⏎I think HHS should be asking different questions before it asks how to accelerate anything. Questions like: what is the liability framework when an AI recommendation contributes to patient harm. What auditing requirements exist before a tool can be used in high-stakes decisions. Whether patients have the right to request a human review of any AI-assisted determination that affects their care.⏎⏎Speeding up adoption without those guardrails in place isn't progress. It's just moving faster toward something we don't fully understand yet, and the people who will absorb the consequences are not the people building the tools or writing the policy. They're people like my mother, lying in a hospital bed at 2am, hoping that whoever is watching the monitors is actually watching.⏎⏎I don't know if HHS has the authority to slow this down or if market forces are just going to push it forward regardless of what any rulemaking process does. That uncertainty is part of what I find so frustrating about this whole♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0004♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Gonzalez♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔Partnership for Health Analytic Research♔♔♔♔♔♔My father spent three days in the ICU at St. Joseph's Medical Center in Stockton in February 2023, and I watched the hospitalist on duty spend maybe four minutes with him per visit because she was constantly reviewing alerts and flags on a screen, trying to clear notifications before moving to the next patient. That's not an AI story exactly, but it's where my head goes when I read about "accelerating adoption" of more of these tools. We already have a problem where the technology is mediating the relationship between doctor and patient, and the answer being proposed is more technology, faster.⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to adoption and how HHS can remove them. I want to register that some of those barriers exist for good reasons and should not simply be engineered away.⏎⏎When a diagnostic algorithm misses a cancer or flags a healthy person as high-risk for insurance purposes, who answers for that? Right now the answer is genuinely unclear. The physician who accepted the recommendation? The hospital that purchased the software? The vendor who trained the model on data that skewed toward certain demographics? I'm not a lawyer so I can't speak to liability frameworks in detail, but I know from following health policy reporting that there have been documented cases where sepsis prediction tools had significantly different performance rates across racial groups, and those tools were deployed widely before that was understood. Accelerating adoption means more of those situations happen before anyone catches the problem.⏎⏎There's also something I keep coming back to regarding workforce effects. Radiology residents, pathology residents, people training in clinical decision-making are going to be trained differently if AI is doing the interpretive work. That concerns me in a way that's hard to quantify but seems real. If you spend years learning to read chest films with an algorithm suggesting answers, what does your independent judgment look like at year fifteen when the algorithm fails or is unavailable? Medical training is partly about building intuition through repetition and error in supervised settings. I don't see HHS grappling with that question in this RFI at all.⏎⏎I'd also push back on the framing that administrative burden reduction is a straightforward good. Prior authorization reform, yes, there I can see a case for automation helping without much patient safety risk. But when "administrative" bleeds into clinical documentation and then into clinical decision support and then into treatment recommendations, the line gets blurry fast and I'm not confident that HHS has a clear framework for where automation is appropriate versus where it represents an unacceptable diffusion of human accountability.⏎⏎The comment period here closes and then presumably HHS uses public input to shape guidance. What I'd want to see is a requirement for any AI tool being used in a clinical context to carry a very specific disclosure to patients -- not a privacy notice that nobody reads, but something that actually says "a computer program contributed to this recommendation and here is the name of that program and the company that makes it." My father didn't know which clinical decision support tools were in use during his stay. I didn't know either until I requested records and saw references to a vendor system in the nursing notes. That seems like a basic starting point before we talk about accelerating anything, and I genuinely don't understand why it's apparently not already required.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0005♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Williams♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔National Organization for Rare Disorders♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was discharged from St. Luke's in Kansas City in February 2023 after a three-day stay for a cardiac event. During that stay, she was flagged twice by some automated system as "low risk for readmission." She was readmitted six days later with congestive heart failure complications. The nurse who called us afterward said she had flagged concerns manually but that the algorithm's output had shaped how aggressively the discharge planning team pursued follow-up. I don't know the name of the system. I'm not sure it matters what it was called. What matters is that a tool nobody in our family consented to or even knew existed played some role in decisions about her care.⏎⏎That's the thing I keep coming back to when I read about HHS wanting to "accelerate" AI adoption. Accelerate toward what, exactly? The problems with these systems aren't theoretical. The bias in sepsis prediction algorithms, the documented disparities in pain management tools that were trained on historically unequal treatment data — this has been written about extensively and the response from the health system has mostly been to keep deploying anyway and say they're monitoring for problems. I'm not sure accelerating that process is the right framing.⏎⏎I understand there are real administrative burdens on physicians. I know documentation requirements are crushing and that something has to give. But the RFI seems to treat the question of whether to accelerate AI adoption as basically settled, and is mostly asking how. That framing concerns me more than any specific policy detail.⏎⏎There's also something nobody seems to want to say plainly which is that when an algorithm contributes to a bad outcome, accountability becomes very hard to locate. The physician says they were following the tool's guidance. The hospital says the physician retained final authority. The vendor says the model performed within its validated parameters. My mother, or patients like her, are just left with a bad outcome and no clear path to understanding what happened or seeking any kind of remedy. I've looked into this and the liability question is genuinely unresolved in most states.⏎⏎I'd ask HHS to at minimum require that any AI system used in clinical decision-making be disclosed to patients in plain language before it's used in their care. Not buried in a consent form. An actual conversation, or at minimum a written notice that says this specific tool will be used and here is what it does. That seems like the floor, not some ambitious ask.⏎⏎I'd also ask that before any further acceleration, there be mandatory prospective outcome tracking by race, age, and insurance status for any AI tool used in clinical settings, with results made public on some kind of searchable registry. Not internal quality reports. Public.⏎⏎Whether those things are even feasible under the current regulatory structure I honestly don't know. I've tried to read through some of the background materials and it's dense. But my concern is that without something like that, we're going to keep having situations where the harms accumulate quietly, patient by patient, while the industry publishes studies about efficiency gains and reduced documentation burden.⏎⏎My mother is fine now, for what it's worth. But I think about the patients whose families aren't following up, aren't asking questions, don't have the time or resources to piece together what happened.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0006♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joseph♔Rivera♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Francis Hospital in Hartford in February 2023. I watched nurses and doctors make dozens of judgment calls every single day — adjusting medications, reading her expressions, deciding when to push harder and when to let her rest. None of that was data. It was presence. It was accumulated human understanding of a specific person in a specific moment of crisis. She survived, and I don't think an algorithm would have known what those nurses knew.⏎⏎So when HHS talks about "accelerating AI adoption in clinical care," I want to be honest: that framing worries me more than any particular technology does. Acceleration implies that speed is the goal. That getting AI into more hospitals faster is an inherently good outcome we should be working toward. I'm not convinced that's true, and I don't think the burden of proof has been met yet.⏎⏎The question I keep coming back to — and I think it's one the RFI doesn't really grapple with seriously — is what happens when the AI is wrong and nobody is accountable. Not in a theoretical sense. In a practical, legal, and human sense. If an AI-assisted diagnostic tool misses something and a patient is harmed, who answers for that? The hospital? The vendor? The physician who was maybe looking at eight other alerts at the same moment? I've read some of the studies on clinical decision support tools and alert fatigue, and the evidence that more algorithmic input leads to better outcomes is genuinely mixed. The 2019 work coming out of Stanford on sepsis prediction tools showed real-world performance that was substantially worse than the published validation numbers. That gap between controlled validation and messy deployment is not a small concern.⏎⏎What I'd actually want HHS to do is slow down and ask harder questions before promoting adoption. Not because AI has no place in medicine, but because the healthcare system right now does not have adequate infrastructure to catch failures, correct biases, or assign responsibility when things go wrong. We don't have standardized post-deployment monitoring requirements. We don't have mandatory adverse event reporting tied specifically to AI tools. We barely have transparency about which AI systems are running in any given hospital. Patients often have no idea.⏎⏎The bias issue also doesn't get enough attention in these conversations. Pulse oximetry was used for decades before researchers documented clearly that it performs worse on patients with darker skin tones. That wasn't theoretical bias. That was embedded in hardware, trusted by clinicians, and it took until around 2020 before it got serious regulatory scrutiny. Training data for AI systems carries similar risks at a much larger scale and a much harder-to-detect level. Accelerating adoption before we have real bias auditing requirements seems backwards.⏎⏎I understand there are administrative burdens in healthcare and that some AI applications might genuinely help with documentation or scheduling in ways that free up clinical time. I'm not completely opposed to any of this. But the framing of "acceleration" as the policy goal rather than "careful, evidence-based, accountable deployment" — that framing itself is a choice, and I think HHS should examine why it's being made and who benefits from it.⏎⏎The vendors pushing this technology have obvious financial interests. Hospitals under margin pressure have obvious incentives to automate. I'm not sure patient welfare is actually driving the urgency here, and I think HHS should be more honest about♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0007♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Martinez♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in October 2022, and what kept her alive wasn't an algorithm. It was a night-shift nurse named Delores who noticed her breathing had changed in a way that didn't show up on any monitor readout, who went and got the resident, who made a call that the chart wouldn't have suggested. That's the thing nobody seems to want to talk about in all this excitement about AI adoption.⏎⏎I've read through parts of this RFI and my honest reaction is that HHS is asking the wrong question. "How do we accelerate adoption" assumes the answer is yes, do more of this, do it faster. But where is the question about what we lose? Because something gets lost when you systematize clinical judgment into a tool that produces outputs a clinician feels pressure to follow, whether or not the tool is right.⏎⏎The bias problem alone should give everyone serious pause. There's documented evidence going back years that some widely-used clinical risk algorithms were trained on data that systematically underrepresented Black patients, leading to worse care recommendations for those patients in real settings. The pulse oximetry issue that got attention during COVID is a version of this same problem. These aren't theoretical risks. And now HHS wants to accelerate deployment of more tools built on more data of uncertain quality, into more clinical settings, faster. I genuinely don't understand how that's the right direction given what we already know.⏎⏎There's also something that I don't think gets said clearly enough: when an AI system makes a wrong call and a patient is harmed, nobody gets held accountable in any meaningful way. The hospital says it followed the vendor's recommendations. The vendor says clinicians retained decision-making authority. The insurer says it was just providing information to support the claim decision. Nobody loses their license. Nobody faces consequences that actually change behavior. Medicine has always depended on accountability as a check on errors and that whole structure falls apart when you insert a system that diffuses responsibility across enough parties that nobody is clearly responsible for anything.⏎⏎I want to be fair here -- I'm not saying AI has no place anywhere in healthcare. I can see the argument for certain narrow diagnostic support tools with strong evidence behind them, used carefully, with real oversight. But that's a very different thing from the broad acceleration HHS seems to be contemplating, which would push AI into insurance coverage decisions, into administrative functions that affect whether patients get care, into triage, into medication dosing recommendations. Each one of those applications carries serious risk and the RFI doesn't seem to treat them as distinct.⏎⏎The workforce question also doesn't get enough attention. If these tools are deployed at scale, what happens to the diagnostic reasoning skills of physicians who trained on AI-assisted workflows? There's already concern in medical education about what happens to clinical thinking when residents rely heavily on decision support tools. You can't accelerate AI adoption and simultaneously protect the kind of deep clinical expertise that caught my mother's breathing change at 3am.⏎⏎I'd ask HHS to reconsider the framing entirely. A request for information on how to ensure AI safety, how to build real accountability structures before deployment scales, how to protect patients who don't speak English or who live in communities historically underserved by health systems -- that would be a more honest starting point. The acceleration framing presupposes a conclusion and I think the evidence warrants a lot more caution than that framing allows for.⏎⏎Also I want to flag that the comment period for something this significant feels very short given the complexity involved and the fact that many patient advocacy organizations have limited staff to respond to federal RFIs in the same way that large technology vendors do.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0008♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔James♔Hill♔♔Missouri♔♔United States♔University of New Mexico School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February of 2023. I sat with her through most of it. What I remember, more than any specific treatment or test result, is how much depended on the night nurse reading her face and deciding something was wrong before any monitor said so. That nurse was right. The attending came in at 2 a.m. and caught something the automated alerts had not flagged.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS wants to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, my gut reaction is: slow down and think about what you're actually speeding up.⏎⏎I want to focus on just one piece of the RFI, which is the question about what regulatory actions could remove barriers to AI adoption. My concern is that the whole framing treats the barriers as problems. Some of them aren't problems. Some of them are the thing standing between a patient and a bad outcome.⏎⏎The push for faster AI integration keeps leaning on efficiency arguments. I understand why. Documentation burdens are real, administrative costs are genuinely crushing, and I don't want to pretend otherwise. But there's a conflation happening between AI that helps with billing codes and AI that participates in diagnosis or treatment recommendations, and I don't think the regulatory discussion is keeping those categories as separate as they need to be. Once you've built infrastructure to accelerate AI adoption generally, and trained providers to trust AI outputs generally, it becomes much harder to draw lines about where the AI's judgment ends and the clinician's begins.⏎⏎What worries me specifically about accelerating adoption before accountability structures are in place is the diffusion of responsibility that comes with it. If an AI-assisted system recommends against a treatment and the patient gets worse, who answers for that? The vendor has a contract limiting liability. The hospital says the physician approved the final decision. The physician says they were relying on a validated tool. I've read enough about how these disputes actually play out to know that patients and families frequently end up with no clear answer and no recourse. Speeding up adoption doesn't fix that. It just means more patients are in that position before anyone has figured out how to handle it.⏎⏎There's also the bias issue, which I know comes up constantly but I don't think it's been taken seriously enough yet. Several studies, including work published in journals like JAMA and NEJM over the past few years, have shown that some widely-used algorithms perform materially worse for Black patients than for white patients, sometimes because the training data reflected historical disparities in care access. The response from the AI developers is usually that they're working on it. That's not reassuring when the proposal on the table is to remove barriers to adoption right now.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no place in clinical settings. But "accelerate adoption" as a regulatory posture assumes the main obstacle is red tape. I think the main obstacle is that we don't yet have good answers to the accountability and safety questions, and moving faster doesn't produce those answers, it just means more patients are exposed while we're still working on them.⏎⏎The RFI asks what HHS can do to help. One thing would be to require, before any reimbursement incentive or certification pathway is created for a clinical AI tool, a publicly accessible, independently audited record of that tool's performance across demographic subgroups. Not a vendor-submitted summary. An independent audit. With the methodology published. That doesn't exist consistently right now and I don't see it being required under the current accelerate-first approach.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0009♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Torres♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Emory University Hospital in January of 2023. During that time, I watched nurses and physicians make dozens of judgment calls that I do not believe any algorithm could have made. There was one resident, maybe 28 years old, who sat with her for forty minutes one evening just reading her face, asking her questions, adjusting her positioning. He wasn't running a protocol. He was paying attention. That is what medicine is. Or at least what it's supposed to be.⏎⏎What worries me about this whole initiative is that HHS is framing the question wrong. The question isn't "what regulatory barriers are slowing AI adoption" -- the question should be "what evidence do we have that faster AI adoption produces better patient outcomes, and what do we lose on the other side of that ledger." I haven't seen that second question taken seriously anywhere in this RFI.⏎⏎I work in health policy, not clinical care, so I want to be honest about the limits of what I know here. But what I do know is that the bias documentation in commercial AI tools used for clinical triage is not a minor technical footnote. The work coming out of researchers like Ziad Obermeyer on how certain algorithms systematically underestimate the health needs of Black patients because they use cost as a proxy for need -- that's a structural problem that doesn't go away because you update a model. It gets baked in, and then it gets scaled.⏎⏎The insurance decision piece concerns me even more than the clinical care piece. There are already documented cases of AI-driven prior authorization systems denying coverage at rates that human reviewers would not replicate. The CIGNA case that ProPublica reported on in 2023 -- where physicians were reportedly spending an average of 1.2 seconds per case review using an AI-assisted system -- that should have stopped this conversation cold. Instead it feels like the response has been to ask how to deploy these tools faster.⏎⏎I understand there are real administrative burdens in medicine and that clinicians are burning out from documentation and paperwork. I'm not dismissing that. But the solution to physician burnout is not to hand diagnostic and coverage decisions to systems that cannot be held accountable in any meaningful way. If an AI system denies a claim and the patient dies, who answers for that? The vendor points to the hospital. The hospital points to the vendor. The payer says the system was advisory. This is what I mean about diffusion of responsibility -- it's not a theoretical risk, it's already happening, and accelerating adoption before we have any real accountability framework in place seems genuinely backwards.⏎⏎There's also something I keep coming back to that I don't know how to quantify, and I recognize that makes it easy to dismiss in a regulatory context. But the relationship between a patient and a physician involves something like moral weight. The doctor knows that a wrong decision has consequences she will carry. That accountability changes how people practice medicine. When you insert a layer of AI into that relationship -- and especially when that AI recommendation starts to function as a de facto default because it's easier and faster and legally defensible to follow it -- you change the nature of the responsibility in ways that I think will take a decade to fully understand, by which point the infrastructure will be so entrenched that reversing course will be nearly impossible.⏎⏎I am not saying AI has no role anywhere in health care. Radiology screening tools with narrow, validated use cases and robust audit trails are a different thing than a general clinical decision support system or an automated prior authorization engine. The RFI conflates these categories in ways that make me suspicious of the underlying assumptions driving the whole exercise.⏎⏎HHS should at minimum be asking Congress for a mandatory pre-market evaluation framework for high-risk AI tools before talking about removing regulatory barriers to adoption. The 510(k) clearance pathway is not adequate for this. Neither is voluntary guidance.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0010♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amanda♔White♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔Northwestern University Feinberg School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at UCSF Medical Center in February 2023 with what turned out to be a fairly complicated medication interaction, and what I remember most from that week is how much it mattered that her night nurse, a woman named Delores who had been doing that job for nineteen years, noticed something was off with her breathing at 2am that the monitors hadn't flagged yet. That's not a story about technology failing. It's a story about what medicine actually is when it works.⏎⏎So when HHS asks what regulatory actions it can take to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, my honest answer is: please stop and think about what you're accelerating toward.⏎⏎I work in healthcare administration, not as a clinician, so I want to be upfront about what I do and don't know. I'm not going to pretend I can evaluate the technical literature on diagnostic algorithms. But I've spent twelve years watching how health systems respond to incentive structures, and I have real concerns about what "acceleration" means in practice. What it typically means is that hospital systems adopt tools fast, the vendors get paid, and the careful evaluation happens later, if ever. The 2021 report from the Government Accountability Office on AI in healthcare noted that most health systems had no formal process for post-deployment monitoring of AI tools they were already using. That hasn't changed much.⏎⏎What concerns me specifically about the framing of this RFI is the assumption that the problem is slow adoption. The RFI asks about removing barriers. But some of what looks like a barrier is actually appropriate caution. A physician who wants to understand a tool before relying on it for patient care is not being obstructionist. A hospital that wants independent validation of a sepsis prediction algorithm before deploying it systemwide is doing exactly what it should be doing.⏎⏎There's also the bias question, which I think gets treated too casually in a lot of these conversations. Pulse oximetry overestimation in patients with darker skin pigmentation was a known problem for decades before it received serious regulatory attention, and that's a relatively simple device. AI systems trained on historically biased data will reproduce and potentially amplify those biases in ways that are much harder to detect and trace. I'm genuinely not confident that we have the infrastructure to catch those failures before they hurt people, and I don't think reimbursement incentives and streamlined certification pathways will fix that. If anything they'll create pressure to deploy faster than the safety work can keep up.⏎⏎I recognize there are real benefits to some AI applications, I'm not saying none of this technology should exist. Administrative burden in healthcare is genuinely terrible and if AI can help with prior authorizations or scheduling I'm not opposed to that. But clinical decision-making is different. The accountability question alone is unresolved: when an AI-assisted diagnosis is wrong, who is responsible? The physician who followed the recommendation? The hospital that deployed the tool? The vendor? Right now there's no clear answer to that, and moving faster doesn't solve it, it just means more patients are affected before we figure it out.⏎⏎The RFI asks specifically about what HHS can do to support healthcare workers in AI adoption. My view is that the most useful thing HHS could do is fund truly independent research on outcomes, not research funded by the vendors selling these tools, and require that health systems publish their post-deployment evaluation data before they're eligible for any AI-related incentive payments. That would at least create some accountability. Right now a hospital can adopt an AI triage tool, get the CMS bonus, and never report whether the tool actually improved outcomes or not.⏎⏎I keep coming back to Delores noticing my mother's breathing. That's not something you automate. And I worry that every layer of algorithmic decision support we add makes it slightly harder for the Deloreses of the world to trust their own judgment, or makes administrators less likely to value it, or makes♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0011♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Hill♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔Yale School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in October 2023, and I watched what it looks like when medicine works the way it's supposed to. A pulmonologist sat with us for forty minutes. He drew a diagram on a paper towel. He changed his mind twice based on things my mother told him about how she felt. That is what I keep thinking about when I read documents like this one.⏎⏎The push to accelerate AI in clinical care treats the efficiency problem as though it's the main problem. Maybe it is an administrative problem. I'm genuinely uncertain about billing software and scheduling, I'll grant that. But the RFI questions about accelerating AI adoption in actual clinical decision-making worry me a lot, and I don't think HHS has reckoned seriously with what gets lost.⏎⏎Here's what I mean. When an algorithm flags a patient as low-risk and that patient deteriorates, who made the error? The hospital that bought the tool? The company that built it? The nurse who trusted the score? The doctor who didn't override it? This isn't a hypothetical — there have already been documented cases with sepsis prediction tools where the accountability just evaporates into the system. Nobody is responsible because everybody was following the process. That diffusion of responsibility is genuinely dangerous and I don't see anything in the framing of this RFI that takes it seriously. The document talks about "governance frameworks" and I understand that's the regulatory language, but governance frameworks don't sit with a family in a waiting room and explain what went wrong.⏎⏎There's also the bias problem, which I know HHS is aware of because it keeps appearing in footnotes and background sections of these documents, but it keeps getting treated as a solvable technical problem rather than a fundamental one. Training data reflects historical clinical decisions. Historical clinical decisions reflect who got good care and who didn't. You cannot fully correct for that by tuning the model. A 2019 study published in Science found that a widely used algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients because it used cost as a proxy for need. That algorithm was already deployed at scale across hundreds of hospitals before the problem was identified. Accelerating adoption means accelerating the propagation of whatever the next version of that problem is before we find it.⏎⏎I work in public health, not clinical medicine, so I don't have standing to tell cardiologists how to read an EKG. But I do understand something about how interventions move through systems and how the incentive structures around health technology tend to favor deployment over evaluation. The companies selling these tools have enormous financial interest in getting them into hospitals quickly. HHS accelerating that timeline does not offset the interests on the other side. It amplifies them.⏎⏎I think the Department should be asking much harder questions about what evidence threshold is required before a clinical AI tool gets anywhere near a patient, and who bears liability when it fails. Right now the honest answer to the liability question seems to be "unclear," and that alone should give pause before any acceleration.⏎⏎The RFI asks what regulatory barriers slow adoption. But I'd want to know whether any of those barriers are actually doing something useful, like forcing a company to demonstrate that their product works before a hospital buys it under pressure from a vendor's sales team at a conference in♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0012♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patricia♔Williams♔♔Texas♔♔United States♔Partnership for Quality Home Healthcare♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was in the ICU at Vanderbilt University Medical Center in February 2023, and what saved her was a pulmonologist who noticed something was off about her color, her breathing pattern, something that wasn't in the chart numbers. He made a call based on that. She's alive. I don't think an algorithm would have caught it, and I'm not being sentimental about that, I genuinely don't think it would have.⏎⏎The question HHS is asking — what can we do to accelerate AI adoption — starts from the wrong premise. It assumes the bottleneck in clinical care is speed or efficiency, that doctors are just slow processors of information and AI can do the same thing faster. But a lot of what medicine actually is, is judgment under uncertainty with a real person in front of you whose life depends on getting it right. I don't see how automating that is an improvement.⏎⏎What concerns me most, and the reason I'm submitting this comment, is the question of who gets blamed when AI gets it wrong. If a physician makes a wrong call, there is a process. It's imperfect but it exists. If an AI tool developed by some company produces a recommendation that leads to patient harm, and the hospital adopted it partly because of federal incentives, the accountability just sort of dissolves. The company points to the physician. The physician says they followed the tool's guidance. The hospital says they were complying with federal recommendations. And the patient or their family is left with nothing clear to hold onto.⏎⏎I've seen this described as a "diffusion of responsibility" problem in some of the medical ethics literature and I think that's the right framing. It's not hypothetical either. There have already been documented cases of commercial AI triage and prior authorization tools producing racially biased outputs — the Optum algorithm case from 2019 is probably the most cited, where the tool systematically underestimated health needs for Black patients. HHS was aware of this. We accelerated anyway.⏎⏎I'm not saying there's zero use for any AI in any clinical context. I can see the argument for administrative tasks, maybe imaging analysis with proper oversight. But the framing of this RFI is about acceleration, and that's where I have to push back. The incentives being discussed — reimbursement changes, regulatory streamlining — will push adoption faster than evidence can possibly accumulate about what's safe. Hospitals are not going to say no to reimbursement incentives. Clinicians are going to be pressured to use tools their institutions adopted. The workforce implications of that alone are something this RFI barely touches on.⏎⏎My mother's pulmonologist spent probably fifteen years developing the kind of pattern recognition that helped him that night. If the next generation of trainees spends those fifteen years deferring to AI outputs, what does their judgment look like at the end of it? There's actually some research coming out of residency programs suggesting that over-reliance on clinical decision support tools is already degrading diagnostic reasoning in trainees, though I'd have to look up the specific citations to give you the exact numbers.⏎⏎HHS should be asking harder questions about safety infrastructure, adverse event reporting for AI tools, and liability frameworks before it asks how to accelerate anything. The reimbursement levers especially worry me because once you tie payment to AI use you've essentially mandated it in practice even if the rule doesn't say that.⏎⏎I'm not sure this comment will be read carefully given how many there probably are, but I wanted to at least say that not everyone writing in is enthusiastic about this direction.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0013♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patricia♔Scott♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February of 2023. I sat in that waiting room for most of those eleven days, and what I kept noticing was how much of the actual care depended on nurses and doctors making judgment calls that I genuinely could not imagine being reduced to a pattern-matching algorithm. The attending physician — I want to say his name was Dr. Okonkwo, though I may be misremembering the spelling — changed her medication regimen twice based on things he noticed when he walked into the room. Subtle things. The way she was holding herself, something in her eyes. He told me he couldn't fully explain it. That bothers me now, because I think HHS is trying to build a system that would require him to explain it, or worse, would replace that instinct with a confidence score generated by a model trained on somebody else's patients.⏎⏎I understand there are efficiency arguments for AI in clinical settings. I'm not completely dismissing them. But this request for information feels like the conclusion has already been reached and HHS is just collecting evidence to support accelerating something that hasn't been proven safe at scale. Accelerating is the word that worries me. Nobody is asking whether we should slow down and actually study what's already been deployed.⏎⏎The bias question gets almost no serious weight in discussions like this one, and it should. Models trained on historical clinical data are trained on a healthcare system that has systematically undertreated Black patients, undertreated women in pain, overtreated certain populations based on insurance status. If you accelerate adoption of tools built on that data, you are accelerating the reproduction of those patterns. The ProPublica reporting on COMPAS-style risk scoring in criminal justice contexts showed exactly how this works — a tool gets deployed, it produces racially skewed outputs, and by the time anyone documents the harm thousands of decisions have already been made. I don't see why clinical AI would be different.⏎⏎What I keep coming back to is the accountability problem. When Dr. Okonkwo changes my mother's medication and something goes wrong, there is a person responsible. That person went to school for a long time, has a license, can be sued, can be reported to a board. When an AI tool makes a recommendation and a physician follows it and something goes wrong — who is responsible? The physician who deferred to the tool? The hospital that purchased it? The vendor? I have not seen HHS grapple seriously with this question in any of the materials I've read. There's language about "human oversight" but that phrase does a lot of work it hasn't earned. If a physician is seeing 30 patients in a shift and an AI flags 8 of them with various recommendations, that physician is not in a position to independently verify each one. The oversight becomes nominal.⏎⏎I also think there's something being lost in how this conversation frames patients. The RFI talks a lot about care delivery and workflow and efficiency. It doesn't talk much about what it means for a patient to be assessed by a machine during a vulnerable moment. I know that sounds soft, maybe, but I think it matters to people in ways that won't show up in outcome metrics for years if ever.⏎⏎My specific concern about the reimbursement question — which seems to be where HHS is most likely to create real momentum — is that tying payment incentives to AI adoption will create pressure on smaller and rural hospitals to deploy tools they don't have the IT infrastructure to validate properly. A critical access hospital in western Kansas does not have the same capacity to audit an AI vendor's claims as Mass General does.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0014♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Clark♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February 2023 with what turned out to be a medication interaction. The attending physician caught it. Not a dashboard. Not an algorithm. A tired doctor who had seen something similar once before and trusted her gut enough to order a second round of labs when the first came back "within normal parameters." I think about that a lot.⏎⏎What I keep coming back to when I read about this RFI is the word "accelerate." Not evaluate. Not study carefully. Accelerate. And I think that word choice tells you something about where HHS's head is at right now, and it worries me.⏎⏎I want to address specifically the questions around barriers to adoption and what regulatory changes would help remove them. My concern is that framing safety guardrails as "barriers" gets the whole thing backwards. The friction in the system, the requirement that a physician review a recommendation, the requirement that someone be accountable when something goes wrong, those aren't bugs. They're doing something important that efficiency metrics don't capture.⏎⏎There's a lot of talk in the AI health space about reducing physician burnout by offloading documentation and decision support to AI tools. I'm not unsympathetic to that. Burnout is real and it's hurting patients too. But what I've read in the literature, and I'll admit I'm not a clinician, I'm a medical social worker with about 18 years in hospital settings, is that the errors these systems make are not randomly distributed. They perform worse on elderly patients, on patients with multiple comorbidities, on patients who don't fit the training data well. Which is to say, they fail most often on exactly the people who are already most vulnerable to being failed by the healthcare system.⏎⏎The ProPublica reporting on algorithmic bias in healthcare risk scoring, I believe that was around 2019, found that a widely used commerical algorithm was systematically underestimating the health needs of Black patients. That wasn't a fringe finding. That was built into a product being used at scale. And the answer from HHS at the time was not to pull back but to ask for voluntary commitments from vendors. I don't have a lot of confidence that voluntary commitments are going to be sufficient here either.⏎⏎I guess what I'd ask HHS to genuinely grapple with is the accountability question, because I don't think it's been answered. When an AI-assisted diagnosis contributes to a bad outcome, who is liable? The physician who approved the recommendation they didn't fully understand? The hospital that purchased the tool? The vendor who trained the model on data that didn't represent the patient population? Right now the answer seems to be "figure it out in court later" and that's not a governance framework, that's an abdication of one.⏎⏎The efficiency gains being promised are real in some narrow sense. I don't want to pretend otherwise. But I've seen what happens in hospital systems when efficiency becomes the primary metric for care quality, and I don't think any of us should be in a hurry to get there faster.⏎⏎I'm also honestly not sure what to make of the administrative AI piece, whether this RFI is also covering things like prior authorization and claims processing. If it is, that's a separate set of concerns I'd want to raise, because the insurance side is where I've seen the most concrete harm in my own work, and the accountability gaps there are if anything even larger than on the clinical side.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0015♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔James♔Moore♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at St. Luke's in Cedar Rapids in February of 2023, and I watched what happened when the clinical decision support software flagged her pain medication as potentially duplicative and delayed her getting relief for almost four hours while nurses tried to figure out how to override the system. She was 74 years old and had just had hip replacement surgery. Four hours. Nobody could tell me who was responsible for that delay — was it the software? The vendor? The hospital's configuration choices? The nurse who trusted the flag? This is what I think about when I read about HHS wanting to accelerate AI adoption.⏎⏎The question you're asking — what regulatory actions can accelerate AI use in clinical care — I think it's the wrong question entirely. Or at least it's incomplete in a way that worries me. I understand the appeal. I've read about how AI tools might help catch sepsis earlier or flag abnormal imaging findings faster. I'm not dismissing that completely. But there's a gap between what these tools do in a research setting and what happens when they're deployed in a hospital at 2am with an overwhelmed nursing staff who has learned to either always trust the alert or always dismiss it, because that's what happens when you flood people with alerts.⏎⏎What I'd want HHS to actually address is the accountability question. When an AI system contributes to a bad outcome — and this will keep happening — who answers for it? Right now the answer seems to be nobody, or everybody, which is the same thing practically speaking. The vendor says the hospital configured it wrong. The hospital says the vendor's model was biased. The doctor says she was following the recommendation she'd been told to rely on. The patient's family just wants to know why their mother spent four hours in unnecessary pain or worse.⏎⏎The efficiency framing bothers me too. I keep seeing language about reducing administrative burden and I understand that's real, physicians spend enormous amounts of time on documentation, I get it. But a lot of what's being called administrative burden is actually the record of medical reasoning. It's the trail that shows someone thought carefully about a decision. When you automate that, you don't just save time, you remove the cognitive engagement that comes from having to articulate why you're doing something. I'm not sure that's a good trade even if it looks like one on a productivity spreadsheet.⏎⏎I'd also ask HHS to look seriously at what these tools do in communities where the training data was never representative. There's documented evidence going back at least to the 2019 Obermeyer et al. study in Science that commercial algorithms used in health systems systematically underestimated illness severity in Black patients. That was five years ago and the problem hasn't gone away, it's just gotten more complicated because now there are hundreds more tools built on data with similar problems and most hospitals don't have the resources or the expertise to audit them before deployment.⏎⏎Accelerating adoption without solving that is not a neutral act. It's a choice to move fast in a way that will fall unevenly on people who already get worse care.⏎⏎I don't have a specific legal background so I can't point to which regulations need changing exactly. But I'd ask that HHS at minimum require some kind of mandatory incident reporting for AI-related clinical errors before loosening any reimbursement or certification requirements meant to push adoption faster. Right now there's no systematic way to even know how often something like what happened to my mother is happening in hospitals across the country.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0016♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Miller♔♔Mississippi♔♔United States♔Optum♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Mercy General in Sacramento in February 2023 with what turned out to be a pulmonary embolism. The triage system flagged her as low-risk. A nurse overrode it. She is alive. I don't know what system it was, I couldn't find out, the hospital wouldn't tell us. That's where I want to start.⏎⏎HHS is asking how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care and I think that's the wrong question entirely. The question should be whether we've even established that current AI tools are safe enough to be used at the scale they're already being used, let alone expanded. And I genuinely don't think we have.⏎⏎What bothers me most about this RFI is that it frames speed as the goal. "Accelerating adoption" is right there in the title. But medicine isn't a software deployment. When a hospital buys an AI triage or diagnostic tool from, say, a company like Epic or Nuance or one of the dozens of smaller vendors nobody's heard of, the accountability for errors becomes incredibly murky. The vendor says the clinician made the final call. The clinician says the system gave them bad information. The patient is just dead or harmed and nobody is clearly responsible. I've seen this play out in news coverage of sepsis detection tools that missed cases, and I've read about at least two wrongful death suits where this exact argument was made and neither side was found clearly liable.⏎⏎I want to be honest that I'm not opposed to technology in medicine categorically. I understand that physician burnout is real, that administrative burden is crushing, and that AI could theoretically help with some of that. I'm not coming at this as a pure technophobe. But the specific framing of this rulemaking process concerns me because it treats regulatory barriers as the main obstacle to AI adoption, when the actual obstacle might be that many of these tools aren't validated well enough yet. Particularly for populations that weren't well represented in training data.⏎⏎There's a study from 2021 or 2022 I believe published in JAMA or one of the JAMA specialty journals, I'm sorry I don't have the exact citation in front of me, that found commercial sepsis prediction algorithms performed substantially worse for Black patients than white patients. That's not a regulatory problem. That's a fundamental problem with the tool. Loosening oversight requirements doesn't fix that, it makes it worse.⏎⏎The question HHS asks about what regulatory changes would help adoption assumes the current regulations are the bottleneck. But what if they're actually providing some minimal protection that, once removed, we'll regret losing? I've seen how quickly "streamlining" can mean "we removed the requirement to document failures."⏎⏎The part that keeps nagging at me is patient consent. If I'm in an ER and an AI tool is influencing decisions about my care, I probably don't know that. I almost certainly haven't consented to it. The RFI doesn't seem to treat this as a major concern, and I think that's a significant gap. People have a right to know when automated systems are making or shaping clinical recommendations about their bodies. That's not a niche concern, that's pretty foundational.⏎⏎There's also just the question of what happens to nurses and physicians who want to override a system recommendation but feel institutional pressure not to. The nurse who overrode the triage flag for my mother, I wonder sometimes whether she had to document that override, whether it counted against her in some performance metric, whether there's now a system that discourages that kind of judgment. I have no way of knowing.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0017♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Wright♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔Health Equity Initiative♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Brigham and Women's Hospital in February 2023, and during that time I watched maybe a dozen different physicians and nurses make decisions about her care in real time, based on things I can only describe as reading her — the way she responded to a touch on her arm, the exact quality of her breathing, what her face did when she was in pain but couldn't say so. None of that is in a dataset. None of that will ever be in a dataset.⏎⏎That's what keeps coming back to me when I read about HHS wanting to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. Not slow, considered integration. Accelerate.⏎⏎The framing of this whole RFI bothers me more than I can easily explain. The questions seem to start from the assumption that AI in clinical settings is good and the problem is just speed of adoption — regulatory friction, reimbursement gaps, things like that. But the prior question, whether the technology is ready to be trusted with sick human beings at scale, doesn't seem to be on the table. That feels like a significant thing to skip over.⏎⏎I want to respond specifically to the part of the RFI asking about barriers to adoption and what regulatory actions HHS could take to remove them. My answer is that some of those barriers exist for reasons worth taking seriously. When a hospital has to go through a rigorous process before deploying an AI tool that influences diagnosis or treatment decisions, that process is doing something. It's not just friction. There are AI-assisted diagnostic tools currently in use that have shown significantly worse performance on Black patients compared to white patients — this has been documented in peer-reviewed literature going back years, the work of researchers like Dr. Ziad Obermeyer whose 2019 study in Science showed a commercial algorithm systematically disadvantaging Black patients by a wide margin. If the answer from HHS is to make it easier and faster to adopt these tools, I genuinely don't understand what problem we're trying to solve.⏎⏎There's also something that doesn't get talked about enough in these discussions, which is what happens to accountability when AI is in the loop. If a physician makes a bad call, there are systems — imperfect ones, but systems — for figuring out what went wrong and why. Malpractice, peer review, licensing boards. If an AI tool makes a recommendation and the physician follows it and the patient is harmed, I have yet to see a clear answer from anyone about who is responsible. The vendor? The hospital that deployed it? The doctor who accepted the output? This is not a hypothetical concern. It's already happening and the legal and ethical frameworks for handling it are genuinely not ready.⏎⏎I'm not a physician or a lawyer. I'm a social worker with about twenty years of experience working with elderly patients and their families, and I've spent a lot of that time sitting in rooms where people are trying to understand what a doctor just said to them, trying to feel like someone is actually with them in the difficulty of whatever they're facing. The trust that makes medical care work is personal and specific. I worry that the pressure to automate and scale clinical functions treats that trust like an inefficiency to be engineered away rather than something the whole enterprise depends on.⏎⏎HHS should be asking harder questions about safety, bias documentation requirements, and liability frameworks before it does anything to speed up adoption. The reimbursement incentives this RFI mentions — paying for AI-assisted care the way you'd pay for other procedures — that worries me a lot because once there's money attached to using these tools, the pressure on clinicians to defer to them is going to be enormous regardless of whether any individual clinician thinks the tool is giving good guidance in a particular case.⏎⏎I also just want to say that the workforce question is underaddressed here. Radiologists, pathologists, medical coders, prior authorization reviewers — these are real people with training and expertise and if AI displaces significant portions of those roles we don't just lose jobs, we lose institutional knowledge that took decades to build, and we narrow the pipeline of people who understand clinical processes deeply enough to catch errors.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0018♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Allen♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔Siemens Healthineers♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was discharged from St. Luke's in Kansas City in February 2023 after a hip replacement, and within 48 hours an automated prior authorization system had flagged her home PT as "not medically indicated" and cut off approval. Her orthopedic surgeon spent three hours on the phone trying to get it reversed. Three hours. That's the AI acceleration we already have, and you want more of it.⏎⏎What bothers me most about this RFI is the framing. Everything is about adoption and acceleration. There's no question in here that asks "what should we stop doing" or "where has this gone wrong already." The whole thing reads like the decision has been made and you're just asking how fast.⏎⏎I keep thinking about what my mother's surgeon told me afterward. He said the algorithm had no idea she was 74, slightly osteoporotic, and lived alone on the second floor of a walkup. It had a procedure code and a discharge date and it made a determination. That's not medicine. I don't know how else to put it.⏎⏎On the question about reimbursement changes to encourage AI adoption in clinical care -- I actually think this is the most dangerous part of the whole proposal. If you start paying providers more for using AI-assisted diagnostic tools, or make reimbursement contingent on using them, you are essentially mandating a technology that has not been tested at scale in diverse populations. The bias literature here is not ambiguous. There have been documented cases where dermatology AI performed significantly worse on darker skin tones because training sets were predominantly light-skinned patients. Cardiac risk algorithms have shown similar patterns. If reimbursement structures push clinicians toward these tools, the patients who get the worst outputs will be the patients who already get the worst care. That seems like the opposite of what this department should be doing.⏎⏎And I'm not even a Luddite about this, honestly. I use automated tools constantly in my own work (I'm a social worker, not a physician). There are things technology does well. Scheduling. Flagging medication interactions. Reminding patients about follow-ups. I get it. But there is a huge difference between using software to remind someone their flu shot is overdue and using it to decide whether a 74-year-old woman needs physical therapy after major joint surgery.⏎⏎The accountability question is the one I don't see addressed anywhere. When the algorithm is wrong -- and they are wrong, they have error rates, every study shows this -- who is responsible? The vendor? The hospital that licensed the software? The physician who clicked approve on a recommendation they maybe didn't fully scrutinize because they had 22 other patients that afternoon? This diffusion of responsibility is not hypothetical. It is already happening and nobody seems to want to name it directly.⏎⏎My mother eventually got her PT approved after her surgeon's staff submitted a peer-to-peer review request. It took eleven days total from discharge. She fell in her apartment on day nine.⏎⏎I don't know what the right regulatory answer is to all of this. I'm not a policy expert. But I do know that "accelerate adoption" is the wrong goal to start from, and I think this comment period deserves responses that say so plainly even if they don't come with a 40-page white paper attached. The question of who is accountable when these systems fail needs an actual answer before any of the reimbursement or incentive changes get finalized, and right now I haven't seen anything from HHS that♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0019♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Brown♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔Meharry Medical College♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City for eleven days in February 2023, and what got her through it was a night nurse named Darnell who noticed something was off about her breathing pattern at 2am and called the attending before the monitors flagged anything. Nothing in a dataset captures what he did. No algorithm was trained on whatever combination of experience, exhaustion, and care that made him stop and look twice. She is alive. I don't know how to quantify that.⏎⏎That's the thing that keeps nagging at me when I read about HHS wanting to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings. I understand there are efficiency arguments. I'm not completely dismissing them. But the word "accelerate" in the framing of this RFI bothers me more than almost anything else in it, because acceleration implies we've already established that the direction is right and just need to go faster. I don't think we've established that.⏎⏎The safety literature is not reassuring. A study published in JAMA in 2023 found that several commercially deployed sepsis prediction algorithms were generating false alerts at rates high enough that nurses at some sites had effectively stopped responding to them with urgency. That's not a minor calibration problem. That's a system that has trained clinical staff to tune out warnings, which is almost the worst possible outcome you could engineer into an ICU environment. And the response from HHS, as far as I can tell, is to want more of this, faster.⏎⏎I keep thinking about who actually bears the cost when these systems fail. It's not the vendor. It's not the hospital administrator who approved the contract. It's the patient in the bed and sometimes the nurse or physician who gets blamed for not overriding the algorithm at the right moment. The accountability structure here is genuinely broken and I haven't seen anything in this RFI that suggests HHS is treating that as the central problem it actually is.⏎⏎The bias issue deserves more than the paragraph it usually gets in these discussions. Pulse oximeter readings, risk scoring tools, kidney function calculators -- we have documented cases where algorithms trained primarily on data from white patients perform measurably worse on Black patients. The FDA has flagged this. Researchers have published on it extensively. And yet the proposal here seems to be that we should be making it easier and faster to deploy more AI tools into clinical settings, which presumably means deploying more tools that carry these same training data problems into settings that already have racial disparities in care. I'm not sure how that math works out to a benefit.⏎⏎What I would want HHS to do instead is require rigorous prospective clinical evaluation -- not just the retrospective validation that most of these tools have been approved on -- before any AI system is used in a way that influences diagnosis or treatment. I'd want mandatory demographic disaggregation of performance data. I'd want clear rules about who is liable when an AI recommendation contributes to patient harm. Right now that's genuinely unclear in most cases.⏎⏎I also think there's something worth naming about what medicine is supposed to be. Not in a sentimental way, though I'm sure this will read as sentimental. There's a reason patients want to talk to a person. There's a reason informed consent involves a conversation and not a form. The relationship has moral weight. Replacing pieces of it with automated systems, even efficient ones, changes something that I don't think can be recovered just by adding a disclosure in a patient portal.⏎⏎I'm not saying no AI anywhere ever. That's not a realistic position and I know it. But the framing of this whole RFI is wrong. The question shouldn't be how do we accelerate adoption. It should be how do we figure out what's actually safe and what's actually beneficial to patients specifically, not to hospital operating margins, and then move at the speed that allows us to know the difference.⏎⏎The liability question alone -- I've looked at some of the existing guidance and it's genuinely♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0020♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patricia♔Torres♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Francis Medical Center in Lynwood, California in February of this year. Sepsis. And what I watched during those eleven days was a team of nurses and doctors making constant judgment calls — not just reading numbers off a screen, but reading her. Reading the way she was breathing, the way she responded when they talked to her, the things she told them about her pain when she could speak. That is not something you can put in a dataset.⏎⏎So when I see HHS talking about "accelerating AI adoption in clinical care" I genuinely don't know where to start with my concerns. I'll try to focus on what I think I understand, which is the question of clinical decision support and what happens to accountability when an algorithm is involved in a treatment recommendation.⏎⏎What bothers me most is the diffusion of responsibility. If a physician uses an AI tool and that tool gives bad guidance, who is responsible? The doctor might say the tool recommended it. The company that made the tool says the doctor should have used clinical judgment. The hospital says it was an approved product. And the patient — or the patient's family — is left with no one to hold accountable. I've seen this logic play out in other industries and it never ends well for ordinary people. There's a reason we have a doctor sign their name on something.⏎⏎I also keep thinking about the bias problem, which I don't think gets taken seriously enough in conversations like this one. There was a study published in Science back in 2019 — Obermeyer and colleagues — that found a widely used commercial algorithm was systematically underestimating the health needs of Black patients because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health. That was a major, peer-reviewed finding about a tool already deployed across millions of patients. And the response from the industry was basically "we'll fix it." That's not sufficient. If we accelerate adoption before we understand how these biases work and how they compound across different clinical settings, we are going to cause harm at scale. Not theoretical harm. Real harm to real patients.⏎⏎I do understand there are administrative burdens on clinicians that are genuinely awful. Prior authorizations, documentation requirements, all of it. I'm not saying technology has no role. But there is a difference between using software to handle paperwork and using software to influence clinical judgment, and I'm not convinced HHS is drawing that line clearly enough in this process.⏎⏎What I want HHS to actually do is slow down. Commission independent safety reviews before expanding reimbursement for AI-assisted diagnostics. Require that any AI tool used in a clinical setting have a publicly accessible summary of what data it was trained on and what populations it was validated in. Make it mandatory that patients know when an AI tool was used in their care, not buried in a consent form somewhere.⏎⏎The framing of this RFI is that speed is the goal. "Accelerating" adoption. But medicine is not a sector where speed is always a virtue. My mother's nurses took time. They asked questions. They came back to check on things that didn't add up. I don't know how to quantify that on a cost-benefit spreadsheet and I'm not sure the people designing these systems have thought carefully about what gets lost when you optimize for efficiency in a place where the margin for error is someone's life.⏎⏎I also want to say that I'm not a doctor or a health policy expert. I'm a high school history teacher from Torrance. I'm not sure I've addressed the right questions in this RFI and honestly the document was pretty hard to parse. But I felt like I had to say something.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0021♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Harris♔♔Wisconsin♔♔United States♔Endocrine Society♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Emory University Hospital in February of 2023, and I sat with her through most of it. What kept her alive was not an algorithm. It was a pulmonologist named Dr. Anand Krishnaswamy who looked at her chart, looked at her, talked to my father for forty minutes at 11pm on a Tuesday, and made a call that the ventilator settings needed adjustment based on something he described as a "gut read" on how she was tolerating the pressure. She survived. I have thought about that moment constantly since I started reading about what HHS is proposing here.⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to AI adoption in clinical care and what the department can do to speed things up. I want to answer that question honestly: the barrier is that AI should not be making or substantially shaping clinical decisions, and speeding up adoption is the wrong goal. The question HHS should be asking is whether any of this is actually safe, not how to remove friction from deployment.⏎⏎I understand there are efficiency arguments. I do. Documentation burdens are real and I've heard physicians complain about EHR time for years. But the framing of this entire request concerns me because it treats adoption speed as inherently good. Faster is not better if what you're speeding up hasn't been adequately studied in real clinical populations. A study published in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2021 found racial disparities baked into a widely used commercial algorithm for identifying patients who needed additional care management, where the tool was systematically underestimating the health needs of Black patients relative to white patients at equivalent levels of illness. That finding came out years after the tool was already deployed in hundreds of health systems. If we're accelerating adoption now, who is catching problems like that before they scale?⏎⏎The accountability question is something I keep coming back to and nobody seems to want to answer it directly. When an AI-assisted diagnostic recommendation is wrong and a patient is harmed, who is responsible? The physician who followed it? The vendor who trained the model on data that didn't represent that patient population? The hospital that configured the system? The way liability is currently structured, the answer is deeply unclear, and I worry that the practical effect of widespread AI adoption is that responsibility gets diffused until it disappears entirely. That is not hypothetical, that is a structural feature of how these tools get deployed.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the workforce dimension that I don't think gets enough attention in these discussions. There are real nurses and medical assistants and clinical support staff whose jobs are going to be eliminated or fundamentally changed by this, and those are disproportionately women, disproportionately people of color. I'm not saying technology shouldn't change jobs. But there's a difference between jobs changing and jobs disappearing under the cover of efficiency, and I think HHS has an obligation to take that seriously rather than treating it as outside the scope of a clinical care rulemaking.⏎⏎The RFI mentions reimbursement pathways as a lever. I'd actually argue that reimbursement incentives for AI-assisted care, before we have robust outcome data showing those tools improve care for diverse patient populations, is premature. CMS already has a track record of creating perverse incentives through payment structures and then spending years trying to unwind them. Building AI adoption into reimbursement before the evidence base is solid seems like it could repeat that pattern pretty directly.⏎⏎What I'm asking for is a slower and more skeptical approach. Mandatory prospective outcome tracking. Independent auditing for bias before deployment at scale. Clear liability frameworks. None of that is anti-technology, it's just how you responsibly introduce something with this much potential for harm into a system where people's lives are at stake.⏎⏎Dr. Krishnaswamy's call that night wasn't documented in a way any model could have learned from.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0022♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Taylor♔♔New York♔♔United States♔University of Pittsburgh School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in February of 2023. I was there almost every day. What I remember most is not the machines or the monitors — it's the faces of the nurses who stopped to explain things to us, the attending physician who sat down in a plastic chair at 10pm to answer my sister's questions. That human attention, that accountability, is what medicine actually is. And when I read this request for information about "accelerating AI adoption in clinical care," I feel something close to dread.⏎⏎I want to focus on one specific thing this RFI asks about, which is what regulatory or reimbursement actions HHS could take to speed up AI use in clinical settings. My honest answer is: please don't. Or at least slow down considerably and think hard about what you're doing.⏎⏎The framing of this whole effort bothers me. "Accelerating adoption" is not a neutral phrase. It implies the direction is already decided and the only question is how fast. But we haven't actually established that patients are better off when AI is inserted into clinical decisions. The efficiency arguments are real, I won't pretend otherwise, but efficiency for whom? When a prior authorization gets denied by an algorithm, the patient doesn't experience that as efficient. They experience it as a wall they can't get past, with nobody on the other side to appeal to. There was a lawsuit in 2023 involving United Healthcare and their nH Predict tool, where the algorithm was apparently overriding physician judgment on post-acute care lengths of stay at rates that alarmed people who looked closely at it. I think about that a lot. The doctors knew their patients. The algorithm had a model.⏎⏎One thing I genuinely don't know is whether there's good evidence that AI diagnostic tools produce better outcomes or just faster outputs. Those are different things. Faster is not the same as better. And when something goes wrong with an AI recommendation, the question of who is responsible becomes genuinely murky. Is it the hospital? The vendor? The physician who technically made the final call? The diffusion of accountability in that chain worries me more than almost anything else about this.⏎⏎I'm also skeptical of the bias problem being solvable in the near term. Training data for these models reflects decades of unequal care. If you train a model on outcomes data from a healthcare system that has systematically undertreated Black patients with pain, the model learns that undertreating Black patients with pain is normal. You can try to correct for that but the corrections are imperfect and the errors compound.⏎⏎HHS should be asking different questions than the ones in this RFI. Instead of asking how to accelerate adoption, ask what patient populations have been harmed by AI tools already in use. Ask whether the workforce reductions that AI enables in administrative roles are translating to better care or just lower operating costs. Ask what happens to the nurse whose clinical judgment gets second-guessed by a tool she didn't choose and can't interrogate.⏎⏎I don't think AI has no role in healthcare. That's not my position. But "accelerate" is exactly the wrong word for what HHS should be doing right now, and I'm not confident that the reimbursement incentive structure this RFI contemplates would correct for that. If you pay hospitals more for using AI-assisted tools, they'll use them regardless of whether they help the patients in front of them.⏎⏎There's also no clear mechanism I can see in the RFI for patients to opt out.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0023♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Hernandez♔♔West Virginia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's Hospital in Kansas City in February of 2023, and during that time I watched a team of nurses and doctors make dozens of judgment calls that I genuinely do not believe any algorithm could have made. Small things. The way her attending physician, Dr. Reyes, noticed she was holding her arm differently one morning and ordered an additional scan that caught a secondary clot before it became catastrophic. That kind of attention is not a data input. I don't know how you quantify it and I don't think you can.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS wants to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, my first reaction is: accelerate toward what, exactly?⏎⏎I want to focus on the question about where AI can appropriately support clinical decision-making, because I think the framing of the RFI gets the problem backwards. The question being asked is essentially "how do we speed this up" and I think the question should be "should we, and under what conditions." Those are very different starting points and the difference matters a lot.⏎⏎There's a study published in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2023 that looked at sepsis prediction algorithms deployed at multiple hospital systems and found that several of them had significantly higher false positive rates for Black patients compared to white patients, which led to overtreating some patients and potentially under-resourcing attention to others. I may be misremembering some of the specifics but the general finding is well-documented and it points to a real problem: these tools are trained on historical data that already contains the inequities of our healthcare system, and deploying them faster doesn't fix that, it just bakes the inequity in at a larger scale faster.⏎⏎What concerns me almost as much as the safety question is the accountability question. When a physician makes a wrong call, there is a person responsible. Patients and families may not always get justice but there is at least a framework, a license board, a malpractice process, some mechanism that acknowledges a human being made a decision and bears responsibility for it. When an AI tool makes a wrong call and a physician defers to it because the liability structure incentivizes deference, who is responsible? The vendor? The hospital? The doctor who clicked "accept recommendation"? I genuinely don't know and I don't think HHS has answered that question, and I'm not sure it's answerable in a way that actually protects patients.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the workforce dimension, because I think it gets mentioned as an afterthought in these conversations. If AI handles documentation, triage screening, prior authorization review, and preliminary diagnostic flagging, what happens to the medical coders, the nurses doing intake assessment, the radiologists reviewing routine films? The efficiency gains that HHS seems excited about are in many cases the jobs of real people. I'm not saying technology shouldn't change healthcare, but "accelerating adoption" without a serious workforce transition plan feels like the department is treating job displacement as someone else's problem to figure out later.⏎⏎I don't have a tidy recommendation to offer. I'm a person whose family has been through a serious medical situation and who reads a lot of health policy coverage, not a clinician or a researcher. But I'd ask HHS to at minimum slow down and require that any AI tool used in clinical decision-making be subject to prospective, not just retrospective, bias auditing before deployment, and that liability frameworks be clarified before hospitals have financial incentives to deploy these tools at scale.⏎⏎The thing that sticks with me is that Dr. Reyes came in on her day off to check on my mother. I have no idea how you train a model to do that or why we'd want to replace the conditions that made it possible with something more♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0024♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Matthew♔Thomas♔♔South Dakota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February of 2023. I watched nurses make judgment calls every few hours that no algorithm could have made. One of them, a woman who had been doing this for over twenty years, noticed something was off with my mother's color at around 2 in the morning and called the attending. That call probably saved her life. Nobody had a dashboard telling them to do that. Nobody's AI flagged a deterioration score. A person who had spent two decades learning how human bodies fail looked at my mother and knew something was wrong.⏎⏎I keep thinking about that when I read materials like this one about accelerating AI adoption. The word "accelerating" is doing a lot of work in that framing and I don't think HHS has fully reckoned with what it's asking.⏎⏎The question I want to address is whether the regulatory environment should be changed to speed up clinical AI deployment. My honest answer is no, not right now, and I want to explain why I think the current framing gets things backwards.⏎⏎When HHS talks about reducing barriers to AI adoption, there's an assumption underneath that the barriers are mostly bureaucratic friction or outdated regulations that haven't caught up with technology. But some of those barriers exist because medicine is not a field where moving fast and finding out later is acceptable. A software error in a clinical decision support tool is not like a bug in a photo editing app. The harms are irreversible. My mother can't be unintubated after the fact if an algorithm told a physician something that wasn't true.⏎⏎The bias question also doesn't get enough honest treatment in these kinds of solicitations. There's substantial published research, including work from researchers at MIT and Stanford, showing that clinical AI tools trained on historical data reproduce the inequities baked into that history. If certain populations were undertreated for pain in the data, the model learns that pattern. If minority patients were less likely to be referred to specialists, that gets encoded too. Accelerating deployment of tools like this before we have real, independent auditing mechanisms is not a neutral act. It's a choice to spread those disparities faster and at larger scale.⏎⏎I understand there are genuine problems with physician burnout and administrative burden and I'm not dismissing that. If AI can help with prior authorization paperwork or scheduling or billing coding, fine, though even there I'd want to see careful oversight of anything touching insurance decisions because we've already seen what happens when those systems go wrong. The reporting on UnitedHealth's nH Predict tool and its use in denying post-acute care was genuinely alarming to me and that situation is not fully resolved.⏎⏎What concerns me most about this RFI is the direction of the pressure. Everything is oriented toward removing friction, streamlining approval, encouraging adoption. I didn't see equivalent weight given to the question of what happens when these tools fail and who is accountable. When a physician makes an error, there is a person who can be questioned, who can explain their reasoning, who faces professional consequences. When an algorithm produces a wrong output and a clinician follows it, accountability gets murky very fast. I've read enough about "automation bias" to know that clinicians under time pressure tend to defer to algorithmic outputs even when their own instincts might have caught the error.⏎⏎I'm not someone who thinks AI has no place in medicine at all. But the question of whether HHS should actively work to accelerate adoption, as opposed to ensuring that whatever does get adopted is genuinely safe and equitable, seems like it's being answered before it's been properly asked.⏎⏎There's also no real discussion in the materials I reviewed about what happens to the nursing workforce, the radiology techs, the coders and coordinators, if AI does what its proponents say it will do. Those are real people with families and mortgages and the fact that their displacement might coincide with efficiency gains for health systems doesn't make♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0025♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Betty♔Ramirez♔♔Wisconsin♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Bakersfield in February of 2023. I sat with her every day. And what I remember most is not the monitors or the medication schedules — it's the pulmonologist who came in on a Saturday morning, pulled up a chair, and spent forty minutes just talking with us about what we were actually facing. No clipboard. Just a doctor who had been doing this for twenty-some years, who could read my mother's face and mine and adjust what he was saying accordingly. That's not something you can put in a dataset.⏎⏎HHS wants to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. I want to ask who exactly is in a hurry here, and why. The patients I've talked to — family members, friends who've dealt with serious diagnoses recently — none of them are clamoring for faster AI. They want more time with their doctors. They want someone to be accountable when something goes wrong.⏎⏎The accountability piece is what keeps me up at night honestly. When an AI system recommends a treatment pathway and it turns out to be wrong, who answers for that? The hospital? The vendor? The physician who relied on it because the workflow pushed them that way? I've read enough about these systems to know that the answer right now is basically nobody, or more accurately the question gets litigated into a fog. A doctor making a mistake is a tragedy with a name and a face. An algorithm making a mistake is a product liability question.⏎⏎There's also the bias problem that I don't think HHS is taking seriously enough in this RFI. Pulse oximeters — which are not AI but are the kind of automated clinical tool we've been deploying without adequate scrutiny for decades — were shown by researchers at UCSF to have meaningful accuracy disparities for patients with darker skin tones, and that went on for years before it became part of mainstream clinical conversation. AI diagnostic tools trained on historical data are going to replicate whatever patterns were in that data, including patterns of who got diagnosed early and who didn't, who was taken seriously and who wasn't. Accelerating adoption means accelerating that problem before we've figured out how to audit for it.⏎⏎I recognize there are administrative uses of AI that are lower stakes, and I'm not saying every use is equally risky. Scheduling tools or whatever, fine. But the RFI lumps clinical care in with all of it and that bothers me. The framing throughout is about efficiency and adoption rates, and I'd want someone at HHS to sit with why efficiency keeps being the metric. Efficient for whom.⏎⏎The thing about clinical judgment that I don't think gets said enough is that it includes knowing when you don't know. A good clinician sits with uncertainty, orders another test, calls a colleague, sleeps on it. An AI system optimized for throughput doesn't do that. It gives you an output. The confidence score might say 87% but what does that actually mean to a nurse at 3am in an understaffed unit who's seen the system flag something similar twice this week and both times it was nothing.⏎⏎I would ask HHS to at minimum require that any accelerated adoption pathway include mandatory independent audits for bias before deployment, not after problems emerge, and that there be a clear moratorium on AI use in insurance coverage determinations until liability frameworks actually exist. The prior authorization AI tools that some payers are already using have denial rates that don't match physician review rates by significant margins — UnitedHealth's nH Predict tool was the subject of a 2023 ProPublica investigation for exactly this reason. That's already happening and it's already causing harm and HHS is talking about accelerating.⏎⏎I don't have a tidy policy recommendation to close with. I just think the framing of this whole RFI is off. "Accelerating adoption" assumes the direction is right and the only question is speed, and I'm not sure either of those things♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0026♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Baker♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in February 2023 after a sepsis diagnosis, and what kept her alive was a nurse named Darlene who noticed at 2am that something looked wrong even though every monitor said she was stable. Not an algorithm. A person who had been doing this for twenty years and had a feeling. I keep thinking about that when I read materials like this RFI.⏎⏎The framing here bothers me. The whole document is built around the assumption that faster adoption is the goal and the only question is what obstacles to remove. I'd push back on that. The question nobody seems to want to ask is whether adoption should be accelerated at all, given how little we actually know about how these systems perform across different patient populations and care settings.⏎⏎I work as a social worker in a hospital system in Ohio and I see how algorithmic tools are already being used, and not always well. We had a risk-stratification tool rolled out about eighteen months ago for identifying patients at high risk for readmission. The staff were told it would help. What actually happened is that the nurses started looking at the score before they looked at the patient. The score became the assessment. Nobody explicitly told them to do it that way, it just happened because people are busy and the number is right there on the screen. The patients who got flagged as low risk got less attention. I don't know if anyone tracked outcomes from that systematically but I have my doubts that it improved anything.⏎⏎The accountability question is the one I want HHS to actually grapple with, and I don't see it addressed seriously here. When an AI recommendation contributes to a bad outcome, who is responsible? Right now the answer is genuinely unclear. The vendor says their tool is a decision support product and the clinician made the final call. The clinician says they were following what the system recommended because deviating from it requires documentation and justification. The hospital says they implemented it according to guidelines. And the patient or their family has essentially no recourse that isn't enormously expensive and difficult to pursue.⏎⏎Accelerating adoption without first solving that problem seems backwards to me. I'm not anti-technology in some blanket way, I understand that there are tools that probably do help in specific narrow applications. But the push to move fast, to remove regulatory friction, to encourage broader deployment -- it treats the healthcare system like a tech market where iteration is cheap and failures are learning opportunities. Patients are not beta testers.⏎⏎There's also the workforce piece. I haven't seen HHS address seriously what happens to the diagnostic and clinical reasoning skills of physicians who are trained in environments where AI tools are doing a lot of the inferential work. Medical judgment is something you build over years of practice. If residents are increasingly deferring to algorithmic outputs during their training, what does that mean for the quality of their judgment in situations where the AI is wrong or unavailable. That's not a hypothetical worry, it's a question that should have an answer before we move forward at the pace this RFI seems to contemplate.⏎⏎I'd ask HHS to commission independent, pre-market safety evaluations for clinical AI tools before reimbursement pathways are opened up, not after. The FDA's current framework for software as a medical device has real gaps and the iterative change exemption in particular has allowed significant modifications to be deployed without new review. That needs to be fixed before anything else.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0027♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Gonzalez♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔LeadingAge♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February 2023, and I watched nurses and doctors make dozens of judgment calls every single day that I genuinely don't think any software could have made. Not because I'm sentimental about doctors, I've had plenty of bad ones, but because what I saw was people reading a room. Reading her face. Changing course when something felt off before any number on a monitor changed. That's not something you can encode.⏎⏎So when I see HHS talking about "accelerating adoption" of AI in clinical care, my honest reaction is: accelerating toward what, exactly? The framing of this whole RFI assumes the problem is that AI isn't moving fast enough into hospitals. I'm not sure that's the actual problem facing American medicine right now.⏎⏎The question I keep coming back to is who is responsible when an AI system recommends the wrong course of treatment. I've read enough about these tools to know that the answer is genuinely murky. The vendor says the clinician should have caught it. The clinician says they relied on the system. The hospital says it was an approved tool. And the patient or their family is left with nothing except a very long process to figure out who, if anyone, is accountable. That diffusion of responsibility isn't a hypothetical, it's already happening. There was a ProPublica piece from 2023 documenting how sepsis prediction algorithms in several hospital systems flagged the wrong patients repeatedly, and nurses described feeling pressured to defer to the alert even when their gut said otherwise. That's the dynamic I'm afraid of normalizing at scale.⏎⏎I also want to say something about bias because I think the way HHS frames this understates the problem. These models are trained on historical data from a healthcare system that has systematically undertreated Black patients, undertreated women with pain, overtreated in ways that reflect insurance status rather than need. Training a model on that data doesn't remove those patterns, it preserves them and then automates them at a speed and scale that makes them harder to challenge or even see. I'm not against technology in medicine at all, but I am against moving fast on this before we have any real standards for auditing these systems for disparate impact, and right now we don't. Not real ones with teeth.⏎⏎I realize the department will say that existing FDA oversight and whatever ONC certification processes cover some of this, but honestly the certification framework for clinical decision support tools is not remotely adequate to the task of catching bias or assessing real-world safety across different patient populations. The 21st Century Cures Act exclusions for clinical decision support created a gap that I think is wider than regulators want to admit.⏎⏎What I'd actually like to see, instead of an acceleration agenda, is a genuine moratorium on AI in high-stakes clinical decisions, things like triage, diagnosis, treatment recommendation, until there's a mandatory post-market surveillance system in place with public reporting requirements. Not voluntary. Mandatory. With timelines that actually mean something.⏎⏎I'm also genuinely worried about what this does to the medical workforce over ten years. If hospitals start substituting AI tools for nurses or for the kind of slow, human diagnostic process that catches the weird cases, we're going to lose something that takes decades to rebuild. I don't have a citation for that, it's more of an intuition based on watching what happened to radiology residents at programs that adopted AI reading tools too quickly and found their trainees weren't developing the foundational pattern recognition skills they used to.⏎⏎There's a version of AI in healthcare that I might support, administrative burden reduction, scheduling, prior authorization paperwork, stuff that genuinely wastes clinician time without touching the patient relationship. But that's not really what this RFI is about, or at least it's not the part getting the most attention.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0028♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Rivera♔♔New Hampshire♔♔United States♔Illumina♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at UCSF Medical Center in September 2023 with what turned out to be a fairly complex cardiac situation, and watching what the nursing staff and cardiologists actually had to do to figure out what was going on — the back and forth, the re-examination, the conversations with her, with us, with each other — I keep thinking about that when I read about how AI is going to "accelerate" clinical care. Accelerate it toward what, exactly.⏎⏎What bothers me most about this RFI is that the framing assumes speed and adoption are the goal. The question isn't how fast we can get AI into clinical settings. The question is whether it belongs there in the roles being described, and I don't think that's been answered. Not even close.⏎⏎There's a study out of Stanford — I think it was published in JAMA Network Open sometime in 2023 — that found sepsis prediction algorithms had significantly worse performance in Black patients than white patients, and the hospitals using those tools didn't even know it because no one was auditing outcomes by race. That's not a hypothetical concern or a future risk. That already happened. And the response from the AI industry was basically to say the tools need more diverse training data, which is probably true but also doesn't help the patients who were undertriaged in the meantime.⏎⏎I work as a social worker in a community health clinic in Fresno, California, so I'm not a physician, and I want to be upfront about that. But I see patients every day who are already treated as less-than by systems that reduce them to billing codes and risk scores. Adding AI to that process doesn't seem like it's going to make them feel more seen or more cared for. It seems like it's going to give institutions another layer of plausible deniability when things go wrong. "The algorithm flagged her as low risk." Okay, but who chose the algorithm? Who validated it on patients like her? Who is accountable when it's wrong?⏎⏎That accountability question is the one I really want HHS to sit with. Right now the answer seems to be nobody. The AI vendor says they provided a tool, the hospital says they were following the tool's recommendations, and the patient has no real recourse. The existing medical malpractice framework wasn't built for this and everyone knows it.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no role anywhere in medicine. I genuinely don't know the answer to that. Maybe there are narrow, well-defined tasks where it performs well and the risks are manageable. But "accelerating adoption" as a policy goal, without first having a serious infrastructure for auditing these tools, for requiring transparency about training data, for making sure patients know when AI is involved in their care and can opt out — that's getting the order of events completely backwards.⏎⏎The reimbursement piece especially worries me. Once you create financial incentives for hospitals to use AI tools, you've created pressure to use them even when clinical staff have doubts. I've seen how that dynamic plays out with other reimbursement-driven mandates. Clinicians end up documenting around the system rather than engaging with it honestly, and the data coming out of the tool becomes less and less trustworthy over time.⏎⏎I'd ask HHS to seriously consider whether a moratorium on new AI reimbursement incentives — at least until there are mandatory post-market surveillance requirements in place — might be the more responsible path here. I realize that's not the direction the department seems to want to go, but I think the people who will be most affected by getting this wrong deserve to have that option on the table.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0029♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Taylor♔♔Tennessee♔♔United States♔Exscientia♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Advocate Christ Medical Center in Oak Lawn, Illinois in February of 2023. I watched physicians sit at her bedside and talk to her, watch her face, read her breathing. That is not something a model trained on retrospective claims data does. I keep thinking about that when I read materials like this RFI, and honestly it makes me angry in a way I'm having trouble fully articulating.⏎⏎The question I want to address is the one about what regulatory actions HHS can take to speed up adoption. My answer is that HHS is asking the wrong question entirely. The framing assumes acceleration is the goal and asks only how to get there faster. There is no serious weight given to the possibility that moving slower, or stopping, is the right answer for some of these applications.⏎⏎I work in hospital administration, not clinical care, so I see this from a particular angle. What I've watched over about the last six or seven years is a procurement process where vendors come in with impressive demos, often citing accuracy percentages in the high nineties, and those numbers come from their own validation studies run on their own curated datasets. We bought a sepsis prediction tool in 2021, I won't name the vendor, and the alert fatigue it generated in the first eight months was bad enough that nursing staff started developing informal workarounds to silence it. That is a safety problem. The tool didn't fail because of malice. It failed because the environment it was tested in was not the environment it was deployed in. This happens constantly and I don't see this RFI grappling with it seriously.⏎⏎The reimbursement angle concerns me too, maybe more than the clinical side in some ways. If CMS starts paying for AI-assisted diagnoses or AI-assisted prior authorization reviews at rates that make them financially attractive, you create a structural incentive to use these tools regardless of whether they're appropriate for a given patient. Insurers have already been using algorithmic tools to deny claims and there have been investigative reports, the ProPublica and STAT News coverage of nH Predict comes to mind, showing how those tools can produce systematically biased outcomes against older patients and patients with certain conditions. Accelerating that by building it further into reimbursement structures is not a neutral technical decision.⏎⏎I'm genuinely uncertain about some of this. I think there are administrative uses, scheduling, documentation burden reduction, some coding tasks, where the risk profile is different and the efficiency argument is more honest. I don't think AI is inherently bad in healthcare. But the RFI doesn't really distinguish between those lower-stakes administrative functions and tools that influence diagnosis or treatment or coverage decisions. It treats them as roughly the same category of thing that just needs regulatory smoothing to move forward, and that flattens a distinction that matters enormously.⏎⏎What would slow this down in a responsible way? At minimum, mandatory post-market surveillance requirements with standardized reporting, and I mean standardized, not whatever each vendor decides constitutes a meaningful outcome metric. Independent validation on representative patient populations before deployment, not after. Real liability clarity so that when an AI tool contributes to a bad outcome, there is an identifiable responsible party and it isn't just the physician who happened to be in the room.⏎⏎The physician accountability piece is the one I can't get past. Right now the legal and ethical frameworks put the clinician at the center of responsibility. If you accelerate AI adoption without resolving where accountability sits when the AI is wrong, you get a system where the clinician is blamed for following a recommendation the institution financially incentivized them to use, from a vendor who disclaims liability in the contract, validated on data that didn't include patients like the one who was harmed.⏎⏎I don't know what the right regulatory structure looks like in detail and I'm not going to pretend otherwise. But I do know that the process of figuring it out should come before the acceleration, not as a condition that gets worked out along the way while hospitals are already deploying these tools at scale.⏎⏎The comment period for this RFI closes in what feels like a very short window given the scope of what's being asked, and I'd also note that the Federal Register notice was not easy to find for someone who isn't regularly monitoring agency publications.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0030♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Hernandez♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔American Society of Nephrology♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati back in February of 2023. I sat with her every day. I watched the nurses and the doctors make decisions that no algorithm could have made, reading her face, adjusting to things that weren't in any chart. She made it out. I've been thinking about that ever since I heard HHS was looking at ways to speed up AI in clinical care, and honestly my reaction is: why the rush?⏎⏎The question the RFI seems most interested in is what regulatory or reimbursement changes could accelerate AI adoption. I want to push back on the premise. Acceleration implies we've already established that the destination is good and the only problem is getting there faster. I don't think we've established that. There are studies showing AI diagnostic tools perform significantly worse on Black patients and patients with darker skin tones, and those aren't fringe findings, the FDA's own literature reviews have flagged this. The pulse oximetry problem alone, where devices systematically over-read oxygen saturation in darker-skinned patients, took decades to get traction, and now we're talking about building more layers of algorithmic decision support on top of a healthcare system that still hasn't fully corrected for those errors.⏎⏎What worries me about the framing of this RFI is the assumption that the main barriers to AI adoption are regulatory friction and reimbursement gaps. Maybe some of them are. But some of the barriers exist because clinicians are skeptical, and clinicians being skeptical of a new technology is not a problem to be solved by policy, it's actually the system working correctly. A cardiologist who won't rely on an AI risk score she doesn't understand isn't being obstructionist. She's practicing medicine.⏎⏎I also want to say something about accountability that I don't see addressed enough in discussions like this. When an AI system recommends against approving a prior authorization and a patient is denied care, who is responsible for that decision? The insurer says it was the algorithm. The algorithm's developer says the insurer configured it. The clinician says their hands were tied. I've seen this happen with UnitedHealth's use of the nH Predict tool in post-acute care denials. There were lawsuits. There were people who didn't get rehabilitation they needed. That wasn't a hypothetical risk, it happened.⏎⏎Speeding up adoption without first getting the liability and accountability frameworks right is going to make that pattern worse, not better. If HHS wants to do something useful here, slow down and answer the harder question first: when an AI system causes a preventable harm, who is legally and ethically on the hook? Right now the answer is basically nobody, and that's a terrible foundation to be accelerating from.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no place in medicine. I'm sure there are narrow applications where it genuinely helps and the evidence is solid. But the RFI reads like the goal has already been decided and we're just working out the mechanics. That concerns me. The efficiency gains that get cited in favor of this stuff almost always accrue to payers and administrators, not to patients, and not to the nurses who are already stretched thin and don't need another system generating alerts they have to document a reason for ignoring.⏎⏎The hospital where my mother recovered had an early warning sepsis algorithm that fired something like 40 alerts per nurse per shift. The nurses had basically stopped trusting it by the time she was admitted.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0031♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Wilson♔♔Tennessee♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City for eleven days in February 2023, and I watched what happens when clinical decision-making gets routed through algorithmic systems. She had sepsis with an atypical presentation and the early warning software kept flagging her as lower acuity than she was. Her nurse, who had 22 years of experience, knew something was wrong before any of the dashboards did. She escalated over the system's implicit guidance. My mother is alive. I think about that a lot when I read documents like this one.⏎⏎The RFI asks what HHS can do to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. My honest reaction is that "accelerate" is the wrong frame entirely. The question should be whether adoption is appropriate, and at what pace, and with what safeguards in place before patients are exposed to these tools at scale. The framing of this document assumes the destination is settled and asks only about the road there.⏎⏎What concerns me about the administrative and insurance side especially is the accountability question. When a prior authorization gets denied because an algorithm said no, who is responsible? I don't think that's been answered. I've read that some large insurers were already using AI tools to deny post-acute care claims at rates far exceeding what human reviewers would have approved, and litigation followed, but the harm had already happened to real patients who couldn't fight it. The diffusion of responsibility is the part that keeps me up at night. It's not just that the AI might be wrong. It's that when it's wrong, nobody is clearly on the hook.⏎⏎I also want to say something about bias that I feel doesn't get enough serious treatment in documents like this. Medical AI tools trained on historical data inherit historical disparities. Pulse oximetry algorithms performed worse on patients with darker skin tones for decades and we only really started reckoning with that recently. If AI tools get accelerated into clinical workflows before that kind of auditing is standard and mandatory, we will bake in those disparities at enormous scale. Acceleration makes that worse, not better.⏎⏎The workforce piece is something I'm genuinely uncertain about. I don't think every use of AI in medicine is harmful. I understand documentation burden is real and if something genuinely helps a physician spend more time with patients instead of typing, that seems worth examining. But the jump from administrative assistance to clinical decision support is enormous and I don't think the regulatory infrastructure exists yet to manage it safely.⏎⏎There's no national framework for post-market surveillance of clinical AI tools that I'm aware of. The FDA's approach to software as a medical device is still being sorted out. And yet this RFI is asking how to speed things up.⏎⏎I would ask HHS instead to focus on what foundations need to be built first. Mandatory transparency about when AI is influencing a clinical or coverage decision. Patient notification rights. Clear liability rules. Required demographic performance auditing before deployment. Independent post-market monitoring with teeth, meaning the ability to pull a tool from use if it's shown to cause harm.⏎⏎I recognize there are real pressures here and I'm not saying AI has no place in medicine. But "accelerating adoption" in the current environment, where the oversight infrastructure genuinely hasn't caught up, feels like prioritizing speed over♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0032♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Moore♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at St. Mary's Medical Center in West Palm Beach in February 2023, and what I remember most from those two weeks is how much the nurses and doctors who actually looked at her, touched her arm, sat with her for a few minutes, mattered. Not the monitors. Not the software flagging her vitals. The people. So when I read that HHS wants to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, my stomach dropped a little.⏎⏎I want to focus on one thing the RFI asks about, which is what actions HHS can take to support AI adoption in clinical settings. My honest answer is: I don't think HHS should be asking how to speed this up. I think they should be asking whether to, and that's a completely different question that this whole RFI seems to skip over.⏎⏎The framing bothers me. "Accelerating adoption" assumes the destination is correct and the only problem is pace. But there are real questions nobody seems willing to sit with. Who is accountable when an AI system misses something a careful physician would have caught? I've read about the Epic deterioration model, which was studied at University of Michigan and found to have significant performance gaps for Black patients compared to white patients. That study came out in 2021. These aren't theoretical future problems, they are happening now, in hospitals that probably feel pretty confident they're using "validated" tools.⏎⏎And I keep thinking about what happens to the clinician who disagreed with the algorithm but went along with it anyway because the workflow made it easier to click through than to push back. That's not a hypothetical. That's how alert fatigue already works. We've spent years trying to fix alarm overload and now the proposal is to add more automated recommendations into the same environment where nurses already silence monitors because they go off constantly.⏎⏎I'm not saying there's zero value in any of this technology. I'm not that naive. But the RFI asks what regulatory actions could remove barriers to adoption, and the honest answer from where I'm standing is that some of those barriers exist for good reason. FDA clearance for a device doesn't tell a hospital how that device performs on their specific patient population. A billing code that reimburses for AI-assisted diagnosis doesn't tell the patient that the AI was trained mostly on data from academic medical centers that look nothing like the rural clinic where they're being seen.⏎⏎My mother was eventually diagnosed correctly, by a hospitalist who walked in at 6am and said something felt off to him and ordered an additional test. He was right. I don't know if he would have trusted that instinct if a dashboard had already told him she was stable. I genuinely don't know. That uncertainty is the thing I want HHS to sit with before they write new reimbursement codes and call it progress.⏎⏎The question of workforce effects is also something I'd want the Department to take seriously before issuing guidance that treats AI as unambiguously beneficial. Several hospitals have already announced staffing reductions justified partly by efficiency gains from automation. If the nurses who would have noticed my mother's change in condition aren't there because the hospital bought a monitoring platform instead, no algorithm makes up for that.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0033♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Thompson♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔Olive AI♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in February of 2023. I watched her doctors make decisions — hard ones, ones where I could see them weighing things that didn't fit on any checklist. At one point her cardiologist sat with me for twenty minutes just to explain why he was choosing one medication pathway over another, not because the numbers pointed one way, but because he knew her, knew she'd had a bad reaction to a related drug fifteen years ago, knew she was anxious about certain side effects. That conversation mattered. The decision mattered because a person made it.⏎⏎I keep thinking about that when I read about what HHS wants to do here. Accelerating AI adoption in clinical care sounds like a neutral, technical goal. It's not. It's a choice about what medicine is for.⏎⏎What worries me most isn't even the safety question, though that's real. It's what happens to accountability when you distribute a decision across an algorithm, a vendor, a hospital system, and a clinician who may or may not have actually reviewed the AI output before signing off. When something goes wrong — and things will go wrong — who answers for it? I genuinely don't know how you assign responsibility in that chain. I'm not sure regulators do either, and the fact that HHS is issuing an RFI rather than a framework kind of confirms that.⏎⏎There's also the bias issue, which I think gets glossed over in these conversations. These systems are trained on historical data. Historical data reflects historical inequities in who got care, what kind of care, and how their symptoms were documented. A tool trained on that data will reproduce those patterns. The health disparities researchers I've read — and I'm thinking specifically of work coming out of the Brigham and Women's research group on pulse oximetry errors in patients with darker skin tones — are documenting harms from existing clinical tools right now, before we've "accelerated" anything. Pushing more AI into more decisions faster does not seem like the right response to that problem.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the workforce piece because it doesn't come up enough. Nurses I know are already dealing with alert fatigue from existing clinical decision support tools. Adding more AI-generated flags, recommendations, and notifications doesn't reduce cognitive burden, it shifts and compounds it. The idea that AI will free up clinicians to spend more time with patients assumes that the time saved on documentation will actually flow back to patient contact, and there's not much evidence that's how it works in practice. Hospitals capture that time as productivity gains and increase patient loads.⏎⏎The question in the RFI about what regulatory or reimbursement changes HHS should make to accelerate adoption — I'd push back on the framing entirely. The question assumes acceleration is the goal. I think the more honest question is whether adoption of any particular AI application in any particular clinical context produces better outcomes for patients, and for which patients, and under what conditions of oversight. That's a much slower, harder, more expensive question to answer than "what rules can we change to get more AI into hospitals faster."⏎⏎I'm not opposed to technology in medicine. I'm opposed to the assumption that faster is better, and that efficiency gains at the system level translate into benefit at the level of an individual patient lying in a hospital bed trying to understand what's happening to her.⏎⏎The reimbursement angle in particular concerns me. If CMS creates billing codes or payment incentives tied to AI-assisted diagnosis or treatment planning before there's real evidence of safety and efficacy across diverse populations, you've built the economic structure before you've built the accountability structure, and that's going to be very hard to unwind once the financial incentives are in place.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0034♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Young♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Mercy General in Sacramento in February 2023 with what turned out to be a medication interaction that had been flagged — incorrectly — as low priority by the hospital's clinical decision support software. The nurse who caught it did so because she noticed my mother's color was off and she'd been a nurse for 22 years and something just felt wrong to her. I think about that a lot when I read things like this RFI.⏎⏎The question HHS seems to be asking is essentially: what can we do to speed this up? And I want to push back on that framing pretty hard. The question I'd want asked is: how do we know when it's safe enough, and who decides that, and what happens when it isn't?⏎⏎Because right now the honest answer to all three of those questions is murky at best. The liability question alone should give everyone pause. When an AI-assisted diagnostic tool misses something, or flags the wrong thing, or makes a recommendation that a tired resident follows without questioning it — who is responsible? The vendor? The hospital that licensed the tool? The doctor who clicked approve? I genuinely don't know and I don't think anyone does, and that's not a small gap to paper over with language about "governance frameworks."⏎⏎I also keep coming back to the workforce piece, which I notice tends to get framed in these discussions as a transition challenge rather than as a real harm. If hospitals replace medical scribes, junior coders, and intake staff with automated systems, those are real jobs held by real people, many of them in communities where those are among the better-paying positions available without a four-year degree. Calling that a "workforce evolution" doesn't make the disruption less serious for the people experiencing it.⏎⏎There's something else that's harder to articulate but I'll try. Medicine is not primarily an information-processing problem. A lot of what makes clinical care work is that a patient trusts that the person in the room with them is actually thinking about them, accountable to them in some human way. I'm not sure what happens to that trust when the recommendation on the screen was generated by a model trained on population-level data and nobody in the room can fully explain why it said what it said. I'm not anti-technology, I want to be clear about that. But there's a difference between a tool that helps a clinician do their job and a system that is effectively making the decision with the clinician serving as a kind of approval layer.⏎⏎HHS is asking specifically about regulatory or reimbursement changes that could accelerate adoption, and I'd actually argue the reimbursement angle is where the most caution is needed. If you build financial incentives for AI use into billing structures before the safety evidence base is solid, you're going to create pressure on providers to deploy these systems faster than is wise, and smaller practices and safety-net hospitals are going to feel that pressure most acutely because they have the least margin to absorb a bad outcome. The 2024 AMA physician survey found that around 38 percent of physicians felt pressure to use digital tools they weren't confident in — I'd expect that number to go up, not down, if reimbursement is tied to AI utilization in some way.⏎⏎I don't think HHS should be accelerating anything right now. The word "accelerating" in the title of this RFI is itself the problem I'd want the Department to sit with for a while.⏎⏎And honestly the informed consent piece hasn't been resolved either — whether patients have a right to know when AI tools contributed to their diagnosis or treatment plan, and in what way, and♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0035♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Martin♔♔Virginia♔♔United States♔American Academy of Allergy, Asthma and Immunology♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Mercy General in Sacramento in February 2023 with what turned out to be a pulmonary embolism. The first alert the monitoring system flagged was a sepsis warning. A nurse overrode it. Then another. The physician on call told us afterward that if they had simply followed what the algorithm was recommending, the treatment pathway would have been wrong and potentially dangerous. She said this to us almost as an aside, like it was just a normal Tuesday, which I suppose for her it was.⏎⏎I think about that a lot when I read about HHS wanting to "accelerate adoption."⏎⏎What bothers me most about this RFI is the framing. Acceleration is treated as an obvious good. The document reads like the main problem to solve is that hospitals aren't adopting AI fast enough, and that regulatory barriers are getting in the way of progress. But that's only the right framing if you already believe the technology is safe and effective and that the holdouts are just being slow or overly cautious. That's a big assumption.⏎⏎The question I'd want HHS to actually grapple with is: who is accountable when an AI system causes harm? Not in a legal liability sense, though that matters too. I mean in the practical, everyday sense. When my mother's care team overrode those alerts, they were taking on personal professional responsibility. They were using clinical judgment built over years of training and experience and they were putting their names on it. If the algorithm had been making those calls, or if the system had been designed to make override harder or require additional documentation as some of these tools do, what then? The diffusion of responsibility isn't just a philosophical concern. It changes how errors get caught, how they get reported, and whether anyone ever learns from them.⏎⏎I understand there are tasks where AI assistance seems genuinely lower-stakes. Scheduling, prior authorization paperwork, some kinds of documentation. I'm not totally opposed to everything here, I want to be honest about that. But the RFI doesn't make a lot of distinction between administrative functions and clinical decision support, and those are very different things with very different risk profiles. Lumping them together in a push for broad acceleration worries me.⏎⏎There's also a workforce piece that I don't think gets enough attention. Nurses I've talked to describe feeling increasingly like monitors of automated systems rather than caregivers. One told me her unit had gone from charting that took maybe 20 minutes per patient per shift to a new AI-assisted system that actually takes longer because it keeps generating suggestions she has to review and either accept or document why she's rejecting. That's not efficiency. And it's wearing people down in ways that I don't think show up in the metrics HHS is likely to be looking at.⏎⏎I'd ask HHS to require independent prospective evidence of clinical benefit before any AI tool is eligible for enhanced reimbursement, not post-market studies conducted by the vendors themselves. The FDA's existing review processes for software as a medical device are still fairly new and the evidence base is thin for a lot of what's already being deployed. Rushing reimbursement policy ahead of that evidence seems backwards.⏎⏎One more thing and then I'll stop. The populations most likely to be harmed by biased or poorly validated AI tools are the same populations that already receive worse care. There is published evidence going back to at least 2019, the Obermeyer et al. study in Science, showing racial bias embedded in widely used clinical algorithms. Accelerating deployment without mandatory bias audits and public reporting of performance across demographic subgroups is not a neutral act.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0036♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Smith♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔American Association of Physician Associates♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Regions Hospital in St. Paul in February of 2023 and I can tell you that what kept her alive was not an algorithm. It was a night nurse named Darnell who noticed something was off about her breathing at 2 a.m., something that wasn't showing up in the monitors the way it should have, and he called the attending. That kind of judgment doesn't come from training data.⏎⏎I keep thinking about how this RFI frames the whole question as one of acceleration. Like the problem is that we're not moving fast enough. But I haven't seen anyone in this process seriously grapple with what happens when an AI system gets something wrong and there's no clear person to hold accountable. Who do you sue? Who loses their license? The hospital that bought the software? The company that built the model on data that may or may not have reflected patients like my mother, who is Hmong, 71 years old, and whose presentation of symptoms apparently doesn't always match the "standard" profile these systems are trained on?⏎⏎That's the thing that bothers me most. The bias problem isn't hypothetical. There is documented evidence going back years -- the Obermeyer study in Science from 2019 is probably the most cited but it's far from the only one -- showing that these systems encode the health inequities that already exist in our data. Accelerating adoption means accelerating that problem into more hospitals, more decisions, faster.⏎⏎I'm specifically responding to the question about what regulatory actions HHS could take to support AI adoption in clinical settings. My answer is that HHS should first require, before any further adoption, mandatory prospective bias audits stratified by race, ethnicity, age, and disability status, with results published publicly before any system gets deployed in a Medicare or Medicaid-participating facility. Not retrospective. Not voluntary. Required before the system goes live. Right now there's essentially nothing stopping a hospital from buying a sepsis prediction tool, deploying it, and only finding out two years later that it was systematically undertriaging Black patients.⏎⏎And the reimbursement question is related. When CMS creates billing codes that reward the use of AI-assisted diagnosis or prior authorization, you're creating a financial incentive that will push providers toward these tools even when their clinical judgment says otherwise. The pressure to use the AI becomes economic. A doctor who overrides the system's recommendation has to document why, takes on liability, slows down the workflow. So the "human in the loop" language that appears in a lot of these proposals is mostly fiction once you factor in what the actual working conditions look like.⏎⏎I'm not saying there's no role for any of this technology ever. I understand that documentation burden is genuinely terrible and if AI can help with that, maybe fine. But the framing of this RFI assumes the direction is correct and we're just debating speed and implementation details. That assumption should be questioned.⏎⏎There's also something that gets glossed over in all the efficiency language, which is that patients trust their doctors partly because they know there's a human being who can be held accountable and who is exercising judgment on their behalf. When my mother was in that ICU, she was scared, she was in a foreign-feeling environment, she didn't always understand what was being said to her. The relationship with the nurses and the physician was what gave her some ground to stand on. I don't know how you quantify that in a cost-benefit analysis but it seems like something worth protecting before we automate it away.⏎⏎One more thing and then I'll stop -- I noticed the RFI mentions "reducing administrative burden" as a key goal and I'd genuinely like to know what percentage of the AI tools currently being marketed to health systems are actually targeting clinical decision support versus billing, prior authorization, and denial management. Because those are very different things with very different risk profiles and the RFI seems to treat them somewhat♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0037♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthony♔Baker♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was admitted to St. Luke's Hospital in Cedar Rapids in February of 2023 with what turned out to be a pulmonary embolism. She almost died. Not because the doctors were bad — they were good, attentive people — but because the initial triage software flagged her as low-risk based on her vitals and she sat in the waiting room for nearly four hours. I don't know what system they were using. Nobody would tell us. That's the part that still makes me angry, honestly. Not just that it happened, but that we couldn't get a straight answer about who made the call or what tool was involved or who was responsible for the outcome if she had died.⏎⏎So when I see HHS talking about "accelerating" AI adoption in clinical care, I have to say something.⏎⏎I want to be fair here. I understand there are real problems with healthcare access and physician burnout and administrative waste and all of that. I'm not saying technology has no role. But there's a difference between using software to schedule appointments and using it to make or inform decisions about whether someone's chest pain is serious. And I worry that the way this RFI is framed, the whole thrust of it is toward speed and adoption and deployment, and the questions about risk and accountability feel like they're kind of bolted on at the end.⏎⏎The question I keep coming back to is: when an AI system makes a wrong recommendation and someone is harmed, who is accountable? Right now the answer is basically nobody, or at least nobody clear. The hospital says the physician made the final call. The physician says the software presented the information a certain way. The software vendor says the tool is not intended to diagnose. I've read about enough of these situations to know that accountability diffuses in exactly this way, and I don't see anything in what HHS is proposing that fixes that before deployment speeds up.⏎⏎There's also the bias problem, which I think deserves more than a footnote. We know, and there's been published research on this going back to at least 2019, that several widely used clinical algorithms produce worse outcomes for Black patients because they were trained on data that didn't represent those populations fairly. Accelerating the adoption of tools that carry that problem baked in is not a neutral act. It's choosing to move fast at the expense of people who already get worse care.⏎⏎I also want to say something about what medicine actually is, because I think it gets lost in these conversations. When a doctor sits with a patient and asks them questions and watches their face and notices that they're scared or that something doesn't quite add up with what they're saying, that's not inefficiency. That's diagnosis. That's the whole thing. I don't know how you replicate that with a model trained on structured data from eHR systems, and I'm skeptical of anyone who says they've solved it.⏎⏎I'm not asking HHS to ban AI in healthcare. I'm asking the Department to stop treating acceleration as the goal. Prove safety first. Prove that the liability framework is clear before the tools are everywhere. Require that patients be told when AI is involved in their care — not buried in consent forms but actually told, in plain language. And slow down on the insurance and prior authorization side especially, because that's where denials happen and people die waiting.⏎⏎The RFI asks specifically about regulatory actions the Department can take. The most important one, in my view, is mandatory prospective bias auditing before any clinical AI tool can be used at scale, with results that are public and updated regularly, not just a one-time certification check done by the company selling the product.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0038♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patricia♔Gonzalez♔♔Maine♔♔United States♔AdvaMed♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Mercy General in Sacramento in February of 2023 for what turned out to be a perforated bowel, and for about six hours the clinical decision support software kept flagging her symptoms as likely anxiety and dehydration. The nurses kept telling me the system wasn't "flagging anything serious." She almost died. A surgeon finally looked at her himself, without the computer, and took her to the OR within the hour.⏎⏎I don't know how to fit that story into whatever framework HHS is using to think about this, but I keep coming back to it when I read language like "accelerating adoption." Accelerate toward what, exactly.⏎⏎What I want to address is the question about removing barriers to AI use in clinical settings, because I think that framing is backwards. The barriers aren't bureaucratic inconveniences. Some of them are the only thing standing between patients and a system that will make confident wrong decisions very fast. Speed is not a virtue here. The thing that saved my mother was a doctor who slowed down, who ignored the software, who put his hands on her abdomen and made a judgment call. That is not a replicable process. You cannot train a model to have the accumulated instinct of a surgeon who has seen ten thousand patients.⏎⏎I understand there are genuine administrative burdens in healthcare and I'm not pretending otherwise. Prior authorization is a disaster, I agree with that. Documentation requirements are eating physicians alive. But the answer to those problems isn't to hand the whole clinical enterprise over to systems that we do not fully understand and cannot fully audit. And I worry that "reducing friction" for AI adoption is really code for reducing accountability. If an algorithm denies someone coverage or misses a diagnosis, who is responsible? The vendor? The hospital? The doctor who was supposed to supervise but had forty patients that day?⏎⏎There's research from the Stanford Medicine group published in 2022 showing that several widely used diagnostic algorithms perform significantly worse on Black patients than white patients, because they were trained on non-representative data. That's not a hypothetical bias problem, that's an actual documented disparity affecting real people right now. And HHS wants to accelerate the deployment of more tools like this before we have mandatory performance standards, mandatory demographic disaggregation of outcomes data, or any real enforcement mechanism if a tool fails a particular population.⏎⏎I'm not a technologist, I'm a retired middle school science teacher and a caregiver, and I probably don't know all the right regulatory vocabulary for this comment. But I do know that medicine is built on trust between a person and another person, and that trust cannot survive if patients learn that their diagnosis came from a product owned by a private company with a financial incentive to produce certain outcomes. That's not a paranoid concern, insurance companies have already used algorithmic tools to deny post-acute care in ways that courts have found to be inappropriate. The UnitedHealth NAAS algorithm litigation is a matter of public record.⏎⏎The RFI asks what HHS can do to support adoption. My answer is: before supporting adoption, do the work to understand what adoption has already cost. Fund independent studies of existing AI deployments. Require hospitals to report adverse events that involved AI-assisted decision making, separately from other adverse events, so we can actually see the data. Make that data public. Don't set up a voluntary reporting system because voluntary means we'll only hear the success stories.⏎⏎I'm also honestly not sure the workforce question is being taken seriously enough. If AI handles more and more of the cognitive work in clinical settings, what happens to the training of the next generation of physicians and nurses? Clinical judgment develops through practice, through making decisions and being responsible for them. If residents spend their training years reviewing AI outputs rather than reasoning through cases themselves, what are we actually producing at the end of that pipeline. I don't have a citation for this, it's just a concern that seems obvious to me and I haven't seen it addressed anywhere in the materials I've read.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0039♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Wilson♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February of 2023. I watched her physicians — actual human beings who knew her chart, who had spoken with our family, who understood that she had a terror of ventilators going back to watching her own father die on one — make decisions that no algorithm would have made the same way. She came home. I don't know if she would have if those decisions had been filtered through a system optimized for outcomes data.⏎⏎That's not an argument against technology in general. I use electronic records, I understand telehealth has helped people in rural areas get access they wouldn't otherwise have. But what HHS is describing in this RFI is different. The framing of the whole thing is about acceleration. That word is doing a lot of work here and I don't think anyone is stopping to ask whether faster is actually what patients need.⏎⏎What bothers me most is the question about reimbursement incentives for AI-assisted clinical decisions. Because once you tie payment to AI use, you have changed the entire structure of how a physician thinks about their own judgment. They are no longer just weighing what they know about the patient. They are weighing what they know about the patient against a system that has been financially incentivized into the room with them. That is a slow-moving kind of harm that won't show up in any short-term outcomes study.⏎⏎There's also the bias problem, which I know gets mentioned a lot but I think people treat it as though it's a solvable technical issue. Dermatology AI trained predominantly on lighter skin tones, sepsis prediction models that underperform for Black patients — these aren't bugs that get patched in the next update. They reflect what data was available, which reflects decades of who got care and who didn't. Running those systems faster and broader doesn't fix that. It scales it.⏎⏎I keep coming back to accountability. When a physician makes a wrong call, there is a person responsible. There is a license, a board, a legal framework. When an AI system contributes to a wrong call, what happens? The hospital says the physician should have overridden it. The vendor says the system was only advisory. The physician says the alert fatigue from seventeen other AI flags that morning made it impossible to evaluate one more. Everyone has a defense. Nobody is responsible. That is a genuinely new problem and I have not seen anything in this RFI that treats it seriously.⏎⏎I'm not saying physicians are infallible or that the status quo doesn't have its own serious problems. But the answer to a flawed system is not to hand more of it over to tools that we do not yet understand well enough to deploy at scale, under time pressure, in emergency conditions, with sick and vulnerable people.⏎⏎The RFI asks what regulatory barriers might be slowing adoption. I would ask instead what safeguards are missing that should be required before adoption proceeds any further than it already has. The FDA cleared over 500 AI-enabled medical devices as of late 2022, and the post-market surveillance requirements for most of them are not remotely comparable to what would be required for a new drug.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0040♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔Mitchell♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at St. Mary's Medical Center in Evansville, Indiana in February of 2023 for what turned out to be a pulmonary embolism. The AI-assisted triage tool flagged her as low-risk. The nurse overrode it. My mother is alive.⏎⏎I don't tell that story to be dramatic about it. I tell it because when I read that HHS wants to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, my first reaction is -- accelerate toward what, exactly?⏎⏎There's a question in this RFI about what barriers are slowing AI adoption and how HHS can remove them. And I want to push back on the whole premise of that question. The "barriers" being described are, in many cases, just... caution. Human judgment. Liability structures that exist because someone has to be accountable when a patient is harmed. Removing those things in the name of efficiency doesn't feel like progress to me.⏎⏎What bothers me most is the way this framing treats speed as inherently good. The assumption seems to be that if hospitals aren't adopting AI fast enough, something is wrong with the regulatory environment. But some of those hospitals are probably slow for reasons that are completely legitimate. The doctors and nurses who are skeptical of these tools aren't just being technophobic. They've watched electronic health record systems get rolled out with enormous promises and then spent ten years doing data entry at midnight.⏎⏎The bias problem also doesn't get taken seriously enough in documents like this. There's real research, including work published in Science in 2019 by Obermeyer and colleagues, showing that a widely used commercial algorithm was systematically underestimating the health needs of Black patients. That wasn't a fringe system. It was used across hundreds of hospitals. And the response from the industry was largely to say they'd fix it going forward. What happened to the patients who were undertreated during the years it was running? What accountability structure addressed that?⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no place in medicine. I'm genuinely uncertain about some of this. There are probably imaging applications where the technology is mature enough to be useful and where the error modes are well understood. I don't want to be absolutist.⏎⏎But the RFI is asking how to remove barriers to adoption broadly, across clinical care settings, and that scope concerns me. A tool that helps a radiologist in a well-resourced academic center read a chest CT is not the same as an AI system making prior authorization decisions for a Medicaid patient in rural Mississippi. Treating those as the same category of "AI in healthcare" and asking how to accelerate both of them together is not careful policymaking.⏎⏎The workforce question also keeps nagging at me and I feel like it gets buried. If AI tools reduce the number of nurses, medical coders, or diagnostic staff that hospitals feel they need to employ, we are not saving the healthcare system money in any meaningful sense. We are shifting costs onto those workers and onto communities that lose middle-income jobs. The RFI mentions workforce briefly but frames it mostly as retraining, which is the standard answer and not really an answer.⏎⏎I guess what I'd ask HHS to do is stop and consider whether "accelerating adoption" is actually the right goal right now, or whether something more like "building the infrastructure for safe and accountable adoption" would be better. Those aren't the same thing and the difference in timeline could be measured in years.⏎⏎The nurse who overrode that algorithm at St. Mary's has probably been trained on the new system they rolled out in late 2024. I have no idea whether that training included any protocol for when to trust the tool and when not to.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0041♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Anderson♔♔Alabama♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Advocate Lutheran General Hospital in Park Ridge, Illinois in March of 2023. During that time, I watched nurses and physicians make dozens of judgment calls that no algorithm could have made, things like reading her face when she couldn't speak, noticing that she was frightened by a particular resident and adjusting who came into the room, deciding when to push her and when to let her rest. She survived. I don't think she would have if the people caring for her had been managing dashboards instead of managing her.⏎⏎That's the thing I keep coming back to when I read about HHS wanting to accelerate AI adoption. Accelerate. That word is doing a lot of work here and I think it deserves more scrutiny than this RFI seems to give it.⏎⏎The question the RFI asks about barriers to adoption kind of assumes the answer. It treats AI integration as an obvious good and asks what's in the way. But what if some of those barriers exist for real reasons? Physician hesitancy about AI diagnostic tools isn't irrational resistance to change. A lot of it comes from the fact that these tools fail in ways that are hard to detect and the failure often falls on the patient without anyone being clearly responsible. When an AI flags the wrong risk level for a patient and that influences a discharge decision, who carries that? The vendor says the physician should have overridden it. The physician says the system recommended discharge. The hospital says everyone followed protocol. The patient is readmitted four days later or isn't readmitted at all.⏎⏎There's also a workforce issue that this RFI barely touches on and I want to say something about it because I think it matters. If AI systems are absorbing the documentation burden, the prior authorization back-and-forth, the triage scoring, the scheduling logic, eventually you are describing a healthcare workforce that is smaller and less skilled because those tasks are where a lot of clinical judgement actually gets developed. Residents learn by doing the tedious stuff. If the tedious stuff is automated away, what exactly are we training them on. This isn't hypothetical, there are already hospitals piloting ambient AI scribes and the early reports from nurses and residents are mixed in ways that the promotional materials from the vendors don't reflect.⏎⏎I'm genuinely not opposed to technology in medicine. I'm not a luddite about this. But there is a meaningful difference between a tool that helps a clinician do something they were already doing and a system that makes autonomous determinations that clinicians are then expected to rubber-stamp because the workflow assumes the AI is probably right. The second thing is what worries me and I think it's where a lot of the commercial pressure is actually pushing.⏎⏎The RFI asks about regulatory actions HHS could take to accelerate adoption. I would ask instead what accountability structures need to be in place before adoption happens at scale. What happens when a patient is harmed by an AI recommendation and the model weights are proprietary. What is the patient's right to know that an algorithm was involved in their care. These questions don't have answers yet and the answer to that isn't to move faster.⏎⏎One more thing. The framing of efficiency throughout this document bothers me. Efficiency for whom. Hospital margins are not the same thing as patient outcomes and I have seen enough to know they can point in opposite directions.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0042♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Thomas♔♔Wyoming♔♔United States♔American Psychological Association♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Emory University Hospital in February of 2023, and I can tell you that what kept her alive was not an algorithm. It was a nurse named Delores who noticed at 2 in the morning that something looked off with my mother's color, something that wasn't in any chart, and called the attending. That judgment call — that human perception of something unquantifiable — led to them catching a PE that the monitoring system had not flagged.⏎⏎I keep thinking about that when I read about HHS wanting to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings. Accelerate. That word bothers me. It assumes the direction is already correct and the only question is speed.⏎⏎My specific concern, since you asked for comment, is on the question of how AI tools get evaluated before being deployed in care settings. Right now my understanding is that the framework for validating these tools is genuinely thin. A hospital system can purchase an AI-assisted diagnostic or triage product, deploy it, and the burden of proving it causes harm falls almost entirely on patients and clinicians after the fact. The FDA's current approach to software as a medical device has gaps that the agency itself has acknowledged, and adding pressure to move faster doesn't close those gaps, it just means more patients are exposed to unvalidated systems before anyone knows whether they work.⏎⏎What worries me more than any single failure is the diffusion of responsibility. When an AI recommends a course of treatment and a clinician follows it and a patient is harmed, who is accountable? The vendor will say the clinician made the final call. The clinician will say the tool's output was a significant factor in their reasoning. The hospital will say they followed vendor implementation guidelines. I don't think HHS has seriously wrestled with this question, and rushing adoption makes it worse.⏎⏎There's also the workforce piece, which I feel like gets framed as either "AI will help overburdened clinicians" or "AI will replace jobs" and neither framing is quite right. What I've heard from nurses and residents I know is that these tools often add cognitive load rather than reducing it — they generate alerts, they require documentation, they surface recommendations that still need to be evaluated, and if you don't follow the recommendation you often have to explain why. That's not relief. That's more work with a veneer of automation over it.⏎⏎I want to be fair. I'm not opposed to technology in medicine in some blanket way. I understand that diagnostic imaging AI has shown real promise in certain narrow applications. But "accelerate adoption" as a policy posture treats all of these applications as if they're in the same category, and they're not. An AI that helps a radiologist flag potential nodules for a second look is a different thing entirely from an AI that an insurer uses to determine whether a hospitalization was medically necessary.⏎⏎That last use case — AI in insurance determinations — should probably not be in this conversation at all. Using algorithmic tools to deny or limit coverage is not clinical care. It is a financial decision dressed up in clinical language. The ProPublica and CBS News reporting from 2023 on how some insurers were using AI review tools to systematically deny post-acute care claims, sometimes overriding physician recommendations at rates above 80 percent, should have prompted much more regulatory attention than it got.⏎⏎I don't know exactly what the right framework looks like, but I'm fairly certain that starting from a posture of acceleration, and asking the public to help figure out how to speed up adoption, is asking the wrong question.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0043♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Paul♔Martin♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔Sickle Cell Disease Association of America♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent four days in a hospital in Tucson in February 2023 after a fall, and during that time I watched three different nurses spend more time staring at screens and clicking through dropdown menus than they did looking at her. That's before any serious AI integration. I keep thinking about what happens when you add another layer of algorithmic recommendation on top of that already mediated experience.⏎⏎The question HHS seems to be asking is essentially "how do we go faster" and I want to push back on the premise. Faster toward what exactly? The RFI frames this as an adoption problem, like the barrier to AI in clinical care is regulatory friction and reimbursement gaps. But I don't think that's the actual barrier for most patients or most clinicians who have reservations. The barrier is that nobody has answered the basic question of who is responsible when an AI-assisted diagnosis is wrong.⏎⏎This isn't a hypothetical concern. There was a widely reported situation involving sepsis prediction algorithms at several health systems where the tools were generating alerts that nurses learned to ignore because the false positive rate was so high. I believe the work on this came out of UCSF around 2019 or 2020 though I may be misremembering the institution. The point being that "adoption" of a tool that clinicians learn to tune out creates its own set of problems that aren't obviously better than the baseline.⏎⏎What concerns me most is the insurance and prior authorization piece. I know that's somewhat adjacent to the clinical care framing in this RFI but it's hard to separate them in practice. When an AI system is making or substantially informing denial decisions, and the appeals process requires a patient to navigate a system that was designed to minimize approvals, the efficiency gains accrue entirely to the payer and the burden lands on the sickest people. I've seen this in my own family's experience with prior auth and adding algorithmic speed to that process without adding accountability mechanisms seems backwards.⏎⏎I also think the workforce question deserves more than the passing mention it tends to get in these conversations. If AI systems handle more documentation, more triaging, more of the cognitive work that currently requires trained humans, what happens to the pipeline of clinical workers who develop expertise by doing those tasks? Residency programs, nursing education, the whole system of learning-by-doing medicine depends on humans actually doing things. I don't have a citation for this, it's more of an intuition, but it seems like a real structural risk that the efficiency framing just doesn't capture.⏎⏎There's also the bias issue which I know gets raised constantly and maybe feels like a talking point by now but it genuinely isn't resolved. Training data reflects historical patterns of care, and historical patterns of care in the United States have systematically undertreated Black patients, undertreated women with pain, overtreated some populations for profit-driven reasons. An AI system trained on that data doesn't neutralize those patterns, it operationalizes them at scale and makes them harder to see because the decision appears objective.⏎⏎I understand HHS has a mandate to think about efficiency and access and I'm not saying AI has zero role in healthcare. But this RFI reads like the conclusion has already been reached and the comment period is about implementation logistics. The question of whether to accelerate this, right now, without liability frameworks, without meaningful bias auditing requirements, without answers to the accountability question, that deserves more than a footnote in the framing.⏎⏎The specific ask I'd make is that HHS not proceed with any reimbursement incentives for AI-assisted clinical tools until there is a working framework for how malpractice and liability attach when those tools contribute to patient harm. That seems like it should come first but I don't see it addressed anywhere in the RFI questions I reviewed.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0044♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donald♔Jones♔♔Missouri♔♔United States♔Imagen Technologies♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in February 2023, and I watched the nurses and doctors make dozens of judgment calls that I cannot imagine any algorithm getting right. When her blood pressure dropped at 2am, the nurse on duty made a face I'll never forget -- she said something felt off before the monitors even alarmed. That's not a data point. I don't know how to put that into a training set.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS wants to "accelerate" AI adoption in clinical care, my gut reaction is: slow down. Please.⏎⏎What worries me most, and what I think this RFI kind of sidesteps, is the question of who is responsible when an AI system gets it wrong. Not theoretically wrong. Actually wrong, with a patient harmed. The way these tools are being deployed right now, it seems like liability just sort of evaporates. The hospital says the vendor is responsible, the vendor says the clinician had final sign-off, and somewhere in that chain the patient or their family is left with nothing. I've read enough about the Epic sepsis prediction model -- which was studied pretty extensively and found to miss a lot of cases while also firing off false alerts constantly -- to know that "FDA cleared" or "clinically validated" doesn't mean what people assume it means.⏎⏎And the workforce piece keeps getting buried. I know nurses and residents who already feel like they're managing the software more than the patient. Charting requirements are crushing people, and now the answer is supposed to be AI assistants that summarize notes and auto-populate fields? Every time I hear that framing I think about how those summaries can flatten or distort a patient's actual presentation. A missed qualifier, a wrong emphasis. It doesn't take much.⏎⏎I want to be fair here. I'm not saying there's zero use for any of this technology. There probably are specific narrow applications where pattern recognition in imaging, for instance, genuinely helps. I'm not a clinician and I don't want to pretend I have all the answers. But the RFI is asking what HHS can do to accelerate adoption, and the framing itself troubles me. The word "accelerate" assumes the direction is right and the only variable is speed. I'm not sure that assumption has been earned.⏎⏎The bias issue also deserves a lot more attention than it seems to be getting in this conversation. Algorithms trained on historical clinical data are going to encode historical disparities in care. This is documented. It's not speculative. A 2019 paper in Science by Obermeyer and colleagues showed a widely used commercial algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients. That study is now several years old and I'm not confident the underlying problem has been solved -- I've seen nothing in this RFI that suggests HHS has a concrete plan for auditing deployed systems for disparate impact on an ongoing basis, not just at the point of initial approval.⏎⏎The reimbursement incentives also concern me, though I'll be honest that I understand the regulatory details there less well than some other commenters probably do. My general worry is that if CMS starts paying for AI-assisted care at higher rates, hospitals will feel pressure to adopt tools even when the evidence for them in their specific patient population isn't there yet.⏎⏎I guess what I'd ask HHS to do is reframe the question entirely. Instead of asking how to accelerate adoption, ask what conditions would need to be true before acceleration is appropriate. That seems like the more honest starting point, and I don't think we're♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0045♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Lee♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Scripps Mercy in San Diego in February 2023 after a sepsis diagnosis that was initially missed. What saved her was a night-shift nurse named Dolores who noticed something was off with her coloring and pushed back on the attending physician's initial read. Not an algorithm. A person who had been doing that job for nineteen years and trusted her own eyes.⏎⏎I keep thinking about that when I read about HHS wanting to accelerate AI in clinical care settings. Accelerate. That word bothers me. It assumes the direction is already correct and we just need to move faster. I'm not sure that's true.⏎⏎What concerns me most, and I'll focus on this because it's what I actually know something about, is the question of who is responsible when an AI system gets something wrong. Right now there's no clear answer to that. The vendor says the tool is decision-support only, the hospital says the physician had final say, the physician says the algorithm flagged it as low-risk and there were twelve other patients that hour. Everybody points somewhere else. That's not a hypothetical scenario, that's already happening. There were cases reported in STAT News going back to 2021 and 2022 where sepsis prediction tools deployed at major health systems generated so many false positives that nursing staff started ignoring the alerts entirely. Which means the tool didn't just fail to help, it actively made things worse by training clinicians to tune out warnings.⏎⏎And I understand the argument on the other side. Physician burnout is real, administrative burden is crushing people, there are genuine access problems in rural areas where AI might help fill gaps. I don't dismiss any of that. But I think HHS is asking the wrong question when it asks how to accelerate adoption. The question should be whether specific tools in specific contexts have actually demonstrated benefit, for which patients, under what conditions, with what failure modes documented and disclosed. That's a much slower, harder process than what I see being proposed.⏎⏎There's also something that doesn't get talked about enough which is what this does to the patient's experience of being cared for. When I sat with my mother in that ICU, the moments that mattered were when a human being looked at her and made a judgment call that took her seriously as a person. I don't know how to quantify that. I'm not sure it can be quantified. But I think we're moving toward a model of care where that gets systematically devalued because it doesn't show up in efficiency metrics, and the people making these decisions are not the ones who will be lying in those beds.⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to adoption and how to remove them. I'd ask HHS to also seriously consider what we might be removing when we remove those barriers. Some friction in the system exists for reasons.⏎⏎I don't have a specific regulatory proposal to offer here. I'm a patient advocate, not a lawyer. But I would at minimum want to see any acceleration effort paired with mandatory, independent post-market surveillance requirements with publicly accessible results, not summaries written by the vendors themselves. The FDA's current framework for software as a medical device has real gaps in how it handles continuous learning systems, and those gaps need to be closed before we♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0046♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Smith♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Emory University Hospital in January of 2023, and during that time I watched physicians make decisions that no algorithm could have made. One of her attending doctors sat with us for forty minutes on a Tuesday night, not because the chart told him to, but because he could see we were falling apart. He adjusted her care plan three times based on things he observed at the bedside that weren't in any data field. She survived. I don't know if she would have if someone had been optimizing for throughput.⏎⏎I keep thinking about that when I read materials like this RFI. The framing of the whole document assumes that the problem in clinical care is speed and efficiency, and that AI is the answer to that problem. But I'm not convinced the diagnosis is right, and I'm pretty worried that accelerating AI adoption before we understand the failure modes is going to hurt people who are already vulnerable.⏎⏎The question I want to address is the one about barriers to adoption and how HHS can reduce them. My answer is: please don't. Or at least, not yet, and not across the board. The word "barriers" implies that caution is the problem. Some of those barriers are just... caution. Which is appropriate. Medicine has a long history of technologies that seemed obviously helpful and turned out to cause harm. The problem isn't that hospitals are being timid. The problem is that we don't have good evidence on outcomes, we don't have clear liability frameworks, and we have almost no longitudinal data on what happens when AI tools are embedded in clinical workflows for three, five, ten years.⏎⏎There's also the bias issue, which I feel like gets mentioned and then sort of moved past in these policy conversations. Tools trained on historical data will encode historical disparities. A study published in Science back in 2019 found that a widely used algorithm for allocating health care resources was significantly less likely to refer Black patients for additional care compared to white patients with the same level of medical need. That wasn't a fringe finding. That was in one of the most prestigious journals in the world. And the response from the field was essentially: we'll fix it going forward. But if HHS accelerates adoption now, we're scaling tools before we know which ones have this problem and how bad it is.⏎⏎I guess what bothers me most is the accountability question. When a physician makes a bad call, there's a framework for that. It's imperfect, but it exists. When an AI tool contributes to a bad outcome, who is responsible? The hospital that deployed it? The vendor who trained it? The doctor who clicked through the recommendation? I have not seen a satisfying answer to this anywhere, and I've been reading about this topic for two years now. The RFI doesn't really grapple with it either, it mostly talks about innovation and adoption rates.⏎⏎And separately, though I realize this is harder to quantify, there is something about the doctor-patient relationship that I think gets lost when we treat it as an information processing problem. My mother's doctor made a judgment call based on knowing her as a person. I don't know how you build that into a model, and I'm not sure the people pushing for rapid AI deployment have seriously asked the question.⏎⏎The workforce piece is also underdiscussed. If AI handles documentation, triage support, diagnostic assistance, prior auth reviews, and discharge planning, what does that do to clinical training over the next twenty years? Residents learn by doing those things. I've talked to two medical residents who said they already feel like they're being trained to supervise systems rather than to♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0047♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Smith♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February 2023 with what turned out to be a pulmonary embolism. The attending physician told us afterward that an AI-assisted triage tool had flagged her condition as "low acuity" based on her initial vitals. He overrode it. She's alive because he overrode it.⏎⏎That's the thing I keep thinking about when I read materials like this RFI. HHS is asking how to accelerate AI adoption, but the question itself assumes the answer. Faster. More. Scale it up. And nobody seems to want to ask what happens when the physician who would have overridden the algorithm has been restructured out of the workflow because the hospital decided the AI was good enough and the human layer was redundant overhead.⏎⏎I'm not a doctor. I work in healthcare administration, have for about fourteen years, mostly on the operations side. So I see both the appeal of these tools and what gets lost in the pitch decks. What gets lost is that when something goes wrong with an algorithm's recommendation, nobody can tell you clearly whose fault it is. The vendor says the clinician should have used their judgment. The clinician says the tool told them one thing and they followed standard protocol. The hospital says the tool was FDA-cleared. Everyone has a defensible position and the patient has a bad outcome and a very confusing path to any kind of accountability.⏎⏎Regarding the question in the RFI about what regulatory actions HHS could take to support broader AI use in clinical settings — I'd push back on the framing entirely. The regulatory gap right now isn't that there are too many barriers to adoption. The gap is that there's almost no coherent framework for what happens after something goes wrong. Who bears liability when an AI-assisted diagnostic tool contributes to a missed diagnosis? Current law doesn't answer this cleanly. Hospitals know this, which is why their contracts with AI vendors are structured the way they are — I've seen a few of these agreements and the indemnification clauses are extraordinary in how completely they shift risk onto the provider institution and away from the developer.⏎⏎Accelerating adoption before that accountability structure exists seems like it's going to produce a situation where the technology is everywhere and the legal and ethical framework for managing harm is still being argued about in law review articles.⏎⏎There's also something I don't see discussed enough in these conversations, which is what this does to the physician-patient relationship over time. Not in a dramatic way, but incrementally. When a patient asks their doctor "why are you recommending this treatment," and the honest answer involves some weight given to a model's output, that changes the nature of the conversation. The physician may not fully understand the model. They may not be able to explain it. And the patient is supposed to give informed consent to a recommendation that the physician themselves can only partially account for.⏎⏎I understand the efficiency arguments. Documentation burden is real, burnout is real, rural access gaps are real. I'm not saying AI has no role. But HHS accelerating adoption through reimbursement incentives and regulatory streamlining, before there's even a standard approach to algorithmic auditing or bias testing across different patient populations, seems like it's getting the order of operations exactly backwards.⏎⏎The bias question alone should give the department serious pause. There's published research — the Obermeyer et al. study from 2019 being probably the most cited — showing how commercial algorithms can systematically underestimate the health needs of Black patients. That study was about one algorithm used by one vendor. Nobody knows how many similar problems exist in tools currently deployed across hospital systems because there's no mandatory post-market surveillance requirement with any real teeth.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0048♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Karen♔Harris♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔Blue Cross Blue Shield Association♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was discharged from Mercy General in Cincinnati in March of 2023 after a five-day admission for a pulmonary embolism. The hospitalist who treated her was excellent. He sat with her for forty minutes on the day she left, went through her medications, asked about her home situation, noticed she seemed anxious and not quite absorbing everything he was saying. He slowed down. He asked her what she was worried about. That conversation caught something that the discharge paperwork didn't — she was scared to take the blood thinner because her neighbor had told her it caused strokes. He addressed that directly. She took the medication. She's fine.⏎⏎I keep thinking about what would have happened if that interaction had been "optimized." If an AI system had generated a discharge summary and flagged her as low-risk based on her vitals and age and comorbidity scores. If the clinical decision support tool had said she was ready to go and moved on to the next patient. The judgment that mattered was not in any data field. It was in reading a person's face.⏎⏎HHS is asking how it can accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. I want to push back on the framing of that question pretty hard. The question assumes the goal is acceleration. It treats speed of adoption as a proxy for quality. That's the wrong way to think about this.⏎⏎The RFI mentions benefits like reducing administrative burden and improving diagnostic accuracy and I don't want to be completely dismissive of those things. Some of that is real. But the way this request is framed, it reads like the Department has already decided the direction and is now asking for help clearing obstacles. That's not the same as genuinely asking whether this is the right path at the pace being proposed.⏎⏎What worries me most is the accountability question. When an AI-assisted system contributes to a wrong diagnosis or a denied insurance claim or a missed deterioration event, who is responsible? Right now the answers being proposed by industry are genuinely slippery. The vendor says the clinician should have caught it. The clinician says the system told them it was fine. The hospital says it followed the approved protocol. The patient is harmed and no one is accountable in any meaningful way. I've read through several of the liability frameworks being floated and they mostly protect the technology developers while leaving patients with very limited recourse.⏎⏎There's also something I don't see discussed enough in these policy documents, which is what happens to clinical reasoning when you outsource pieces of it to automated systems over many years. Medical training involves building judgment through practice, through uncertainty, through being wrong and figuring out why. If residents and attending physicians increasingly rely on algorithmic recommendations, what happens to that capacity over time? I don't think anyone knows the answer to that. It's not a reason to never use any tools, but it's a reason to be much more careful than this RFI suggests.⏎⏎The workforce piece also deserves more than a passing mention. There are real people who do coding, prior authorization review, scheduling, transcription. The argument that AI will free clinicians from administrative tasks assumes those tasks just disappear. They don't. Someone does them. And when that someone is an algorithm making coverage decisions, patients lose a human point of contact who could exercise discretion, who could notice something off, who could escalate.⏎⏎I would ask HHS to at minimum require pre-deployment clinical evidence at the same standard we'd require for a drug or device before endorsing or creating reimbursement incentives for specific AI applications. The FDA's current framework for software as a medical device has significant gaps and the De Novo pathway has been used in ways that I don't think most clinicians are aware of when they start using these tools in practice.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0049♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Jones♔♔Wisconsin♔♔United States♔United Cerebral Palsy♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Mercy General in Sacramento for six weeks in 2019 after a misdiagnosed cardiac event, and what I kept seeing during that time was how little the nurses and doctors actually had time to sit with her. They were always looking at screens. Always documenting. That experience made me very skeptical of any proposal that says the answer to medicine being too impersonal and too rushed is to add more technology to it.⏎⏎What worries me about this RFI is that the framing assumes the problem is slow adoption. Like the goal is already decided and we're just asking how fast to go. But nobody is seriously asking whether the efficiency gains are worth the tradeoffs, or who gets to decide that, or what happens when an algorithm denies someone's prior authorization and there's no doctor who actually made that call. I've read enough about the UnitedHealthcare algorithm lawsuits to know that this is not a hypothetical concern. These systems are already being used to deny care and the accountability just disappears into the software.⏎⏎I want to respond to the part of the RFI that asks about barriers to adoption, because I think the framing there is completely backwards. The assumption is that barriers are bad and should be removed. But some of what gets called a "barrier" is actually a doctor taking time to examine a patient, or a clinician applying judgment that doesn't fit neatly into a training dataset. Those aren't inefficiencies. The reason adoption is slow in some areas might be because practitioners don't trust these tools, and that distrust is sometimes correct.⏎⏎There's also the bias question which I don't think can be brushed aside with promises of future auditing. Dermatology AI tools trained predominantly on lighter skin tones have been documented to perform significantly worse on darker skin. The researchers who published on this in journals like JAMA Dermatology aren't fringe critics, they're showing real gaps in how these products get to market. Accelerating adoption means accelerating the deployment of tools with these known problems into clinical settings that serve patients who are already underserved.⏎⏎What I'd actually want HHS to do is slow down and be honest that we don't have good post-market surveillance for AI tools the way we do for drugs. A medication goes through phases of trials and even then gets recalled sometimes. An AI model gets updated by the vendor without the hospital even necessarily knowing, and there's no equivalent of a recall process. The FDA has been working on this for a while now and it's still not resolved.⏎⏎The workforce piece is something I keep coming back to. If AI handles more of diagnosis, documentation, triage, coding, prior auth - and I know those are different functions but they're all jobs people do right now - then what happens to medical coders, to health information technicians, to the people doing care coordination? The RFI doesn't really address this. It talks about freeing up clinician time as though that's automatically a good thing without asking what the clinicians are freed up to do, or whether the support staff around them just get eliminated in the next round of budget cuts.⏎⏎I don't think AI has no place in medicine. I'm not saying that. Some of the imaging applications seem genuinely useful in settings where there aren't enough radiologists. But "some specific applications in specific contexts with strong oversight" is very different from a government-wide push to accelerate adoption across clinical care generally, and I think HHS should be honest about which one it's actually proposing here.⏎⏎The thing about accountability is the part I can't get past. When my mother was misdiagnosed, there was a physician responsible for that. He was wrong, and we knew who was wrong. If an algorithm had made that call or shaped it significantly, I genuinely don't know how that conversation even happens.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0050♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Campbell♔♔Texas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Emory University Hospital in February 2023 after a sepsis diagnosis. I watched nurses and doctors make dozens of judgment calls every hour — adjusting medications, reading her face, talking to her even when she couldn't respond, arguing with each other about her care plan. That arguing was important, actually. That back-and-forth between the night attending and the pulmonologist on day four is probably why she's alive. No algorithm was doing that.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS wants to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings, I get genuinely worried. Not because I'm anti-technology — I use plenty of it — but because the framing of this whole RFI seems to assume that the main problem with AI in medicine is just getting it deployed faster. The question I keep coming back to is: faster for whom, and toward what end?⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to adoption, and I understand why that's the frame. But I think asking only about barriers to adoption quietly presupposes that adoption is the right goal. What about the question of whether a given AI tool should be deployed at all in a given context? The sepsis prediction tools that have been studied — the one from Epic in particular drew a lot of criticism in a 2021 JAMA Internal Medicine paper — have shown that high alert volumes lead to alert fatigue, meaning clinicians start ignoring the warnings. So you get this situation where the AI is technically running, technically producing outputs, and nobody's actually using them meaningfully because there are too many false positives. That's not a benefit. That's a new kind of harm layered on top of the existing system.⏎⏎I'm also honestly scared about what happens to accountability when AI is in the loop. If a physician uses an AI recommendation and something goes wrong, who is responsible? The physician? The vendor? The hospital that chose to implement it? I don't have a law degree and I'm not going to pretend I know how liability works in these cases, but I know that right now there's no clear answer, and HHS accelerating deployment without that answer seems backwards to me.⏎⏎The workforce piece keeps nagging at me too. If AI handles more of the cognitive work of diagnosis or documentation, what happens to the training pipeline for the next generation of clinicians? You learn to read a chest X-ray by reading thousands of them, including the hard ones, including the ones where you're wrong and someone corrects you. If AI is flagging everything first, residents may never develop the independent judgment they need. I've read some commentary from medical educators raising exactly this concern but it doesn't seem to be getting much attention in the policy conversation.⏎⏎I don't think HHS should halt all research into clinical AI. That would be unrealistic and probably counterproductive in some areas. But accelerating adoption as a goal, as opposed to accelerating rigorous evaluation of AI tools before they reach patients — that distinction matters enormously and I don't see this RFI making it clearly enough.⏎⏎The reimbursement angle is where I get most concerned, honestly. If CMS starts paying more for visits where AI-assisted tools are used, that's going to push hospitals toward adoption regardless of whether the tools are actually working for their patient populations. Smaller safety-net hospitals with different patient demographics than whatever data the model was trained on are going to be especially exposed to that risk.⏎⏎I'm not sure what the right regulatory mechanism is here. But I do think any framework HHS develops needs to require post-market surveillance with mandatory outcome reporting broken down by race, income, primary language, and disability status before any tool gets broadly reimbursed.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0051♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Sanchez♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔Electronic Frontier Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February 2023 with what turned out to be a pulmonary embolism. The AI-assisted triage tool flagged her as low-risk. A nurse disagreed. The nurse was right. My mother is alive because a human being overrode what the algorithm said.⏎⏎I think about that a lot when I read something like this RFI, which is essentially asking how the government can move faster on putting AI into clinical decisions. The premise of the whole document seems to be that speed is good, that more adoption is better, and that the main obstacles are regulatory friction and reimbursement gaps. I don't think that's the right way to look at this at all.⏎⏎What worries me specifically — and I'll try to stay focused because I could go on about this for a long time — is the question of who is accountable when something goes wrong. Right now, a physician can be held responsible for a misdiagnosis. A hospital can face liability. There are imperfect but real mechanisms for accountability. When an AI system makes a recommendation and a clinician follows it and a patient is harmed, that chain gets very murky very fast. The vendors say the doctor should have caught it. The hospital says they were following best practices by using a certified tool. The doctor says the software told them it was fine. I've seen this exact dynamic play out in conversations with people who work in risk management at health systems, and nobody has a clean answer.⏎⏎The RFI asks about actions HHS can take to accelerate adoption and specifically mentions reimbursement as a lever. Paying providers more to use AI tools is not a neutral act. It creates a financial incentive that will push systems toward using these tools even in situations where the clinical evidence for them is thin. There are a lot of AI products on the market right now that have been validated on patient populations that look nothing like the patients who will actually be treated. The validation datasets are often predominantly white, predominantly male, predominantly from academic medical centers. Deploying those tools faster in community hospitals and rural clinics and safety-net settings is not going to help the people those places serve. It might actively hurt them.⏎⏎I'm not categorically opposed to any use of technology in medicine. That would be a silly position. But there's a real difference between a tool that helps a radiologist catch something they might have missed and a system that is making or strongly shaping decisions about whether someone gets admitted, what medications they receive, or whether an insurance claim is approved. The latter category is where I think the risks are seriously underweighted in this RFI.⏎⏎The framing of the whole document treats delay as the danger and acceleration as the solution. I'd ask HHS to sit with the possibility that moving more slowly, requiring more robust independent clinical validation before reimbursement, and investing in accountability infrastructure before deployment — that those things might actually produce better outcomes for patients than getting to adoption faster. The nurse who overrode the algorithm at St. Luke's had thirty years of experience and trusted her gut. There's no timeline for reimbursing that.⏎⏎I do want to flag one more thing that didn't get much attention in the RFI, which is what this does to the health care workforce. If AI systems take over documentation, prior authorization review, preliminary reads on imaging, and other tasks that currently require trained clinicians and support staff, the argument will be that it frees them up for higher-value work. But in practice, in the hospital and clinic environments I'm familiar with, it tends to mean fewer positions.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0052♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Davis♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February 2023. During that time, I watched three different physicians make judgment calls that no algorithm would have made the same way, and two of those calls almost certainly saved her life. One of them involved a resident noticing that she seemed more agitated than her vitals suggested she should be, and pushing for an imaging order that the attending was initially reluctant to approve. That's not a data point. That's a human being reading another human being.⏎⏎I keep coming back to that when I try to think through what HHS is actually proposing here. The framing is "accelerating adoption" and I understand why that sounds appealing administratively, but acceleration implies direction. Where exactly are we going?⏎⏎My specific concern is with the question of accountability when AI is involved in clinical decisions and things go wrong. Not hypothetically wrong, actually wrong. A patient gets the wrong dosage recommendation because the model was trained predominantly on data from younger male patients. A screening gets flagged or not flagged because the algorithm's sensitivity was calibrated on imaging equipment different from what a rural clinic can afford. Who is responsible? The vendor? The hospital that licensed the tool? The physician who relied on the output because they had fourteen patients to see that morning and the system was supposed to help?⏎⏎I don't think HHS has answered this. I've read through what's been published and I genuinely can't find a clear framework for liability when an AI system contributes to patient harm. The RFI asks how to accelerate adoption but I think the more urgent question is what the accountability structure looks like before we get there. You can't build patient trust on a foundation where nobody is clearly responsible.⏎⏎There's also something that troubles me about the workforce piece that I don't think gets enough attention. I'm not a physician myself, I work in hospital administration, and I've watched over the past two years as certain diagnostic support roles have been restructured with the explicit expectation that AI tools would "augment" the workflow. In practice what happened is that two positions were eliminated and the remaining staff were expected to review AI outputs rather than develop their own clinical judgment. The skills atrophy. And then when the AI fails, which it does, the human fallback is weaker than it was before the tool was introduced. That seems like a real and underappreciated risk.⏎⏎I want to be honest that I'm not opposed to any use of AI in clinical settings. That would be a silly position. There are administrative applications that probably do save time without putting patients at risk. Scheduling. Prior authorization documentation, though that's its own disaster for other reasons. But the RFI conflates a lot of different use cases under one umbrella of "adoption" and I think that conflation is doing some work that should be made explicit.⏎⏎The question I'd urge HHS to actually answer before moving further is this: for each category of clinical AI application, what is the minimum evidence threshold for deployment, and who bears legal responsibility when that threshold turns out to have been insufficient? Right now those answers seem to vary enormously by vendor, by state, and by whether anyone thought to ask the question at all in the contracting process.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0053♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Robinson♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati last February. I watched nurses and physicians make dozens of judgment calls every day — when to adjust her oxygen, whether her agitation meant pain or confusion or something neurological, how hard to push her toward eating. None of those decisions came from a protocol. They came from people who knew her, watched her, talked to her family. She came home. I think about that constantly when I read materials like this RFI.⏎⏎What concerns me most about the framing here is the word "accelerate." The entire premise seems to be that AI adoption in clinical settings is good and the question is only how fast and through what mechanisms. That's a conclusion dressed up as a starting point. HHS is asking how to speed up something it hasn't fully justified yet.⏎⏎I'm not opposed to technology in medicine on principle. I understand there are administrative tasks that are genuinely burdensome and probably don't require a physician's attention. Fine. But the RFI lumps together things like billing automation and clinical decision support as though they're in the same category of risk, and they're not. When an algorithm flags a prior authorization wrong, someone gets delayed care. When a diagnostic AI misses something or confidently suggests the wrong path, someone can die. The difference matters and I don't see enough acknowledgment of it here.⏎⏎There's also a responsibility problem that I keep coming back to and I'm not sure the RFI takes seriously. When a physician makes a wrong call, there is a person accountable for that. When an AI system contributes to a bad outcome, accountability gets diffused across the vendor, the hospital that bought it, the regulatory body that cleared it, the physician who technically "retained oversight." That's a lot of people to point fingers at each other while a patient's family tries to figure out what happened. And we've already seen this play out — there was reporting in 2023 around several health systems using algorithmic tools for sepsis prediction that were generating so many false positives that nurses were starting to ignore the alerts. That's not a hypothetical risk. That's a documented failure mode.⏎⏎Regarding reimbursement specifically, which I think is question 4 or 5 in the RFI — I genuinely worry that creating financial incentives for AI use will push hospitals to adopt tools before those tools are ready, because the incentive exists and the pressure to cut costs is already enormous. Hospitals in rural areas especially are under financial strain and if there's a reimbursement pathway tied to AI adoption, administrators will adopt whether or not clinicians think it's appropriate. That's not a hypothetical either. It's how healthcare purchasing decisions get made.⏎⏎I work as a medical social worker in a community health setting, not as a physician, so I don't have a technical opinion on algorithm validation methods or whatever specific regulatory standard applies here. But I do see patients every day who already feel like they're being processed rather than cared for. The electronic health record system my clinic uses already creates situations where providers are staring at a screen for half the visit. I'm not sure adding more automated decision support is going to help patients feel heard.⏎⏎The thing I keep wanting to ask is: who asked patients whether they want this? Not in a survey designed to get a particular answer, but genuinely — are the people receiving care part of this conversation in a real way? The RFI mentions patient trust but the action items all seem to be aimed at providers, payers, and developers.⏎⏎And honestly the workforce question is getting almost no attention. If AI reduces the need for certain types of clinical judgment, what happens to the people trained to exercise that judgment? Not just physicians but nurses, technicians, medical coders, the entire infrastructure of human expertise that took decades to build. Once you deskill a profession it's very hard to rebuild that capacity if the technology fails or♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0054♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthony♔King♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Mercy General in Sacramento in February 2023 with what turned out to be a pulmonary embolism. The AI triage tool the hospital was using flagged her as low-risk and suggested she be discharged. A nurse — an actual human being who had been doing this for nineteen years — looked at my mother, looked at the numbers, and said something was wrong. She pushed back. They kept her. She's alive.⏎⏎I think about that a lot when I read things like this RFI.⏎⏎What HHS is asking is basically: how do we speed this up? And I understand the impulse. Clinician burnout is real, administrative waste is real, rural access gaps are real. I'm not pretending those problems don't exist. But the framing of "accelerating adoption" treats skepticism as an obstacle to be overcome rather than a signal worth listening to.⏎⏎The question I'd want HHS to actually grapple with is the accountability one. When an AI system recommends a treatment or flags someone as low-risk or denies a prior authorization, and that recommendation turns out to be wrong, who answers for it? The hospital? The vendor? The physician who clicked "accept"? Right now there's no clear answer to that and I don't think there will be just because we write a new reimbursement code. Liability diffuses. Responsibility gets passed around until nobody holds it.⏎⏎There's also something that bothers me about the insurance side of this, though HHS's RFI is focused on clinical care specifically. UnitedHealth's use of an algorithm called nH Predict — which a 2023 lawsuit alleged was denying post-acute care claims at something like a 90% rate — is the kind of thing that happens when you let efficiency metrics drive decisions that should involve a person looking at a person. That's not clinical care exactly but the same logic applies. Speed and scale are not inherently good things when the inputs are biased and the outputs affect whether someone gets treatment.⏎⏎What I'd actually want HHS to consider is a moratorium on new reimbursement incentives for AI clinical tools until there's a real post-market surveillance system in place. Not a voluntary reporting thing. Mandatory. With teeth. The FDA's framework for software as a medical device has moved slowly for a reason and I'm not sure the answer is to accelerate around it.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the workforce piece because it keeps getting ignored in these conversations. If AI handles documentation, triage flagging, diagnostic support, and patient communication, what exactly is the role of a medical assistant or an LPN in ten years? The RFI doesn't ask about that. It should. The people most likely to be displaced are not physicians, they're the people making $38,000 a year who actually spend the most time with patients.⏎⏎The nurse who saved my mother's life was not following a protocol. She was paying attention in a way that I genuinely do not believe a system trained on historical data can replicate, at least not now, maybe not ever.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0055♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michael♔Martinez♔♔Maine♔♔United States♔Commonwealth Fund♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was admitted to Mercy General in Sacramento in February of 2023 with what turned out to be a pulmonary embolism. The algorithm the hospital was using flagged her as low-risk for clotting based on her age, weight, and recent bloodwork. Her nurse disagreed. Her nurse had been doing this for nineteen years and something about the way my mother was breathing and the color in her face made her push back, hard, on the discharge recommendation. My mother is alive because of that nurse's stubbornness.⏎⏎I keep thinking about what happens when we build systems that make that kind of stubbornness harder to justify. When the algorithm has a confidence score and the nurse has a feeling. Who wins that argument in a hospital that is watching its liability exposure and trying to move patients through faster?⏎⏎That's what bothers me about this RFI. The framing of it is acceleration. How do we go faster. And I understand there are real problems with administrative burden and prior authorization delays and all of that, but the question of whether we should be accelerating AI use in actual clinical decisions — not billing, not scheduling, actual diagnosis and treatment — that question isn't getting asked here. It's already been answered in the framing.⏎⏎What I want HHS to think about, and I'm not sure this maps cleanly onto any specific question in the RFI, is what happens to accountability when AI is in the loop. Not in a theoretical sense. In a practical one. If my mother had been discharged and died, and the decision had been made with significant AI input, who do you sue? Who gets their license reviewed? The hospital will say the physician signed off. The physician will say the system recommended it. The vendor will say the model performed within its stated parameters. I've talked to two medical malpractice attorneys about this scenario, not for my mother's case specifically but just to understand the landscape, and both of them said the accountability structures we have right now are genuinely not built for this. That's not a reason to never use AI in medicine. But it seems like a reason to not accelerate before those structures exist.⏎⏎There's also something I find harder to articulate about what medicine is supposed to be. I know that sounds vague. But my mother's nurse sat with her for twenty minutes and asked her questions that weren't on any checklist. She noticed things. That kind of noticing is shaped by relationship and presence and all the things that are actually hard to quantify. I worry we are building systems that will reward hospitals for replacing that with something cheaper and faster, and that we will not know what we've lost for a long time.⏎⏎The efficiency argument is real. I don't want to pretend it isn't. Prior auth delays kill people too, and if AI can fix that I'm not going to oppose it reflexively. But clinical judgment at the bedside is different from administrative processing, and I'm not confident HHS is drawing that line clearly enough in how it's thinking about this.⏎⏎One more thing that didn't fit anywhere above: there's been very little public discussion of what training data is being used for these models and whether the populations in that data reflect the patients who will actually be affected. The bias literature on diagnostic AI is not reassuring, and I saw a study from 2021 — I believe it was published in NEJM though I'd have to look up the exact citation — showing significant racial disparities in how certain commercial algorithms weighted patient severity. Accelerating deployment before that's resolved seems like it would make those disparities worse, not better.⏎⏎I don't have a clean policy ask here. I'm a person who almost lost her mother because an algorithm said she was fine.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0056♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Moore♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔Urban League♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Mercy General in Sacramento for eleven days in February 2024 with a complicated UTI that had spread to her kidneys. During that stay, I watched nurses and doctors consult screens constantly, running her information through what one resident casually referred to as "the sepsis prediction tool." Nobody explained to us what it was, what it recommended, or whether any decisions were being made based on it. She recovered fine, thankfully, but I left that hospital with a feeling I still can't shake — that nobody in that building could fully tell me who was actually making the calls about her care.⏎⏎That's the thing I want HHS to sit with before pushing any further on AI adoption. Not the abstract question of whether AI can be accurate, but the concrete question of who is responsible when something goes wrong. Because right now, from where I stand as a family member of a patient, that question has no good answer.⏎⏎I keep thinking about the liability side of this. If an AI tool flags someone as low risk and they're sent home and then they die — who answers for that? The hospital? The vendor who sold the algorithm? The doctor who was technically supervising but had 30 other patients? I've read a little about how these contracts often include indemnification clauses that protect the software companies pretty heavily, which means the accountability ends up diffuse in a way that helps nobody except the people selling the product.⏎⏎And then there's the bias problem. I know this has been studied. The ProPublica reporting on the COMPAS algorithm in criminal justice showed how these prediction tools can systematically disadvantage Black patients or defendants without anyone explicitly programming that in — it just emerges from the training data reflecting historical inequities. The same thing can happen and probably is already happening with clinical AI trained on decades of records from a healthcare system that undertreated certain populations. I don't think accelerating adoption fixes that problem. More hospitals using a biased tool faster is not progress.⏎⏎What bothers me is that HHS framing this as "accelerating adoption" suggests the department has already decided the answer is yes and is now just working out the details. The RFI doesn't really ask whether AI should be integrated more deeply into clinical care — it asks how to do it. That framing concerns me more than any specific technical question.⏎⏎I'm genuinely uncertain about some of this. I can see that administrative burden on clinicians is real and that some AI tools for scheduling or billing or prior authorization might be lower-stakes applications. But the comment solicitation seems to lump all of that together with clinical decision support, which is a very different category of risk. A tool that helps a biller code a claim correctly and a tool that influences whether a patient gets admitted to the ICU are not the same thing and should not be evaluated or approved under the same framework.⏎⏎The pace of this also worries me. The FDA has cleared something like 700+ AI-enabled medical devices as of mid-2023, and there's been very little post-market surveillance data published on how they actually perform in real-world hospital settings versus the controlled conditions they were validated on. Distribution shift is a known problem in machine learning — a model trained on data from one patient population can degrade significantly when deployed at a different hospital serving a different demographic. I don't see how encouraging faster adoption addresses that gap rather than widening it.⏎⏎My ask, if I can call it that, is for HHS to at minimum separate out the question of clinical AI from administrative AI in any subsequent guidance, and to require meaningful informed consent processes so that patients like my mother know when algorithmic tools are influencing their care. Right now most patients have no idea, and I'm not sure most clinicians fully understand the tools either based on what I observed.⏎⏎The resident who mentioned the sepsis tool to me did so almost offhandedly, like it was just part of the furniture of the hospital. He didn't know who made it or when it had last been validated on a population like my mother's — an 81-year-old woman with a history of autoimmune♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0057♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Walker♔♔New Hampshire♔♔United States♔Intelerad Medical Systems♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was discharged from Riverside University Health System in Moreno Valley in February 2023 after a five-day admission for heart failure. Before she left, a nurse mentioned, almost in passing, that her readmission risk score had been flagged as "low" by some algorithm the hospital was using to allocate follow-up calls. She was readmitted within nine days. Nobody called her in between.⏎⏎I don't know which system they were using. I've tried to find out and gotten nowhere. That's part of the problem I want to talk about.⏎⏎The RFI asks about actions HHS can take to accelerate adoption. I want to push back on the word accelerate because I think it's doing a lot of work in this document. Speed is being treated as obviously good here, and I'm not sure why. The question of whether clinical AI is ready for broader deployment is separate from whether hospitals and insurers want to deploy it, and those two things keep getting collapsed together in policy discussions like this one.⏎⏎What worries me most is the accountability gap. When my mother got that low-risk score, who was responsible for it? The nurse who glanced at the screen and moved on? The hospitalist who maybe never saw it? The vendor who sold the tool? The hospital that bought it without apparently doing much to validate it for their specific patient population? I genuinely don't know, and I suspect if I tried to pursue it formally I would find that nobody knows, either. The liability just kind of evaporates.⏎⏎There's research on this. The study Obermeyer et al. published in Science in 2019 showed that a widely used commercial algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health status. That paper came out six years ago and the tools just kept getting sold. Accelerating adoption means accelerating that kind of thing too, unless there's real pre-deployment validation with demographic breakdowns that actually mean something, and I haven't seen HHS propose anything that would require that at a level with teeth.⏎⏎I think the honest version of this RFI would acknowledge that we don't really know how many patients have been harmed by clinical AI tools already, because the reporting infrastructure doesn't exist to capture it. My mother's situation almost certainly doesn't appear in any dataset anywhere as an AI-related adverse event. It's just a readmission statistic.⏎⏎I'm not saying never use AI in hospitals. That would be its own kind of extreme. There are probably narrow, well-validated applications where it helps. But the framing of this RFI assumes that the main obstacle to AI adoption is regulatory friction, and I think that framing is wrong. The obstacle should be uncertainty about safety. Those are not the same thing and treating them the same way leads somewhere I don't want to go.⏎⏎One specific thing I'd ask HHS to consider: before expanding reimbursement for AI-assisted clinical tools, require that any tool used in care settings publish its validation data broken down by race, age, sex, and insurance status, and that this data be updated annually and made publicly available, not just submitted to a review board internally. Not as a checkbox. As a condition.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the workforce piece, though I recognize I'm running long. The efficiency gains that get cited in support of AI adoption often translate, in practice, to fewer nurses, fewer case managers, fewer people whose job is to call a patient after discharge and ask how they're doing. My mother needed a person. The algorithm said she didn't.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0058♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Walker♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Joseph's Medical Center in Stockton, California in February of 2023, and during that time I watched three different hospitalists make judgment calls that no algorithm would have made the same way. One of them, on day six, decided to hold a scheduled medication because something about how she was breathing "seemed off" to him. He was right. Her potassium had dropped to a level that would have made the medication dangerous. He caught it because he was paying attention to her, not to a dashboard.⏎⏎I keep thinking about that when I read about what HHS is trying to do here.⏎⏎The RFI asks what regulatory actions could accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, and I think that's the wrong question entirely. Acceleration implies the direction is already settled and we just need to go faster. But a lot of clinicians and patients haven't agreed that this is the right direction at all, and the fact that HHS is framing this as a question of speed rather than a question of whether feels like the decision has already been made somewhere and the public comment is just a formality.⏎⏎The part of this that worries me most, honestly, isn't the flashy diagnostic AI. It's the administrative and insurance-related uses. When a tool flags a patient's claim for denial, or determines that a hospital stay is "medically unnecessary," who is accountable for that decision? The company that built the model will say it's the insurer. The insurer will say it's the tool. The doctor will say their hands were tied. The patient ends up with no one to hold responsible and no recourse that's actually accessible to them. I've seen this with my own family and it is not theoretical.⏎⏎There's also the bias problem, which I don't think HHS is taking seriously enough based on what I've read in this RFI. Training data reflects historical patterns of care, and historical patterns of care in this country have been shaped by decades of unequal treatment. A model trained on that data doesn't correct for bias, it encodes it and then executes it at scale and with an air of objectivity that makes it harder to challenge. A doctor who makes a biased decision can be confronted. A model that makes a biased decision gets called "evidence-based."⏎⏎I'm not someone who thinks technology is inherently bad. I use electronic health records, I understand why they exist, I've seen the benefits of things like remote monitoring for people in rural areas. This isn't a reflexive anti-technology position. But there's a difference between tools that assist a clinician and tools that replace the judgment of a clinician, and I think HHS is not being clear-eyed about where that line is or about how financial incentives push health systems toward the latter even when the clinical evidence only supports the former.⏎⏎The RFI mentions reimbursement as a lever for accelerating adoption. That's what concerns me most about the direction of this whole effort. If you build reimbursement structures that reward AI-assisted care without first establishing that the AI is actually producing better outcomes, you've just created a financial incentive for hospitals to deploy tools that may not work and that carry real risks, and you've made it very hard to walk that back once the infrastructure is in place and the contracts are signed.⏎⏎I think HHS should instead be asking what the minimum evidentiary standard should be before any AI tool is used in a clinical decision that affects patient care or insurance coverage, and who is responsible when that tool causes harm.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0059♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sarah♔Hill♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was in the hospital for six weeks in the fall of 2022, at Mercy Medical Center in Baltimore. Congestive heart failure, then a secondary infection, then questions about whether she had an underlying autoimmune issue that nobody could quite pin down. What got her through it, honestly, was Dr. Ramos. Not the monitoring equipment, not the electronic records system that kept crashing, not any algorithm. It was the fact that he would come in at odd hours and actually talk to her, and that he noticed when something was off before the numbers showed it. I keep thinking about that when I read about this push to bring AI into clinical decision-making.⏎⏎I'm not anti-technology. I want to say that because I know how this sounds. But there's a difference between using software to manage scheduling or flag a drug interaction, which fine, and using it to make or meaningfully shape decisions about whether someone gets a treatment, or how serious their symptoms are assessed to be. That second category scares me.⏎⏎The part of this RFI I want to focus on is the question about what regulatory actions HHS could take to speed adoption. My answer is: the question itself is backwards. The burden of proof should be on demonstrating that these tools are safe and unbiased before they get into clinical settings, not on figuring out how to remove the barriers to getting them in faster. "Accelerating adoption" treats speed as self-evidently good. It isn't.⏎⏎I've read enough about the studies on diagnostic algorithms, including the well-documented issues with pulse oximetry and how its inaccuracies in darker-skinned patients weren't caught for decades, to know that these systems carry assumptions baked in from their training data. AI trained on health records from predominantly white, male, or insured populations is going to perform differently for patients who don't fit that profile. And the thing that worries me is that when a doctor makes an error, there's at least a mechanism, however imperfect, for accountability. When an algorithm makes an error, the hospital says the vendor is responsible, the vendor says the algorithm was used outside its intended parameters, and the patient or their family is left with nothing. I don't think we have remotely adequate frameworks for liability right now.⏎⏎There's also the workforce question, which I feel like isn't being taken seriously enough in this conversation. If AI takes over the cognitive tasks that justify the time physicians and nurses spend with patients, we're not going to end up with doctors who have more time to care. We're going to end up with fewer doctors, because that's how these efficiency arguments always play out in practice. Hospitals are not going to say great, now our cardiologists can spend an extra hour with each patient. They're going to say great, now we need fewer cardiologists.⏎⏎I recognize that administrative burden in healthcare is genuinely bad and that physicians are burning out partly because of documentation requirements. I'm not dismissing that. But the solution to overworked clinicians isn't to hand their judgment to a system that can't be held responsible and that patients can't meaningfully question or appeal. The solution is staffing, reimbursement reform, fixing the insurance prior-authorization nightmare, things that require political will rather than a tech deployment.⏎⏎I don't know exactly what the right regulatory ask is here, I'm not a lawyer or a policy expert. But at minimum I would want to see requirements for prospective, not just retrospective, bias audits on any AI system used in direct clinical decisions, with results that are publicly available and broken down by race, sex, age, and insurance status. And I'd want clear, mandatory disclosure to patients when AI has played a role in their care, not buried in a consent form somewhere on page 11.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0060♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Mitchell♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔Eli Lilly and Company♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent 11 days in the cardiac unit at Mercy General in Des Moines in February 2023, and what I keep thinking about when I read materials like this RFI is how many times a nurse or physician caught something that no monitoring system flagged. Not because the systems were broken. They were working fine. A nurse noticed my mother seemed more confused than her vitals suggested she should be, and that observation led to them catching a medication interaction that the automated dosing software had not flagged as a concern. That is not an argument against technology in hospitals. I use technology, I understand it has a place. But there is something about the way HHS is framing this whole question that bothers me.⏎⏎The RFI asks how to accelerate adoption. The whole premise assumes acceleration is the right direction. I'd push back on that framing pretty hard.⏎⏎When I look at what's actually being proposed here, the thing that concerns me most is the insurance and prior authorization side of this. There have already been cases, the ProPublica reporting on UnitedHealth's nH Predict algorithm comes to mind, where AI tools were used to systematically deny post-acute care to elderly patients at rates that the company's own internal data apparently showed were inconsistent with physician recommendations. That lawsuit was filed in 2023. The patients whose care was denied were real people. Some of them died while the appeals were pending. I don't know all the legal details and I'm not a lawyer, but it seems like before HHS moves to make AI a bigger part of coverage determinations, someone should be asking hard questions about what accountability looks like when an algorithm is wrong and a person is harmed.⏎⏎The workforce piece is something I feel less confident about, honestly. I do think some administrative burden on clinicians is genuinely awful and if AI can help with documentation I don't want to be reflexively against that. But I'm worried that efficiency gains on the administrative side become justification for reducing staff, and then you've traded one problem for a worse one down the road. Hospitals have already been cutting nursing staff in ways that have real consequences for patients and I don't see why adding AI tools would reverse that pressure rather than intensify it.⏎⏎What I'd really want HHS to focus on, if this process is going to be meaningful at all, is some kind of requirement that AI systems used in clinical decisions have to be explainable to the patient they affect. Not just auditable by the hospital or the vendor. Explainable to the actual person. My mother deserved to know if an algorithm was influencing her care. She's 74 and not particularly technical but she's a person with rights and she should be told when a computer is making recommendations about her body.⏎⏎I don't know if that's something HHS can require through this process or whether it would need to go through a different regulatory channel. The transparency rules that already exist for EHR systems seem like they might be a starting point but I genuinely don't know how they'd apply here or whether they cover AI decision support tools the same way they cover records access.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0061♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sarah♔Green♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔National Association of Social Workers♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Lehigh Valley Hospital in Allentown in October of 2022, and I can tell you that what kept her alive wasn't an algorithm. It was a pulmonologist named Dr. Reyes who sat with our family at 2 in the morning and walked us through a decision that no decision-support tool could have made for us. That's what I keep thinking about when I read through this RFI.⏎⏎What worries me most isn't that AI is useless. I'm not saying that. I'm saying that there's a difference between a tool that helps a physician and a system that starts to replace the physician's judgment in ways that are hard to detect and harder to contest. The push to "accelerate adoption" — that word accelerate is doing a lot of work here — seems to assume that the main barrier is regulatory friction, when honestly the barriers might be there for good reason.⏎⏎Nobody seems to want to talk about what happens when these systems are wrong. A 2023 study out of Stanford found that several commercially deployed clinical AI tools performed significantly worse on Black patients than white patients, and the performance gap wasn't disclosed in the product marketing. Hospitals bought these tools, deployed them, and clinicians were using outputs they didn't know were biased. I don't see how moving faster fixes that problem. It seems like it makes it worse.⏎⏎The reimbursement angle is where I get most frustrated. If CMS starts paying more for AI-assisted visits or creates billing codes that reward AI integration, you've basically created a financial incentive for hospitals to adopt these tools regardless of whether they work well. Health systems are already under enormous financial pressure. Give them a reimbursement carrot and they will deploy whatever earns that reimbursement. The patients don't get a vote.⏎⏎And then there's the question of who's responsible when something goes wrong. If a physician follows an AI recommendation and the patient is harmed, does the liability fall on the doctor, the hospital, the software vendor? Right now this is genuinely unsettled legally. Accelerating adoption before those questions are answered means real patients will be harmed during a period when there's essentially no clear accountability structure in place. I find that troubling in a way that I'm not sure I can fully articulate, but it feels like HHS is treating the health system as a testing ground.⏎⏎I don't want to come across as someone who thinks medicine should never change. It should change. It has to. But there's a difference between thoughtful integration and what this RFI is proposing, which reads more like a push to remove barriers and get product into hospitals faster. The framing of existing regulations as obstacles to be cleared is concerning. Some of those regulations exist because we've watched what happens when medical devices and health IT get deployed without adequate evidence of safety.⏎⏎My specific ask would be this: before HHS identifies any new regulatory flexibilities or reimbursement structures to speed up AI adoption, the department should first require mandatory post-deployment outcome reporting for any AI clinical decision tool already in use. We don't even know what's happening right now with the tools that are already deployed. Mount Sinai, Mass General, Mayo — these systems are already running AI tools in clinical settings and there is no standardized reporting requirement for adverse events that are potentially attributable to AI recommendations. That seems like the obvious place to start, not with acceleration, but with basic surveillance.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0062♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Ramirez♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in March of 2023, and what kept her alive was not an algorithm. It was a night-shift nurse named Darlene who noticed that her breathing had changed in a way that the monitors were not flagging, who pushed back on the attending physician's initial read, who sat with us at 2am and explained what was happening in words we could actually understand. I think about that a lot when I read documents like this one.⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to AI adoption and how HHS can help clear them. That framing worries me. The question presupposes that faster adoption is the goal and that whatever is slowing it down is a problem to be solved. But what if some of those barriers are load-bearing? What if the friction in clinical decision-making is doing something important, like making sure a human being is actually responsible for what happens to a patient?⏎⏎I work in hospice care, so I see medicine at its most human and its most irreducible. There is no efficiency metric for sitting with someone who is dying and their family, for reading the room, for knowing when to speak and when to be quiet. I am genuinely not opposed to technology in healthcare in general. Electronic records have made coordination better in real ways, I have seen that firsthand. But there is something different about AI systems that generate clinical recommendations or that are used to make prior authorization decisions, which a lot of insurers are already doing with tools like Evicore even before HHS formally accelerates anything. Those systems have already produced documented harm. A 2023 ProPublica investigation found that UnitedHealth Group's algorithm was denying post-acute care claims at a rate that its own internal data suggested was far exceeding what clinical review would have approved. People were sent home before they were ready. Some of them ended up back in the hospital.⏎⏎The accountability question is one I keep getting stuck on and I don't think the RFI engages with it seriously enough. When an AI system contributes to a bad outcome, who is responsible? The vendor? The hospital that deployed it? The physician who technically signed off? In my experience the answer tends to be nobody, or at least nobody in a way that actually changes anything for the patient or their family. We already have problems with diffuse responsibility in healthcare. Building AI deeper into clinical workflows without first establishing clear liability frameworks seems like it would make that significantly worse.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the workforce piece because I think it gets treated too abstractly. HHS talks about AI reducing administrative burden for clinicians and I understand the appeal of that framing. Documentation burden is real. Burnout is real. But a lot of the people who work in healthcare support roles, medical coders, prior auth coordinators, health information technicians, are not highly paid and are not going to transition easily into whatever the AI-adjacent jobs are supposed to be. The rulemaking process should be asking hard questions about who absorbs the cost of that disruption.⏎⏎I recognize there are genuine potential benefits here and I am not trying to pretend otherwise. But accelerating adoption before the safety, accountability, and equity frameworks are in place is not moving forward, it's just moving fast. And in medicine, moving fast without looking is how people get hurt.⏎⏎The specific question I want HHS to sit with is this: what is the minimum viable evidence threshold that should be required before an AI clinical decision support tool can be used in a way that affects patient care without a mandatory independent human review of its recommendation? Right now I do not believe there is a clear answer to that question in federal policy, and I think that gap should be closed before anything else gets accelerated.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0063♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Adams♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔National Association of Chain Drug Stores♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was discharged from Mercy General in Cincinnati in March 2023 after a six-day stay for a cardiac event, and before she even got home, an automated system had flagged her for "low readmission risk" and denied the home health follow-up her cardiologist ordered. Nobody could tell us which system made that call or why. The cardiologist's office said it wasn't them. The insurance company said it was a "clinical decision support tool." We spent four days on the phone. She was back in the hospital eleven days later.⏎⏎That experience is what brought me here, to this comment, though I want to be honest that my concerns go beyond just what happened to my mother.⏎⏎The RFI asks about regulatory actions HHS can take to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. I want to address specifically the question about removing barriers to AI use in coverage and utilization management decisions, because I think the framing of that question gets things exactly backwards. The question assumes the barriers are the problem. I think the barriers are doing important work, and the answer is not to remove them but to understand why they exist.⏎⏎When an algorithm denies care, there is no one accountable. That's not a technical problem that better AI will fix. It's a structural one. Physicians can be sued, licensed, disciplined, and asked to testify. They carry malpractice insurance. They can be deposed. An algorithm cannot. A vendor can disclaim liability. A hospital can say it was just following the tool's recommendation. This diffusion of responsibility is not some theoretical risk, it happened to my family and it happens constantly, and I have read enough accounts from nurses and hospitalists to know we are not unusual.⏎⏎I also want to say something about efficiency, because efficiency is the main argument for moving faster here. I understand the appeal. Physician burnout is real, administrative burden is crushing, and I don't think people who want AI tools in medicine are acting in bad faith. But I keep thinking about what gets lost when the interaction between a patient and a provider gets intermediated by a system neither of them fully understands. My mother's cardiologist is a good doctor. He knew she needed follow-up. The algorithm overruled him based on variables he couldn't see, using weights nobody disclosed. He spent more time fighting the denial than he would have spent on the follow-up visit itself. Where is the efficiency in that.⏎⏎HHS should not be accelerating this. The right move is a moratorium on AI in coverage determination until there is mandatory, meaningful explainability for every denial, a clear legal liability framework that attaches to the system's output and not just the human who theoretically "supervised" it, and independent auditing requirements with real teeth, not voluntary self-reporting. I realize that is a harder path than the one this RFI seems to be pointing toward.⏎⏎I also just want to register that I find the whole framing of medicine as a sector that needs to "accelerate adoption" of any technology troubling. Medicine moves carefully on purpose. The FDA doesn't accelerate drug approvals because speed is good in the abstract. There are reasons clinical processes are slow and conservative and I worry this RFI treats that conservatism as an obstacle rather than a feature.⏎⏎The specific question about what regulatory barriers HHS should remove — I would urge the Department to instead ask what protections are currently absent, because that list is much longer and more urgent. At a minimum, patients should have a right to know when AI was involved in a clinical or coverage decision affecting them, and that right should exist in statute, not just as a suggestion in agency guidance that♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0064♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Taylor♔♔West Virginia♔♔United States♔American Diabetes Association♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in February of 2023, and I watched nurses and doctors make dozens of judgment calls that no algorithm could have made. Not because the technology isn't sophisticated enough yet, or because we need better training data. Because those decisions were woven into relationships — a nurse who had been with her since day two and noticed something was off in how she was holding her arm, a hospitalist who knew from a five-minute conversation that my mother would refuse a certain intervention on religious grounds and worked around it without making a production of it. That's what I keep thinking about when I read about HHS wanting to "accelerate" AI adoption in clinical care.⏎⏎I understand the efficiency argument. I do. Burnout is real, rural access gaps are real, and the paperwork burden on physicians is genuinely obscene. But the question being asked in this RFI — what regulatory and reimbursement actions can HHS take to speed this up — feels like it's starting from the wrong place. Faster isn't the right goal. Better might be. Safer definitely should be.⏎⏎What worries me most is the insurance side of this, though I realize that's slightly different from the clinical care focus here. There are already documented cases of AI systems being used to deny claims — Cigna's algorithms were reported in 2023 to have processed tens of thousands of claim reviews in a matter of seconds, with a physician spending an average of 1.2 seconds per case. That's not medicine. That's automation wearing a stethoscope. And I don't see anything in this RFI that makes me confident the department is treating that as a cautionary tale rather than a template.⏎⏎On the clinical side specifically: who is accountable when an AI-assisted diagnostic tool misses something or points the clinician in the wrong direction? I've read that these systems perform unevenly across patient populations — they were often trained on data that skews white, male, and relatively affluent — and errors in those cases fall hardest on patients who are already underserved. Speeding up adoption without resolving that is going to hurt people. I'm not saying don't ever use these tools, but there's a difference between piloting something carefully in specific, well-monitored contexts and treating acceleration itself as a policy objective.⏎⏎The RFI asks about research and development priorities. My honest answer is that before investing in expanding these systems, HHS should be funding serious independent auditing of the tools already in use. Not audits conducted by the vendors. Not self-certification. Third-party review with findings made public, so patients and clinicians can actually see what they're dealing with.⏎⏎I'm a retired social worker, not a physician or a technologist, so I'm probably not the audience HHS imagined for this comment. But I sat in that hospital for eleven days and I know what good care looks like when I see it, and it doesn't look like a dashboard.⏎⏎One practical thing I'll add: whatever comes out of this process, patients should be told when AI was involved in their care or in any coverage decision affecting them. Right now there's no consistent requirement for that disclosure and most people have no idea it's happening.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0065♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Moore♔♔Tennessee♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February 2023 with what turned out to be a pulmonary embolism. The algorithm flagging her risk level kept showing "low" for the first eighteen hours. The nurses were suspicious and pushed for imaging anyway. They were right. She's alive because two nurses overrode what the system was telling the attending physician.⏎⏎So when HHS asks how it can "accelerate AI adoption in clinical care," I genuinely don't understand what problem you think you're solving. Speed? Efficiency? Those words don't mean much to me anymore after watching my mother's face when she finally understood how close it had been.⏎⏎What concerns me most, and this is the question in the RFI about barriers to adoption — I think you're framing it backwards. The barriers aren't the problem. Some of those barriers are the point. Physician skepticism, liability uncertainty, lack of interoperability between systems — these aren't bugs in the adoption process, they're the immune response of a system that knows something could go wrong. Flattening those barriers without understanding why they exist seems like exactly the wrong priority.⏎⏎I keep thinking about who absorbs the risk when AI gets it wrong. Right now if a doctor misses something, there's a person, a license, a chain of accountability. It's imperfect, obviously, but it exists. When an algorithm makes the same mistake, the responsibility diffuses immediately. The vendor says the hospital configured it wrong. The hospital says the vendor's training data was flawed. The insurer says the physician should have caught it. My mother would have been a closed case while three corporate legal teams figured out whose fault nobody's fault was.⏎⏎There's also something I can't quite articulate but feel strongly about, which is the texture of what a clinical encounter is supposed to be. A doctor looking at you, thinking about you specifically, being wrong sometimes and right more often — that relationship is not just a mechanism for producing accurate diagnoses. It's something else. I don't have the policy language for what I mean but I think HHS should at least be asking whether accelerating AI changes that relationship in ways that can't be measured in outcome metrics.⏎⏎The workforce piece matters too and I don't think it gets taken seriously enough. If AI handles documentation, triage flags, prior authorization, imaging reads — what does that do to the pipeline of people learning those skills? A radiologist learns to read a film by reading thousands of films, including the edge cases, including the misses. If AI is reading 95% of films, the radiologist who's supposed to catch the AI's errors has a lot less practice catching anything. This seems like a compounding problem that won't show up in the data for years.⏎⏎I want to be fair — I'm not saying there's nothing useful here. I can see that administrative burden is crushing and if AI can handle prior auth paperwork that would genuinely help. But the RFI isn't really asking about that narrow use. It's asking how to accelerate adoption broadly, across clinical care, which is a much bigger thing than fixing paperwork.⏎⏎My recommendation, if it means anything, is that HHS commission independent failure analysis of AI tools already deployed before expanding the footprint. Not vendor-funded studies. Independent review of actual adverse events where AI was in the decision loop. I'd want to know how many cases like my mother's there have been where the algorithm was wrong and a human caught it, and how many where nobody did.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0066♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Sanchez♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was discharged from Mercy General in Sacramento in February 2023 after a three-day stay for what turned out to be a pulmonary embolism. The attending who finally caught it — Dr. Reyes, I think was his name — caught it because he sat with her for forty minutes asking questions that didn't seem connected to anything, questions about her travel schedule and her ankles and whether she'd been sitting more than usual. He told us later that her initial bloodwork looked "basically fine" and that the imaging flags were ambiguous. A machine looking at those numbers would have sent her home.⏎⏎I keep coming back to that when I read about what HHS is trying to do here.⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to AI adoption and how to remove them. I want to answer that question honestly, which means saying that some of those barriers are there for good reason. The friction in medicine — the requirement that a physician actually examine a patient, actually talk to them, actually take responsibility for a decision — is not a bug. That friction is what caught my mother's clot. Removing it in the name of efficiency seems like exactly the wrong framing.⏎⏎And I do understand the argument on the other side. I'm not pretending burnout isn't real or that rural access isn't a genuine crisis. Those things are real. But the solution being proposed feels like it was designed to solve a financial problem and is being dressed up as a patient-safety solution. If the underlying concern is that physicians don't have enough time with patients, the answer to that is not to hand the diagnostic process to software trained on datasets that we mostly can't inspect. The answer is to have more physicians with more time. I know that sounds naive. I don't care.⏎⏎What specifically worries me about accelerating adoption — and this is the part I hope someone at HHS actually reads — is the accountability question. When an AI system recommends against a treatment and a patient is harmed, who is liable? The hospital that bought the software? The vendor that sold it? The physician who deferred to the recommendation because their malpractice insurer told them the algorithm was validated? I've read through some of the literature on this and the honest answer seems to be that nobody quite knows yet, and the people proposing to accelerate adoption don't seem particularly interested in resolving it first. They want deployment, and they'll figure out accountability later.⏎⏎That is not a reasonable sequence of events when the thing being deployed is being used to make decisions about whether someone gets a medication or a surgery.⏎⏎There's also the insurance context, which the RFI mentions only briefly. The use of AI in prior authorization is already happening and it is already causing harm. Cigna's review of 1.2 million claims in roughly a month and a half — that story from 2023 — is the logical endpoint of what "accelerating AI adoption" means in practice. That's not clinical care. That's cost containment with a technical vocabulary attached to it.⏎⏎I don't have a specific regulatory fix to propose. I'm a retired school administrator, not a health policy lawyer, so I'll leave the specific rule citations to people who know what they're talking about. What I can say is that the presumption embedded in this RFI — that speed of adoption is the goal and the barriers are the problem — seems like it gets things exactly backwards. The question shouldn't be how do we get AI into clinical settings faster. It should be whether we've established, to a standard that would satisfy a reasonable patient, that it belongs there at all.⏎⏎My mother is fine, by the way. But she almost wasn't, and the thing that saved her was a physician who had the time and the inclination to think carefully and the professional authority to act on his judgment without running it through a scoring system first.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0067♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Thomas♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February of 2023. I sat with her every day. What got her through it was not an algorithm — it was a pulmonologist named Dr. Reyes who noticed something was off with her breathing pattern at 6am on a Tuesday, something that didn't show up clean on any chart, and made a call that the numbers didn't obviously support. She's alive. I think about that constantly when I read about HHS wanting to speed up AI in clinical care.⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to AI adoption and how to remove them. I want to push back on the framing of that question entirely. The assumption baked into it is that slower adoption is a problem to be solved. What if slower adoption is actually the right pace? What if the friction is doing something useful?⏎⏎I work in healthcare administration, not clinical care, so I want to be honest about where my expertise stops. But I've watched our hospital system roll out two AI-assisted tools in the last three years and in both cases the clinical staff were told the tools were decision support, not decision making, while in practice the pressure to follow the AI's output was enormous because going against it required documentation and justification that took time nobody had. So the distinction between "support" and "replacement" collapses pretty fast on the ground.⏎⏎The question about reimbursement is where I really get concerned. If HHS creates payment incentives tied to AI adoption, you will get AI adoption, full stop, regardless of whether the specific tools being adopted are safe or appropriate for particular patient populations. We already know that many of these models perform worse for Black patients, for elderly patients, for patients with multiple comorbidities — these are not theoretical concerns, there's published research on this going back to at least 2019 with the Obermeyer study in Science that showed a widely used algorithm was systematically underestimating illness severity in Black patients. Pushing adoption faster does not make that problem go away. It scales it.⏎⏎What I haven't seen addressed anywhere in the framing of this RFI is the question of what happens when something goes wrong and nobody is sure who's responsible. The physician who followed the AI recommendation? The hospital that licensed the tool? The vendor who trained the model on data that didn't represent the patient population? These questions aren't resolved. We don't have a liability framework that works for this. Accelerating adoption before those questions are answered seems backwards to me.⏎⏎I understand there are real inefficiencies in healthcare and I'm not pretending AI has nothing to offer. Prior authorization processes are genuinely broken and I can see why people want automated tools there. But even that is more complicated than it looks because those same tools can be used by insurers to deny care faster and with more cover than before.⏎⏎I guess what I want HHS to actually hear is that speed is not a neutral goal. Somebody benefits from faster adoption, and it's worth asking who. The patients I've seen navigating serious illness are not asking for faster. They're asking for someone who knows them to be in the room.⏎⏎Dr. Reyes didn't have an AI flagging that Tuesday morning. I don't know what would have happened if she'd been looking at a dashboard instead of my mother's face.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0068♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Walker♔♔Missouri♔♔United States♔American Health Information Management Association♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was discharged from Mercy General in Sacramento in February 2023 after a three-week inpatient stay for sepsis, and the discharge summary she received had been partially generated by an AI documentation tool the hospital had quietly rolled out. Nobody told her. Nobody told me. We found out because one of the nurses mentioned it offhand when I asked why the summary described her as having a "history of alcohol use disorder" — which she does not have, has never had. When I raised it, the attending physician said the flag had probably been pulled from a prior patient's record that got mixed into her chart through the AI intake system. He seemed almost as confused as I was.⏎⏎I keep thinking about what would have happened if she had been transferred to a skilled nursing facility with that flag attached. Or if an AI-assisted insurance review had used it to deny coverage for something.⏎⏎HHS is asking how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. I think that's the wrong question entirely. What I want to know is who was responsible for what happened to my mother's record. Was it the hospital? The vendor? The attending? Nobody could give me a clear answer, and that's not a technical problem you solve by deploying more AI faster. That's a fundamental accountability problem that gets worse the more these systems are embedded.⏎⏎The efficiency argument for AI in medicine always sounds plausible until you ask what happens when it goes wrong. Physicians are already overloaded, I understand that, and I'm not pretending that the status quo is fine. But the answer to physician burnout is not to hand off clinical judgment to systems that nobody can fully explain and that don't carry any liability when they misidentify a patient's history or flag someone incorrectly for an insurance denial. There's a company called Cigna that made news in 2023 for using an algorithm to deny claims at a rate of roughly 1.2 seconds per case. The physicians supposedly reviewing those denials weren't really reviewing them in any meaningful sense. Accelerating that kind of thing is not an improvement in clinical care. It's just automating the worst tendencies that already exist.⏎⏎I also want to push back on something implicit in how this RFI is framed, which is that the barriers to AI adoption are mostly regulatory friction and reimbursement gaps. Some of those barriers exist because clinicians and patients don't trust these tools yet, and that distrust is not irrational. A 2022 study in JAMA showed that several commercial AI diagnostic tools performed significantly worse on Black patients than white patients, often because of training data that didn't reflect the population being served. I don't know that HHS has a good answer to that yet, and it worries me that the push to accelerate adoption seems to assume these bias problems will get resolved along the way rather than needing to be resolved first.⏎⏎Medicine is not primarily an information processing problem. I know that's almost a cliche to say, but I mean it in a specific way. Diagnosis requires contextual judgment about a particular person, and that judgment depends on the kind of ongoing relationship and direct observation that AI cannot substitute for. When my mother's sepsis was finally caught, it was because a nurse noticed she was slightly more confused than she had been at morning rounds, not because any monitoring system flagged it. That nurse had talked to her enough to know what her baseline was.⏎⏎I'm not asking HHS to ban AI from healthcare. I don't think that's realistic and I'm genuinely uncertain about where exactly the lines should be. But accelerating adoption before there are clear liability frameworks, before bias testing requirements are mandatory rather than voluntary, and before patients have real informed consent rights about when these tools are being used on them — that sequence seems backward to me.⏎⏎At minimum, I think any reimbursement incentive for AI use should be conditioned on documented informed consent from the patient, not just general notice buried in an admission form that nobody reads.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0069♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Campbell♔♔South Dakota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was discharged from Mercy General in Sacramento in February 2023 after a four-day stay for a cardiac event, and the attending cardiologist spent maybe eleven minutes with her total across those four days. The rest of the time she was being "monitored" by systems that flagged her oxygen readings twice incorrectly and nearly caused a medication adjustment she didn't need. So when HHS talks about accelerating AI adoption in clinical care, I want to ask: accelerating toward what, exactly?⏎⏎The question in this RFI about removing barriers to AI adoption in care settings is the one I keep coming back to. And I understand there are genuine inefficiencies in healthcare, I'm not naive about that. Physicians drowning in paperwork, prior auth delays that cost lives, diagnostic gaps in rural areas. I get it. But the framing of "barriers" as the problem bothers me because some of those barriers are doing real work. They slow things down on purpose. They force a human being to be accountable for a decision.⏎⏎What concerns me is that when an AI system makes a wrong call, nobody is quite sure who to hold responsible. The vendor says the clinician should have overridden it. The clinician says the system flagged it as low risk. The hospital says they followed the approved protocol. My mother is still sitting there with the wrong medication order. That diffusion of accountability isn't hypothetical, it's already happening, and this RFI seems to want to pour gasoline on that situation by making adoption faster and easier, with fewer checkpoints.⏎⏎There's also the bias issue, which I know people raise all the time and sometimes it gets dismissed as generic concern-trolling, but the pulse oximetry problem is documented extensively at this point. Dark-skinned patients were systematically misread for years and clinical AI trained on those readings carries that error forward. The New England Journal published on this in 2020. We know this. So when someone proposes expanding AI-assisted monitoring across more patient populations faster, I genuinely don't understand how that's considered acceptable before the underlying data problems are resolved.⏎⏎I also want to say something about what medicine actually is, or should be, because I think HHS is approaching this almost entirely as an efficiency problem. My mother's cardiologist, the eleven minutes aside, knew her. Knew she'd had anxiety about procedures since a bad experience in 2018. Made a decision about sedation based on that. No model trained on population data is going to surface that. The relationship carries information that doesn't fit in a structured field.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no place anywhere in healthcare. Radiology screening tools, administrative scheduling, maybe certain narrow diagnostic support functions where the evidence base is actually solid. But "accelerating adoption" as a general posture, without specifying what's been proven safe and what hasn't, seems backwards. The burden of proof should be on demonstrating safety before deployment, not after.⏎⏎There are hospitals right now piloting ambient AI transcription tools that record everything said in an exam room, and patients often don't know it's happening, or they're told in a brief disclosure buried in the intake paperwork they're signing while trying to manage a sick child or their own pain. I don't know what the legal status of that is under existing consent frameworks and I suspect HHS doesn't have a clean answer either.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0070♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Torres♔♔Kansas♔♔United States♔Muscular Dystrophy Association♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in February of 2023, and I watched every single one of those days what it actually means to practice medicine. Her attending physician, Dr. Okonkwo, would come in at odd hours, sometimes 6am, sometimes 10 at night, and he would sit with her chart but he would also sit with her. He'd ask her how she felt about the treatment options. He noticed when she was scared versus when she was in pain, which are different things, completely different things, and he adjusted accordingly. No algorithm running on a server somewhere in Virginia is going to do that.⏎⏎I know that's not the question HHS is asking. You're asking about barriers to adoption, how to speed things up. But I think the framing itself is the problem and I want to push back on that before getting into anything else.⏎⏎The question of whether AI should be accelerated in clinical settings is not the same as the question of whether AI can be technically deployed there. Plenty of things can be deployed. What bothers me is the assumption baked into this entire RFI that faster adoption is inherently good and the only task is clearing obstacles. What if the obstacles exist for reasons?⏎⏎Take diagnostic AI. There's been a lot of coverage of tools like the ones used in radiology, where an algorithm flags potential findings on a scan. I've read enough to know that some of these tools perform well in aggregate on benchmark datasets, but then when you look at their error rates broken down by patient demographics the picture gets murkier. A study published in JAMA in 2021 found meaningful disparities in AI diagnostic accuracy across racial groups for at least one major application. I don't remember the exact figures but the pattern was clear enough that it stuck with me. My concern is that if HHS pushes rapid adoption before we have solid post-market surveillance requirements in place, hospitals and clinics are going to deploy these things and nobody is going to be tracking whose care suffers disproportionately. The burden of those errors will not be distributed evenly. It never is.⏎⏎And who is accountable when something goes wrong? This is the thing I can't get a straight answer on from anything I've read about AI in medicine. If a physician uses an AI tool that recommends a course of treatment and the patient is harmed, the liability question gets murky fast. Physicians may start to defer to the algorithm in ways that erode their own judgment, partly because of liability fears working in the opposite direction too. I've heard doctors describe this already. The technology changes the incentive structure in ways that HHS doesn't seem to be grappling with in this RFI.⏎⏎I'm not entirely against AI in medicine. I want to be honest about that. There are probably administrative uses, scheduling, prior authorization paperwork, that are lower stakes and could save time without putting patients at risk in the same way. But even prior authorization is not purely administrative, it determines whether someone gets care or not, and when insurance companies start running those decisions through AI the consequences are real. Cigna's use of automated claim denials became a major news story for exactly this reason.⏎⏎The workforce question also doesn't get enough attention. If AI handles more and more diagnostic and documentation work, what happens to the pipeline of people who develop those skills? Radiology residents in 2024 are training in an environment where the AI does a first read on most scans at many programs. What does that do to their development over a five or seven year residency?⏎⏎I keep coming back to the idea that medicine's value is not separable from the human relationship at its center. That's not a sentimental point, it has measurable effects on outcomes. Patient trust affects medication adherence, affects willingness to report symptoms, affects whether someone comes back for follow-up. If patients don't know whether they're being evaluated by a physician or a system, or if the answer is some opaque combination of both, that trust erodes in ways that are hard to quantify but very real.⏎⏎HHS should be asking how to ensure safety and accountability before asking how to speed up adoption. The sequencing matters. Right now this RFI has the order reversed, and I don't think that's a minor procedural detail.⏎⏎I'd also want to know more about what input actual frontline clinicians had in developing this RFI, not just health system administrators and technology vendors, who have obvious financial interests in rapid deployment. The comment period itself is only open until♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0071♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Robinson♔♔Kentucky♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Advocate Christ Medical Center in Oak Lawn, Illinois in February of 2023. During that time, I watched a rotating cast of physicians, nurses, respiratory therapists, and social workers try to coordinate her care under impossible conditions. What saved her — and I mean this literally — was a pulmonologist who noticed something in how she was breathing that the monitors weren't catching. She told me afterward that she almost didn't catch it herself because she was so tired. That human near-miss, caught by a human, is what I keep thinking about when I read proposals like this one.⏎⏎I am deeply opposed to the direction HHS is pushing here. Not because I think technology is inherently bad, but because the framing of this RFI treats "acceleration" as obviously good, and I don't think that's obvious at all. The question isn't whether AI can do some things faster. The question is what we lose when we build systems that make clinicians dependent on outputs they can't fully audit or understand.⏎⏎There's a specific part of the RFI that asks about barriers to AI adoption in clinical settings. I want to push back on the premise of that question. Framing caution as a "barrier" assumes that faster adoption is the goal and that resistance is something to overcome. But some of those barriers are there for reasons. Physicians who are skeptical of AI-generated treatment reccomendations are not being obstructionist — they're doing exactly what we trained them to do, which is to question.⏎⏎What concerns me maybe more than anything is the accountability problem. When an AI system recommends a course of treatment and that treatment causes harm, who is responsible? The hospital that licensed the software? The vendor? The clinician who followed the recommendation without understanding how it was generated? I have not seen a satisfactory answer to this anywhere, and this RFI doesn't seem particularly interested in asking the question. It talks about removing friction, streamlining adoption, identifying regulatory barriers. It does not spend nearly as much time asking what happens when these systems fail, and they will fail. Every system fails sometimes.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the workforce question, even though I know it's not the main focus of the RFI. If AI takes over documentation, triage support, diagnostic flagging, and increasingly clinical decision-making, what does that do to the pipeline of clinical judgment? You don't develop good instincts by watching an algorithm make decisions. You develop them by making decisions yourself, under supervision, over years. A medical education system that increasingly defers to AI outputs is going to produce clinicians who are less capable of functioning when the AI is wrong or unavailable. I don't know exactly how to quantify that risk but it seems serious to me.⏎⏎There's also the bias issue which I know gets mentioned a lot but I don't think it's been taken seriously enough in practice. These models are trained on historical data and historical data reflects historical inequities in diagnosis, treatment, and access. The FDA cleared over 500 AI-enabled devices as of late 2022, and I'd genuinely like to know how many of those went through any kind of systematic bias auditing before deployment in majority-Black or majority-Hispanic patient populations. My guess is not many.⏎⏎I am not saying stop all research. I'm not even saying AI has no role in medicine. I'm saying that HHS should not be in the business of accelerating adoption without first building out the accountability and audit infrastructure that would let us know whether these tools are actually helping patients or just helping hospital systems process more patients per hour, which is a very different thing.⏎⏎The pulmonologist who saved my mother was working a fourteen-hour shift. What she needed was fewer patients on her roster, not an AI tool to help her manage more of them.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0072♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Jackson♔♔Texas♔♔United States♔Connected Health Initiative♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Mercy General in Sacramento in February 2023 with what turned out to be a pulmonary embolism. The algorithm flagged her as low-risk for DVT on intake. The nurse caught it anyway because she noticed my mother's breathing pattern and the way she was sitting. That's not something you can put in a dataset.⏎⏎I keep coming back to that when I read through what HHS is proposing here. The framing of this whole RFI bothers me more than any specific question in it. The word "accelerating" is doing a lot of work. Acceleration implies we've already determined the direction is correct and we just need to go faster. I don't think we've determined that, not even close.⏎⏎There's a question in the RFI about what regulatory barriers are slowing AI adoption in clinical settings. I want to push back on the premise. Some of what gets called a "barrier" is actually oversight. Requiring validation of a diagnostic tool before it's used on patients isn't red tape, it's just medicine. The history of medical devices is full of things that worked great in controlled trials and then hurt people at scale, and AI systems have additional problems on top of that because they can degrade silently, because the population you validate on isn't always the population you deploy on, and because when something goes wrong it's genuinely unclear who is responsible. The doctor? The hospital? The vendor? The company that trained the model on data that turned out to have racial skew baked into it?⏎⏎That last part really concerns me and I don't think the RFI takes it seriously enough. Studies going back to 2019 have shown that some clinical algorithms perform measurably worse on Black patients than white patients, sometimes by quite a lot. If you accelerate deployment before that's solved you're accelerating a disparity, not treating one.⏎⏎I should say I'm not completely opposed to AI in medicine in every form. Administrative burden is genuinely awful and if AI can help with prior authorization paperwork or scheduling I don't really object to that. But that's a long way from diagnostic decision support or, worse, using AI outputs to make coverage determinations. Insurance companies are already doing this, and there have been lawsuits over it, and it is not going well for patients.⏎⏎What I'd want HHS to actually do is slow down, require independent auditing of any AI tool used in clinical or coverage decisions before deployment, create a real mechanism for patients to appeal decisions that were influenced by an algorithm, and be honest that "accelerating adoption" is a choice with tradeoffs rather than an obvious good. I'd also want to see the agency ask harder questions about workforce. If AI is replacing the judgment of nurses and doctors, where does that judgment go? You can't just assume it sits there waiting to catch the algorithm's mistakes. If people aren't exercising judgment because the system tells them not to, they get worse at it over time.⏎⏎The nurse who caught my mother's embolism had 22 years of experience. I don't know how you train a model on that.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0073♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Taylor♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔Michigan State University College of Human Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was discharged from Mercy General in Sacramento in February 2023 after a five-day stay for pneumonia, and before she even got home, she had received three automated messages telling her she was at "low risk" for readmission. She was back in the emergency room within 48 hours. Her physician later told us, quietly, that the risk-stratification tool the hospital had recently adopted had flagged her as low-risk partly because she had a listed caregiver — me — and partly because her oxygen numbers had technically improved. What the tool didn't know, couldn't know, was that she lives alone, that I work full time two hours away, and that her "improvement" was relative to a baseline that was already pretty bad for a 78-year-old with COPD. A human nurse who had spent twenty minutes with her would have caught that. The algorithm didn't.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS wants to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, I have to be honest — my first reaction is that the people writing this request haven't had a family member discharged by a system that's already optimized more for throughput than for actual care. I'm not against technology across the board. I use plenty of it. But there's something different about what's being proposed here, which seems to treat AI as a solution to a problem that is at least partly about resources and staffing, and I'm not sure swapping in more automation fixes that.⏎⏎The RFI asks what regulatory actions HHS can take to accelerate adoption, but I'd push back on the framing. The question I'd want answered first is: what evidence do we have that current AI tools in clinical settings are producing better outcomes, and for which patients? Because from what I can find — and I've read a few things on this since my mother's situation — the tools being deployed now are often validated on datasets that don't reflect elderly patients, patients with multiple chronic conditions, patients who are poor or who don't have reliable internet or transportation. The performance statistics that get used to justify these tools often don't break down by those subgroups. That's not a minor technical detail. That's the difference between a tool that works and one that gives you false confidence.⏎⏎I'd also ask HHS to think carefully about what happens to accountability when something goes wrong. My mother's situation worked out okay in the end, though I wouldn't say "okay" is the right word for an 78-year-old with two hospitalizations in a week. But when I asked the hospital who was responsible for the readmission risk assessment, the answer was basically that the physician signed off and the tool informed the decision. Which means there's nobody. The physician didn't design the algorithm. The vendor isn't liable in any way that matters. And the hospital can point to the physician's signature. That diffusion of responsibility isn't theoretical, it already exists, and accelerating adoption without addressing it first seems like exactly the wrong order of operations.⏎⏎I don't think HHS should halt all research or all use of these tools. But "accelerating adoption" as a goal, stated that plainly, with so many open questions about safety and equity still unresolved, seems premature. The momentum here feels like it's coming from vendors and from administrators who see cost savings, not from clinicians or from patients, and I'd want the comment record to at least reflect that not everyone is on board with moving fast.⏎⏎One specific thing I'd ask: before HHS takes any action to expand reimbursement incentives for AI-assisted clinical decision tools, there should be a mandatory requirement that outcomes data be collected and published by patient demographic subgroup, including age, race, income level, and rurality, with independent auditing. Not self-reported by the vendor.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0074♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Lee♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔Inovalon♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Advocate Good Samaritan in Downers Grove twice in 2023, and both times what mattered was whether the nurse actually looked at her, whether the doctor sat down and listened. Not the portal, not the algorithm flagging her medication interactions, not any of that. The thing that got her through was a hospitalist named Dr. Reyes who spent forty minutes with her on a Tuesday afternoon when he clearly had other places to be. No AI does that. I don't know how else to say it.⏎⏎What bothers me about this whole RFI is the framing. HHS is asking how to accelerate adoption. The word "accelerate" is doing a lot of work there. It assumes the direction is correct and the only question is speed. But I think the direction itself deserves scrutiny, and I'm not sure the department is asking that question seriously.⏎⏎I work in social work, so I'm not a clinician, but I've spent twenty years watching what happens when efficiency logic gets applied to care settings. You get systems that look good on paper and fail people in practice. The bias problem with AI in clinical settings is not a hypothetical. There's documented evidence going back to at least 2019, the Obermeyer study in Science, that commercial algorithms systematically underestimated the severity of illness in Black patients because the training data used cost as a proxy for need. And the proposed response from HHS seems to be: let's move faster and figure it out later. That's backwards.⏎⏎The accountability question also keeps me up at night, honestly. When an AI system recommends a course of treatment and something goes wrong, who is responsible? The vendor? The hospital that deployed it? The physician who followed the recommendation? I've seen what happens in social services when you diffuse responsibility across a system -- nobody ends up holding the bag, and the patient or client is the one who suffers. I don't see anything in this RFI that seriously grapples with that.⏎⏎There's also the workforce piece. I understand that physician burnout is real and administrative burden is crushing, and I'm not dismissing that. But the solution being proposed is essentially to automate the human out of more and more decisions. I'm skeptical that this helps in the long run. What it probably does is deskill the workforce over time, create dependency on systems that can fail catastrophically, and shift power to the vendors who own the models.⏎⏎I want HHS to slow down. Not stop forever, necessarily, but actually do the safety and equity validation work before pushing adoption. Require independent audits of any AI tool used in clinical decisions before it can be deployed, not after. Make the training data and model logic available for outside review. And please don't let reimbursement policy be the lever that drives adoption -- if you pay for AI-assisted care at higher rates, you're going to get AI-assisted care whether it's appropriate or not, because that's how incentives work.⏎⏎I realize I'm probably not telling HHS anything it doesn't already know. But I keep thinking about the fact that this RFI closes on a Friday and most of the formal comments are going to come from vendors and health systems with a financial stake in expansion. Someone should say that the patient in the hospital bed doesn't care about throughput optimization. My mother wanted someone to look her in the eye and tell her what was happening. I'm not sure what role there is for a language model in that moment, and I haven't seen a good answer to that question anywhere in♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0075♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Jackson♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was discharged from St. Luke's in Cedar Rapids in February of this year after a three-day stay for a cardiac event. During her discharge, a nurse mentioned almost in passing that a "clinical decision support tool" had flagged her as low-risk for readmission and that her follow-up was scheduled accordingly. She was readmitted six days later. I don't know what algorithm was involved or who made the final call, and that's actually the whole problem right in one story.⏎⏎What bothers me about this RFI is that the framing is already decided. You're asking how to accelerate AI adoption, not whether to. The questions are structured around removing barriers, streamlining reimbursement pathways, building confidence among clinicians. That's a policy conclusion dressed up as an open inquiry. I understand why agencies do this but I think it's worth naming.⏎⏎The question I actually want to address is the one about what regulatory actions HHS can take to support responsible AI use in clinical settings. My answer is: slow down and require proof that these tools work before you pay for them or pressure hospitals to use them. That sounds obvious but apparently it needs to be said.⏎⏎There's a real accountability gap that I don't see being addressed anywhere in this document. When an algorithm contributes to a bad outcome, who is responsible? The hospital that purchased it? The vendor who trained it on data that may or may not have reflected patients like my mother, a 74-year-old woman with limited English? The clinician who technically had override authority but works a 12-hour shift and is seeing 30 patients? In practice what happens is the responsibility gets diffused until it belongs to nobody. That's not a hypothetical concern, there's already been substantial reporting on sepsis prediction tools and deterioration algorithms that have performed very differently across demographic groups, and in some cases hospitals have deployed them anyway because the vendor relationship was already in place.⏎⏎I'm also skeptical of the efficiency argument. The pitch is always that AI reduces administrative burden and frees up clinicians to spend more time with patients. But what I've seen, and what a lot of nurses I've spoken with describe, is that these tools add a new layer of tasks. You have to review the alert. Document your decision to override or accept it. Explain to the patient why the computer said one thing and you're doing another. That's not obviously less work.⏎⏎I recognize there may be specific narrow applications where AI assistance is genuinely useful and well-validated. Radiology image review is the example people always give, and I don't have a strong objection there if the evidence is solid. But the scope of what's being contemplated here goes well beyond that. Prior authorization decisions. Discharge planning. Mental health triage. These are areas where the stakes are high and the margin for error is very personal to the patient.⏎⏎The reimbursement question is particularly worrying to me. If CMS creates payment incentives for AI-assisted care before there's a robust evidence standard in place, you create pressure on providers to adopt tools regardless of whether those tools have been validated in their specific patient population. A community hospital in rural Iowa is not Johns Hopkins. The training data for most of these models skews toward large academic medical centers and that mismatch matters.⏎⏎I don't think the answer is to never use AI in healthcare. But the default posture of this RFI, which is essentially "what's in the way and how do we move it," seems like the wrong posture when we're still in a period where we genuinely don't have good longitudinal data on outcomes, where liability frameworks haven't caught up, and where patients often have no idea these tools are being used in their care at all. My mother didn't know. I only found out because I was there and I asked.⏎⏎The consent and disclosure piece alone deserves its own rulemaking before we talk about acceleration.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0076♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Edward♔Robinson♔♔Pennsylvania♔♔United States♔NAACP♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Regions Hospital in St. Paul in February 2023, and I watched what it actually looks like when a physician is paying attention to a human being in front of them. Not a dashboard. Not a risk score. An actual person whose oxygen numbers were doing one thing while her face was doing something completely different, and the nurse caught that discrepancy because she was looking at my mother, not at an alert that had or hadn't fired. I keep thinking about that when I read about HHS wanting to "accelerate" AI adoption in clinical care. Accelerate toward what, exactly.⏎⏎The question I want to address is essentially the one about what regulatory actions HHS should take to promote AI use, and my answer is that the department should be far more focused on what guardrails need to exist before any of this moves faster, rather than on how to speed things up. I understand there are efficiency arguments. I'm not dismissing them entirely. But the framing of this whole RFI treats acceleration as obviously good and caution as the thing that needs justification, and I think that's backwards.⏎⏎There's a pattern with these systems where the errors they make are different from human errors in ways that are hard to catch. A physician who is tired might miss something, but their errors tend to be somewhat random and recoverable. An algorithmic system that has been trained on biased data makes the same error, consistently, on the same population, at scale, potentially for years before anyone notices. The sepsis prediction tool that Epic deployed and that was studied by researchers at the University of Michigan showed false alert rates that were genuinely alarming in real-world conditions, not in the vendor's testing environment. That's not a small thing. Clinicians who get flooded with false positives start ignoring alerts, and then the real ones get missed too. This is documented. It's not hypothetical.⏎⏎What I haven't seen addressed adequately anywhere in how HHS is framing this is the question of who is accountable when an AI-assisted decision leads to patient harm. The physician who followed the recommendation? The hospital that purchased the system? The vendor? In practice right now the answer seems to be nobody in particular, or rather, it gets litigated expensively and inconclusively. Speeding up adoption without first resolving that liability question seems genuinely reckless to me.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the workforce piece, though I'm not sure this is the right venue. When hospitals talk about AI reducing "administrative burden" what they often mean in practice is that they plan to reduce staff. The people who do prior authorization processing, the people who do clinical documentation support, a lot of those are jobs held by workers who don't have a lot of other options. The efficiency gains get captured by health systems and insurers, and the costs get distributed onto communities. I don't think HHS has seriously grappled with that.⏎⏎The thing that bothers me most, honestly, is that medicine involves a person being vulnerable in front of another person and trusting that the other person is actually present with them. That dynamic is part of what makes treatment work. It affects patient honesty, it affects adherence, it affects outcomes in ways that don't show up cleanly in whatever metrics an AI system is being optimized against. I'm not sure how you regulate for that but I wish somebody at HHS was asking the question instead of just looking at how to remove friction from♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0077♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Williams♔♔Delaware♔♔United States♔Stanford University School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Sacramento in February of 2023, and during that time I watched her care team make decisions that no algorithm could have made. Her attending physician noticed something in the way she was breathing — not a data point, just the way her chest moved — and changed course on her treatment. She survived. I don't know if she would have if the clinical judgment had been routed through a system optimizing for discharge timelines.⏎⏎That's what keeps coming back to me when I read about HHS wanting to accelerate AI adoption. The word accelerate is doing a lot of work in this document. It assumes the direction is correct and the only question is speed. I don't think that's right.⏎⏎What bothers me most is the framing around efficiency. Every time AI gets introduced into clinical settings, the conversation eventually circles back to reducing administrative burden, speeding up throughput, cutting costs. Those are not inherently bad goals. But they become bad goals when the thing being made more efficient is the relationship between a sick person and the person trying to help them. I've seen what happens when that relationship gets squeezed. Fifteen-minute appointments. Checkboxes substituting for conversation. Physicians staring at screens instead of patients. AI doesn't fix that problem, it accelerates it, and there's that word again.⏎⏎There's also the question of who gets harmed when these systems fail. The research on diagnostic AI has shown over and over that training datasets underrepresent Black patients, elderly patients, patients whose conditions present atypically. A study published in JAMA in 2019 found that a widely used commercial algorithm was significantly less likely to refer Black patients for additional care compared to equally sick white patients, not because of malicious intent but because of how the training data was constructed. If HHS moves fast on AI adoption, those biases get baked in at scale. And then when something goes wrong, who is accountable? The physician who relied on the tool? The vendor who sold it? HHS for endorsing it? My experience is that accountability in healthcare already diffuses in ways that hurt patients, and adding AI into the chain makes that worse, not better.⏎⏎I want to be honest that I'm not opposed to technology in medicine. I understand that AI tools might genuinely help with things like imaging analysis or medication reconciliation in specific, well-studied contexts. My concern is with the pressure this RFI creates — the pressure on hospitals, on insurers, on clinicians — to adopt broadly and quickly in order to not fall behind, to access reimbursement incentives, to satisfy accreditation requirements. That pressure doesn't wait for evidence. It moves faster than the evidence.⏎⏎The RFI asks about actions HHS can take to accelerate adoption. I would ask HHS to consider instead what actions it can take to ensure that adoption, wherever it happens, is slow enough to be safe. Require independent pre-deployment audits. Require that any AI system used in clinical decision-making be tested on populations that reflect the patient population it will actually serve. Require genuine informed consent for patients whose care is being influenced by algorithmic tools — not buried in a hospital intake form but real disclosure.⏎⏎And honestly, require some mechanism for patients to opt out entirely. That feels like a baseline that should have been established before any of this got this far, and I'm not sure why it wasn't included in the questions being asked here.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0078♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sarah♔Sanchez♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati back in February of 2023, and what got her through it was not an algorithm. It was a night-shift nurse named Darnell who noticed something was off about how she was breathing at 2am and called the attending before the monitors flagged anything. I think about that a lot when I read documents like this one.⏎⏎What bothers me about this whole RFI is the framing. "Accelerating adoption" is treated as obviously good, like the question is just how fast and through what mechanism, not whether the speed itself is the problem. The risks get a paragraph or two and then we're back to talking about opportunity. I'm not against technology in medicine, that's not what I'm saying. But there's a real difference between a tool that helps a physician do their job and a system that starts making or shaping decisions that used to require someone to be accountable for them.⏎⏎The accountability piece is where I keep getting stuck. When an AI flags someone as low-priority for a procedure, or when a clinical decision support tool nudges a doctor toward one treatment and away from another, who is responsible if that's wrong? The vendor says the doctor should have overridden it. The doctor says the system was telling them one thing and there wasn't time to second-guess it. The hospital says they implemented it according to the manufacturer's guidelines. The patient just gets a bad outcome and a bill. I don't think HHS has answered this at all, and accelerating adoption before the answer exists seems backwards.⏎⏎There's also the bias question, which I know comes up in every comment like this, but I want to say something specific about it anyway. A lot of these models are trained on data from academic medical centers, which skews heavily toward certain populations. A patient in rural Appalachia or on a reservation in the Dakotas does not look like the training set. The model doesn't know that. The doctor using the model may not know that either, especially if the output comes formatted like a confident recommendation rather than a probability with confidence intervals and a note about which populations the training data covered. That information almost never gets surfaced at the point of care.⏎⏎I work in health policy, not as a clinician, so I don't have the clinical expertise to say whether a specific tool is safe or not. But I've read enough post-market surveillance literature to know that many of these products go through FDA clearance via the 510(k) pathway, which means they're compared to a predicate device, not tested for clinical outcomes in the specific setting where they'll be used. Deploying them at scale before we have real-world evidence from diverse populations isn't acceleration, it's a gamble with other people's health.⏎⏎HHS is asking what regulatory or reimbursement changes would help. My answer is that before we talk about incentives for adoption, we need mandatory post-deployment monitoring requirements with public reporting, and we need clear liability rules that don't let everyone in the chain point at everyone else. Neither of those things are in this RFI in any serious way. Reimbursement reform as an adoption accelerant, without those guardrails already in place, is just going to move faster toward problems we haven't figured out how to fix yet.⏎⏎I'm genuinely uncertain whether there's a version of this that works well. Maybe there is. But I notice that the people most enthusiastic about the speed tend to be the people selling the systems or advising the companies that do, and the people most worried about the speed tend to be the nurses and the patients, and I think that asymmetry deserves more weight than this RFI gives it.⏎⏎The comment period for this closes October 4th and I hope HHS actually reads the ones that push back, not just the ones from the vendors with full-time regulatory affairs staff.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0079♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Williams♔♔Tennessee♔♔United States♔Aidoc♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Mercy General in Sacramento for six days in February 2024 after a fall. During that stay, I watched nurses spend more time correcting what the documentation AI was auto-populating than they would have spent just charting it themselves. One of them told me, quietly, that the system kept flagging my mother as a fall risk due to age and medication profile, which she already was, obviously, but that the flag was being used to justify less frequent in-person checks because the monitoring system was supposed to compensate. She fell again on day four. Not severely, but she fell.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI caused that. I genuinely don't know. But that's sort of the problem I want to raise here, which is that when something goes wrong in these environments, nobody can quite say what happened or who is responsible. The nurse? The system? The hospital for deploying it? The vendor? This diffusion of accountability is something HHS doesn't seem to be grappling with seriously enough in this RFI, at least not from what I can read.⏎⏎The framing of this whole request bothers me. "Accelerating adoption" is the goal stated upfront, and then the questions follow from that assumption. But acceleration toward what, exactly. The efficiency gains that get cited in these conversations are usually measured in billing time or documentation minutes, not in patient outcomes. I've looked at some of the literature on ambient AI scribes, for instance, and the studies showing time savings are almost all funded by the companies making the products. The independent replication is thin.⏎⏎What I'd actually want HHS to answer before pushing further down this road: what is the liability framework when an AI-assisted clinical decision contributes to patient harm. Right now there isn't a clear one. Physicians can be sued for malpractice, but if they're relying on a tool that was certified and recommended by the very agency now asking how to deploy it faster, that muddies everything. I'm not a lawyer so I can't speak to the exact statutes, but I know enough to know this hasn't been resolved.⏎⏎There's also the workforce question that keeps getting glossed over. The argument is that AI handles administrative burden and frees clinicians for patient care. Maybe. But health systems don't hire to that freed capacity, they reduce staff. We've already seen this in radiology, where AI-assisted reading is being used to justify larger caseloads per radiologist rather than maintaining staffing and improving quality. The math on "efficiency" always seems to land somewhere that benefits the institution and not the patient or the worker.⏎⏎I'm not opposed to technology in medicine categorically. That would be a strange position. But there's a difference between a diagnostic tool that a physician uses and interrogates and something that operates as a background layer shaping decisions before the physician is even in the room. The RFI doesn't really distinguish between these things in a meaningful way, and I think that distinction matters enormously for how you regulate it.⏎⏎The question of patient consent also gets very little attention here. My mother did not know, and was not told, that an AI system was involved in monitoring her care or flagging her risk profile. I only found out because I asked a nurse directly. Whether that's currently required to be disclosed I honestly don't know, but it should be.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0080♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Campbell♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔American Gastroenterological Association♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Mercy General in Sacramento in February 2023 with what turned out to be a pulmonary embolism. The algorithm her care team was using flagged her as low-risk for clotting. The nurses were skeptical. They pushed back. She survived because a human being with twenty years of experience looked at her and said something is wrong here, regardless of what the screen said.⏎⏎That's where I'm starting from when I read this RFI.⏎⏎The question HHS seems to be asking is essentially: what can we do to speed this up? And I understand the pressure behind that question. Clinician burnout is real, administrative burden is real, and I'm not someone who thinks technology is always bad. But the framing of "accelerating adoption" worries me deeply because it assumes the direction is already settled and we're just working out logistics. I don't think the direction is settled.⏎⏎What bothers me specifically is the reimbursement piece. When you tie payment incentives to AI adoption, you're not giving clinicians a choice in any meaningful sense. A small rural practice that can't afford to push back against a payer decision is going to implement whatever tool gets them reimbursed. That's not informed adoption. That's financial coercion dressed up as innovation policy. And then when something goes wrong — and things will go wrong, they already are — who is accountable? The vendor will point to the physician. The physician will point to the algorithm. The patient or their family will be left trying to navigate a liability structure that nobody has honestly sorted out yet.⏎⏎There's also the bias problem, which I think gets mentioned in these conversations and then kind of set aside. Training data reflects historical clinical practice. Historical clinical practice has well-documented racial and gender disparities. A system trained on that data will, in the absence of very careful correction, reproduce those disparities at scale and reproduce them faster and more invisibly than a human clinician would. At least when a human makes a biased decision, there's a human to confront. With an algorithm, the response tends to be "we'll look into improving the model in a future version."⏎⏎I don't think HHS should halt all research into clinical AI. I'm genuinely not saying that. But this RFI is asking about acceleration, and the honest answer to that question, given where we actually are, is: not yet, or at least not without a set of conditions that don't currently exist. Independent safety evaluation before deployment, not after. Clear liability rules. Opt-out rights for patients. Workforce protections so that "efficiency gains" don't just become a cover for eliminating nursing positions.⏎⏎The thing about my mother's situation is that the efficiency argument would probably still come out fine even if the nurses hadn't caught it. The algorithm would have a good overall accuracy rate. The one case where it failed would be a statistical outlier. That's how these evaluations work. But that outlier was my mother.⏎⏎I'm not sure HHS is fully reckoning with the fact that for every percentage point of "accuracy," there are real people in the tail of that distribution, and those people don't get averaged out.⏎⏎Anyway, I'd ask that the Department at minimum address the liability question before moving forward with any reimbursement incentives tied to AI tool adoption, because right now that piece is genuinely♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0081♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patricia♔Thompson♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔National Association of the Deaf♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent three days in a hospital in Tucson in February 2023 after a fall. During that time, I watched nurses spend what felt like half their shifts staring at screens, clicking through documentation fields, trying to satisfy whatever the system needed from them before they could move on. One of them apologized to my mother for not being able to sit with her longer. She said the charting just never ended. I thought about that a lot afterward.⏎⏎So when I see HHS talking about accelerating AI adoption in clinical care, my first reaction is not excitement. It's dread. Because the problem in that hospital room was not that there wasn't enough technology. The problem was that the technology already there had pulled the human beings away from the human being in the bed.⏎⏎I understand that AI is supposed to help with this, that it's supposed to reduce administrative burden and free up clinicians. That's how it gets sold. But I have a hard time believing that adding another layer of automated systems is going to solve a problem that was itself created by adding automated systems. The burden didn't come from nowhere. It came from EHR mandates and billing requirements and compliance documentation that got layered on over years. Why would AI be different.⏎⏎What I'm specifically worried about, since the RFI asks about barriers and risks, is accountability. When an AI system flags a patient as low-risk and they're discharged early and something goes wrong, who is responsible for that. The hospital? The vendor? The physician who technically signed off but was managing fourteen other patients at the time and had forty seconds to review the recommendation? I don't think this question has a good answer right now, and I don't think accelerating adoption before there's a good answer is a reasonable thing to do.⏎⏎There's also something that bothers me that I don't hear talked about much. Medicine is not just information processing. When a doctor sits with a patient and listens, and asks follow-up questions, and notices that the patient seems frightened or isn't telling the whole story, that is not a task that can be delegated to pattern recognition on historical data. I know there will be responses to this that say AI assists rather than replaces. But the pressure will be toward replacement because replacement is cheaper. That is just how healthcare economics work in this country.⏎⏎I'm not a physician or a researcher. I'm a retired school librarian from New Mexico and I probably don't have the technical background to comment on the specific regulatory mechanisms being considered. But I know what I saw in that hospital room. And I know that my mother, who is 81 and has some cognitive decline, would have had no idea if a computer had made a decision about her care. She would have assumed it was her doctor. She trusted her doctor. That trust was built over years of actual conversation.⏎⏎I would ask HHS to think very carefully about whether accelerating any of this is the right direction before there are binding standards for transparency, mandatory disclosure to patients when AI tools were used in their care decisions, and real legal clarity on liability. I don't think those things are in place and I have not seen a convincing argument that they will be in place just because adoption moves faster.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0082♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Miller♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February 2023, and I watched the nurses and doctors make judgment calls that no algorithm could have made. One of the night nurses, who had been there for maybe twenty years, noticed something in how my mother was breathing that wasn't showing up on any monitor. She called the attending at 2am. They adjusted her treatment. My mother came home. I think about that a lot when I read things like this RFI.⏎⏎What concerns me about HHS pushing to accelerate AI adoption is the word "accelerate." Nobody is asking whether we should slow down and actually understand what we're doing. The framing assumes that faster adoption is better adoption, and I don't think that's been demonstrated. The efficiency argument gets made constantly but I keep wondering, efficient for whom? Because from where I sat in that ICU waiting room, the thing that mattered was not efficiency. It was that a human being was paying close attention.⏎⏎I want to address specifically the question HHS asked about what regulatory changes could remove barriers to AI adoption in clinical settings. My answer is that I'm not sure removing barriers is the right goal right now. I know that's probably not what you want to hear. But I've been reading about algorithmic tools being used to approve or deny insurance coverage and the patterns that have come out of investigative reporting on companies like Cigna and UnitedHealth suggest that AI in those contexts has been used to deny claims at scale without meaningful human review. If we accelerate adoption without first establishing hard accountability rules, I think we make that problem worse, not better.⏎⏎There's also something that doesn't get talked about enough, which is what happens to the nurses and the experienced clinicians when institutions decide AI can do more of their work. Workforce questions aren't even really addressed in the RFI summary I read. If a hospital deploys an AI diagnostic tool and then uses that as justification to cut staff or reduce the experience level they're willing to hire for, the safety net that caught my mother's problem doesn't exist anymore. That experienced night nurse is gone. And the AI, whatever its accuracy rate in a controlled study, was not trained on my mother specifically, in that specific moment, with whatever combination of factors made her case unusual.⏎⏎I do understand there are administrative burdens in healthcare that are genuinely crushing. Prior authorization paperwork, documentation requirements, billing codes. I'm not saying AI has zero role anywhere. But clinical decision-making and insurance adjudication are different categories than scheduling software, and I worry HHS is treating them the same way.⏎⏎The RFI asks about reimbursement structures that could encourage AI adoption. I'd ask HHS to think hard before creating financial incentives that make AI use essentially mandatory in practice even if it's technically optional on paper. Hospitals respond to reimbursement pressure. If CMS starts reimbursing differently for AI-assisted versus non-AI-assisted diagnoses, smaller rural hospitals are going to feel that pressure in ways that the big academic medical centers won't, and the patient populations in those rural hospitals are often already the ones who have historically gotten worse care.⏎⏎I don't have a policy credential. I'm just someone whose family went through something serious and came out okay partly because of human judgment that I'm not confident any current AI system could replicate. The burden of proof here should be on demonstrating safety, not on justifying caution.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0083♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patricia♔Young♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔American Autoimmune Related Diseases Association♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Regions Hospital in St. Paul in February of 2023. I watched nurses and doctors make decisions about her care constantly, reading her face, noticing how she reacted when they adjusted her position, picking up on things that weren't on any monitor. She came home. I think about that a lot when I read about HHS wanting to "accelerate" AI in clinical settings.⏎⏎What I want to respond to specifically is the question about barriers to AI adoption, because the framing of that question bothers me. The assumption built into it is that barriers are problems to be removed. But some barriers exist because the thing being blocked is actually dangerous, or at minimum not ready, and calling it a barrier doesn't change that.⏎⏎The accountability problem keeps me up at night more than anything else. When an AI system recommends against a treatment and a patient is harmed, who is responsible? The hospital that bought the software? The company that built the model? The physician who followed the recommendation or the one who didn't? I've read about cases where insurance companies used AI tools to deny post-acute care claims at rates that human reviewers would never have approved, and when patients sued, the legal liability was just... diffuse. Nobody owned the decision. That diffusion of responsibility is a feature for the vendors and a catastrophe for patients.⏎⏎I do understand there are genuine administrative burdens on clinicians. I'm not completely dismissing the efficiency argument. Prior authorization is a nightmare and I've heard from physician friends that documentation takes up half their working hours. But the answer to that problem should be fixing the administrative requirements, not layering AI on top of a broken system and calling it progress. And honestly I worry that once AI is embedded in workflows it becomes very difficult to question because questioning it starts to look like questioning the institution itself.⏎⏎There's also the bias question which I don't think HHS is taking seriously enough based on the way this RFI is framed. Pulse oximetry readings are less accurate on darker skin and it took decades for that to be widely acknowledged. AI systems trained on historical clinical data are going to inherit every disparity baked into that data. Accelerating adoption means accelerating the spread of those disparities before anyone has time to measure what's happening.⏎⏎I think HHS should at minimum require that any AI tool used in direct clinical decision-making undergo prospective clinical trials with demographically stratified outcome data before deployment, and that liability frameworks be established before adoption is encouraged, not after. The current approach seems to be deploy first and figure out the safety questions once the tools are everywhere and the vendors have contracts with every major health system in the country.⏎⏎I keep coming back to my mother's nurses. There was one in particular, a woman who had been doing ICU work for maybe twenty years, who came in at 2am and just looked at my mother and said something was off and started checking things. Nothing in the chart had changed. I don't know what she was reading. I don't think it can be parameterized.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0084♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Wright♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔University of Pennsylvania Perelman School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in March of 2023. I watched nurses and doctors make judgment calls that no algorithm could have made — not because the data wasn't there, but because the data wasn't the point. The point was knowing that the woman in bed 4 was terrified of being alone at night and that her breathing got worse when she was anxious, and that you had to sit with her sometimes, not just adjust the ventilator settings. I don't know how you code that.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS wants to "accelerate" AI adoption in clinical care, my first reaction is honestly just — why the hurry? Accelerate toward what, exactly?⏎⏎I want to focus on one thing the RFI asks about, which is what regulatory actions HHS can take to speed this up. My answer is that HHS should instead be asking what guardrails need to exist before any of this goes further, because right now the accountability question is completely unresolved and I don't think the department is taking it seriously enough.⏎⏎When an AI system recommends against a treatment and a patient doesn't get that treatment and then something goes wrong, who is responsible? The hospital that licensed the software? The vendor? The physician who technically signed off but was working through 40 patients in a shift and trusted the flag? I've read through some of the existing guidance on this and it's genuinely unclear. There's language about "clinical decision support" that seems designed specifically to make it hard to assign liability. And now HHS wants to go faster.⏎⏎The bias problem is also not solved and I wish people would stop talking about it like it's a known, manageable risk. There's published research going back to at least 2019 — the Obermeyer study in Science is the one most people cite — showing that commercial algorithms used in health systems were systematically underestimating the health needs of Black patients because they used cost as a proxy for need. That study was about one algorithm. We don't have a full accounting of how many similar problems exist in systems currently deployed. And the response from the industry has mostly been to say they fixed it, or they're working on it, or that new models are better. That's not the same as proof.⏎⏎I'm also thinking about what this does to the workforce over time. Not the immediate displacement question, though that matters too. More what happens when a generation of clinicians is trained in an environment where the AI is always there suggesting things. Do you end up with physicians who are less capable of independent diagnostic reasoning? I genuinely don't know the answer to that, and I'm not sure anyone does, but it seems like the kind of question you'd want answered before you accelerate anything.⏎⏎There's something else that bothers me that I'm having trouble articulating precisely. Medicine involves a kind of trust that is built on the perception that another human being is responsible for your care and is accountable if something goes wrong. Patients already feel like they're being processed rather than treated. Every time I talk to someone about a hospital experience the word that comes up is "overwhelmed" — they felt like a number. AI doesn't fix that, it probably makes it worse, and yet efficiency gains are being treated as if they're the only variable that matters here.⏎⏎I'm not saying technology has no place in healthcare. That's not my position. But "accelerate" is the wrong frame and the wrong goal right now, and I'd like HHS to explain more specifically who asked for this acceleration and what evidence exists that the risks are adequately understood before we go any further down this road.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0085♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Garcia♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Mercy General in Sacramento in February 2023 with what turned out to be a medication interaction that had been flagged — and then dismissed — by an automated clinical decision support tool her cardiologist's office was using. The tool said low risk. The cardiologist, who was seeing something like 30 patients that day, went with the tool. She spent four days in the ICU. She's fine now, but that's not really the point I'm trying to make here.⏎⏎The point is that when we talk about "accelerating adoption," I don't think HHS has fully reckoned with what it means to speed up something that isn't working consistently yet. Acceleration implies the direction is right and we just need to go faster. I'm not sure the direction is right.⏎⏎I want to focus on one specific thing in the RFI, which is the question about what regulatory changes could remove barriers to AI adoption in clinical care. My concern is that the framing of AI access as something being impeded by regulation gets this backwards. The regulations that exist — informed consent requirements, liability standards, the expectation that a licensed physician is accountable for a clinical decision — those aren't bureaucratic obstacles. They're the structure that makes patients trust that someone is responsible when something goes wrong.⏎⏎And here's what I keep coming back to: if a physician uses an AI tool that recommends a treatment and the patient is harmed, who exactly is liable? The doctor who followed the recommendation? The vendor who built the model? The hospital that deployed it? I've read some of the existing guidance on this and it's genuinely unclear. Accelerating adoption before that question has a real, enforceable answer seems like it benefits vendors and health systems a lot more than it benefits patients.⏎⏎There's also something that bothers me about the workforce side of this that I haven't seen addressed directly in what I've read of the RFI. When a nurse practitioner or a hospitalist or even a specialist starts deferring to AI outputs because the AI is confident and they're busy and the documentation burden is already crushing them — that's a skill atrophy problem over time. The clinician who relied on the tool for five years may genuinely be less capable of practicing without it. I don't know exactly how to quantify that risk but I think it's real and I think it's being ignored because it's not measurable in the short term.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no place in medicine. I use GPS navigation and I know it has made me worse at reading maps. That tradeoff is fine for driving. I'm less sure it's fine for diagnosing a pulmonary embolism at 2am.⏎⏎What I'd ask HHS to do instead of focusing on removing barriers is to require independent, prospective clinical validation before any AI tool can be used in a decision that affects patient treatment or insurance coverage. Not vendor-conducted studies. Independent. And the results should be public, broken down by patient subgroup, so we can actually see whether these tools perform equitably across race, age, and insurance status — because the evidence that they often don't is already out there and has been for several years.⏎⏎I realize this comment is more focused on caution than on the specific regulatory mechanisms HHS asked about. I'm a retired nurse, not a policy attorney, so I can't tell you which specific rules need to change. But I spent 31 years watching what happens when hospitals adopt new systems too fast because of cost pressure or competitive pressure, and the patients who pay the price for those decisions are rarely the ones who get mentioned in the press release announcing the new technology partnership.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0086♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Anderson♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔Edwards Lifesciences♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February of 2023, and I watched her care get managed through three different software dashboards that the nurses openly complained about. One of them told me, quietly, that the sepsis alert system flagged so many false positives overnight that the night staff had started mentally filtering them out. That's not a technology success story. That's alarm fatigue baked into the infrastructure, and you want to accelerate more of this.⏎⏎What bothers me about this whole request for information is the framing. "Accelerating adoption" is the goal before the question of whether adoption is safe has been answered. That's backwards. I understand HHS has to respond to industry pressure and there are real administrative burdens in healthcare that everyone agrees are painful, but the answer to those burdens isn't to hand decision-making over to systems that nobody can fully explain or audit after the fact.⏎⏎I want to focus on the question about what regulatory changes would help AI move faster into clinical care, because I think this is where the biggest risk sits. The assumption embedded in that question is that the regulation is the bottleneck. But the actual bottleneck, or at least a huge part of it, is that we don't have reliable evidence that these tools work safely across diverse patient populations. A study published in JAMA in October 2023 found that several commercially deployed clinical AI tools performed significantly worse on Black patients than white patients, not by a little, not in some narrow edge case, but in ways that would have changed treatment decisions. And those tools were already deployed. Already in use. Accelerating that is not something I can get behind.⏎⏎There's also something that I don't think gets said plainly enough in these proceedings, which is that when an AI system makes a wrong call and a patient is harmed, the accountability disappears into a fog. The hospital says it was the vendor's model. The vendor says the hospital implemented it wrong. The physician says the recommendation came from the system. I've talked to two patient advocates who work in medical malpractice support and they both said the same thing: the legal frameworks are completely unprepared for this, and in the meantime patients who are harmed have very little recourse.⏎⏎My concern isn't that AI has no place in medicine. I think there are probably narrow, well-defined tasks where it helps. Imaging analysis with a radiologist still in the loop, maybe. But the push seems to be toward AI making or heavily shaping decisions in real time, with clinicians who are already overworked and undersupported trying to supervise outputs they weren't trained to critically evaluate.⏎⏎The workforce piece is something I feel like HHS is not taking seriously enough. If you cut administrative costs by replacing billing staff and prior authorization reviewers with AI systems, those are real jobs held by real people, many of them women, many of them in communities that don't have a lot of other options. And that's before you get into what happens to the clinical workforce when AI-assisted care gets used to justify lower staffing ratios.⏎⏎I don't know exactly what the right pace is for any of this. I'm not a technologist. But I know that "accelerate" is the wrong verb when we haven't figured out how to handle the failures that are already happening.⏎⏎The FDA clearance process for these tools is also genuinely confusing to me as someone trying to understand it from the outside. Some of these clinical AI systems seem to be getting through as software modifications to existing cleared devices, which means the scrutiny applied to the original device doesn't necessarily apply to♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0087♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Harris♔♔New Hampshire♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was admitted to a hospital in Tucson in February 2024 with what turned out to be a pulmonary embolism. Before her physician even saw her, an AI triage tool had flagged her as "low acuity" based on her intake vitals. The nurse told us later she almost wasn't prioritized for imaging. A human doctor overrode that assessment. My mother is alive because someone with actual clinical judgment looked at her and thought, that's not right.⏎⏎I keep thinking about that when I read about HHS wanting to accelerate AI adoption. Accelerate. Like the problem is that we're moving too slow.⏎⏎What I want to comment on is really the framing of the whole effort — the assumption baked into this RFI that the main barriers to AI in clinical care are regulatory and reimbursement obstacles that need to be cleared away. That framing worries me more than any specific question in the document. When you structure a public comment process around "how do we speed this up," you've already decided the direction. The question of whether we should be moving this fast, or in some of these directions at all, barely has a place to fit.⏎⏎The safety question is the one that feels most urgent to me, though I'm not a clinician. I've read enough incident reports and coverage of algorithmic failures — the sepsis prediction tools that miss cases in Black patients at higher rates, the insurance prior-authorization systems that deny care based on population-level statistics that don't apply to the individual in front of the doctor — to know that these tools carry real risk and that the risk is not evenly distributed. It tends to land hardest on people who already get worse care.⏎⏎And what happens when the AI is wrong? This is where I get genuinely confused by what HHS seems to be proposing. If a physician uses an AI-recommended treatment pathway and the patient is harmed, who is responsible? The doctor who followed the recommendation? The hospital that deployed the tool? The vendor who sold it? I haven't seen a clear answer to that, and I don't think one exists yet. That ambiguity is not a small problem to sort out after deployment. It's a foundational problem that probably should be resolved before you're talking about loosening barriers to adoption.⏎⏎There's also something that I find hard to articulate without sounding sentimental, but I'll try. Medicine is not just information processing. When a doctor sits with a patient who has just received a serious diagnosis, what's happening in that room is not something an algorithm is doing or could do. The judgment, the weighing of what this particular person values, the relationship that makes trust possible — I'm skeptical that efficiency gains in documentation or billing justify gradually eroding that. And once you build a system where AI is embedded everywhere, it gets very hard to pull back.⏎⏎I'm not saying no AI tools should ever be used in healthcare. I'm not sure I believe that. There are probably narrow, well-defined tasks where the risk profile is genuinely low and the benefit is real. But that's a very different thing from what this RFI seems to be contemplating, which is broad adoption across clinical decision-making, administrative functions, and insurance determinations, all at a pace that outstrips our ability to monitor what's actually happening to patients.⏎⏎The reimbursement piece especially concerns me. If Medicare and Medicaid start paying for AI-assisted care at higher rates than non-AI care, you create a financial pressure that will push adoption regardless of whether individual health systems think the tools are ready. Small rural hospitals in particular will feel that — they don't have the IT infrastructure or the legal and compliance staff to properly evaluate these tools, and they may end up deploying whatever is cheapest or most heavily marketed.⏎⏎I'd ask HHS to at minimum slow down and commission some independent safety evaluations before changing reimbursement structures. Not industry-sponsored research. Independent. The FDA's process for medical devices, imperfect as it is, at least has some structure around demonstrating that a product does what it claims before it goes to market. I'm not sure the equivalent exists here and I haven't seen this RFI grapple seriously with that gap.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0088♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Garcia♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔Susan G. Komen♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at St. Luke's in Kansas City in February of 2023, and what kept her alive was not an algorithm. It was a night nurse named Denise who noticed something was off about how my mother was breathing and called the attending at 2am rather than waiting for the morning rounds. No model trained on population data would have flagged that. Denise knew my mother. That is what medicine is.⏎⏎I want to respond specifically to the question HHS is asking about what regulatory actions could accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, because I think the question itself is the problem. The department is asking how to speed something up without first establishing whether the destination is safe or even correct. That seems backwards to me, though I recognize I'm not a policy expert and there are people who would push back on that framing.⏎⏎What worries me most is the diffusion of accountability. When an AI system recommends a treatment or flags a diagnosis and that recommendation turns out to be wrong, who exactly is responsible? The physician who followed it? The vendor who sold it? The hospital that deployed it? Right now there is no clear answer and I don't see anything in this RFI that takes that problem seriously enough. It gets mentioned and then sort of moved past.⏎⏎There's also the bias problem, which is not theoretical. ProPublica's reporting on the COMPAS algorithm in criminal justice showed years ago what happens when you train a system on historically biased data and then present the outputs as objective. Healthcare data has the same problem, arguably worse, because decades of unequal treatment are baked into the records these models learn from. A system trained on data from institutions that historically undertreated Black patients for pain is going to replicate that undertreatment. Calling it AI does not launder the bias out of it.⏎⏎The reimbursement angle also concerns me. If HHS creates payment incentives for providers to use AI tools in clinical decision-making, smaller practices that can't afford those tools get disadvantaged, and there's pressure on every provider to adopt systems even when clinical judgment says not to. That's not accelerating good medicine. That's just accelerating adoption for its own sake.⏎⏎I keep coming back to the workforce question too. I work in healthcare administration, not clinical care, but I talk to nurses and physicians regularly and the honest answer a lot of them give when asked about AI tools is that they feel pressure to use them and then feel responsible when the outputs don't make sense but they can't easily override them without documentation burdens. The tool is supposed to save time but the workaround when it's wrong costs more time than just doing it manually.⏎⏎HHS should at minimum require rigorous independent clinical validation before any AI tool is eligible for reimbursement incentives, and that validation should include performance data broken down by race, age, sex, and socioeconomic status, not just aggregate accuracy numbers. It should also require clear disclosure to patients when AI has been used in any decision affecting their care. Right now that disclosure is not consistently happening and patients often have no idea.⏎⏎I am genuinely uncertain about some of this. I can see a version of AI-assisted medicine that catches errors humans miss and helps overworked clinicians manage impossible caseloads. That would be good. But that version requires getting the safety and accountability infrastructure right first, and this RFI seems oriented toward moving fast on adoption and building the guardrails later, which in my experience is how♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0089♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Karen♔Jones♔♔California♔♔United States♔Availity♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was admitted to Jefferson University Hospital in Philadelphia in October 2023 with what turned out to be a pulmonary embolism. The triage nurse almost missed it. What caught it was another nurse, an older woman who'd been doing the job for 22 years, who said something felt off and pushed for the CT. No algorithm flagged that. A person did, because she'd seen hundreds of patients and something in how my mother was sitting and breathing didn't match the chart.⏎⏎I think about that a lot when I read things like this RFI.⏎⏎The question HHS seems to be asking is essentially: what can we do to speed this up? And I want to say, respectfully, that I think you're asking the wrong question. The right question is whether we should be speeding it up at all, and I don't see that question anywhere in this document.⏎⏎I work in healthcare administration, not clinical care, so I'm not going to pretend I understand all the technical dimensions here. But I've watched what happens when systems get implemented before they're ready. We rolled out a new prior authorization screening tool at our organization in 2022 and within four months we had documented cases where the tool was recommending denial for patients who clearly met coverage criteria. The vendor said it was a training data issue. Okay. But those were real people waiting for care while we sorted out a training data issue.⏎⏎The framing of this RFI worries me. Words like "accelerating adoption" assume the direction is already settled and we're just figuring out logistics. But there are genuine, unresolved questions about what happens when an AI system is wrong and someone gets hurt. Who is responsible? The hospital? The vendor? The physician who relied on the output? I've asked this to people who work in health IT and I genuinely cannot get a consistent answer. That's not a good sign.⏎⏎There's also something that gets left out of almost every conversation about AI in clinical settings, which is what this does to the workforce. If you degrade the role of nurses and physicians to basically confirming what a machine already decided, you lose the kind of judgment that saved my mother. You can't train that judgment in people who spend their careers supervising dashboards. You develop it through years of actually doing the work, reading patients, being wrong sometimes and learning from it. I don't know how you quantify that in a cost-benefit analysis but I suspect HHS isn't really trying to.⏎⏎I understand there are real problems with physician burnout and administrative burden and I'm not dismissing any of that. If AI can help with scheduling or billing or documentation, maybe that's different, I'm genuinely less certain about those applications. But the RFI doesn't draw those lines clearly. It talks about AI in clinical care as though ambient documentation and diagnostic decision support are basically the same category of thing, and they're not.⏎⏎The bias question also deserves more than a passing mention. There's published research going back to at least 2019 showing that some clinical algorithms perform measurably worse for Black patients than white patients, and the response from the industry has largely been to say they're working on it. That's not sufficient when we're talking about expanding deployment.⏎⏎I would ask HHS to at minimum commission an independent review of safety incidents involving AI-assisted clinical decisions before issuing any guidance that encourages broader adoption. Not a review conducted by the vendors or by organizations with financial stakes in the technology.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0090♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔George♔Torres♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Emory University Hospital in January of 2023, and what kept her alive was not any algorithm. It was a pulmonologist named Dr. Anand who came in at 6 in the morning on a Saturday because something felt off to him. He couldn't explain it in a way that would satisfy a model. He just knew. And he was right.⏎⏎That's what I keep thinking about when I read through this RFI. HHS is asking how to speed up AI adoption, but I don't think I've seen enough honest engagement with the question of what we might be trading away in the process.⏎⏎I want to focus on one thing specifically, which is the use of AI in clinical decision-making, because I think that's where the stakes are highest and where the optimism in this document feels the most unexamined.⏎⏎The efficiency argument is real, I won't pretend otherwise. Physicians are drowning in documentation and prior authorizations and every other administrative burden that has nothing to do with actually caring for patients. If AI can help with that, fine. But this RFI keeps conflating administrative assistance with clinical judgment, and those are very different things. When the document mentions "AI-assisted diagnosis" and "treatment recommendations," it moves into territory that I don't think HHS has fully reckoned with.⏎⏎There's a 2021 study published in Nature Medicine that found dermatology AI models performed worse on darker skin tones at rates that weren't trivial. That wasn't a fringe finding. It's been replicated in radiology and in sepsis prediction models. The data these systems are trained on reflects decades of unequal access to care, which means the outputs will encode those inequities unless someone is actively correcting for them at every stage. Who is doing that? The RFI mentions "bias" almost in passing. That word is doing a lot of work for a problem that is genuinely hard and hasn't been solved.⏎⏎And then there's the accountability question. If an AI-assisted recommendation leads to a missed diagnosis, who is responsible? The physician who followed the recommendation? The hospital that licensed the software? The vendor? Right now the answer is genuinely unclear, and that unclarity isn't just a legal technicality. It changes how physicians behave. If you know that deferring to a tool diffuses your culpability, that changes the decision-making environment in ways that are hard to predict and potentially dangerous.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no place in medicine. I'm saying the framing of this RFI treats acceleration as the obvious goal and caution as the obstacle, and I think that's backwards. The question shouldn't be "what can we do to move faster?" The question should be "what conditions would have to be true before we could trust these systems with consequential decisions about human health?"⏎⏎I don't see that question being asked here. There's a lot about reimbursement pathways and regulatory sandboxes and reducing friction for developers. There's very little about what patients are owed in terms of disclosure when AI is involved in their care, or about whether they have any meaningful ability to opt out.⏎⏎My mother did not consent to being diagnosed by a machine. She consented to being treated by Dr. Anand. That distinction mattered to her. I'm not sure it matters to HHS right now, and that concerns me more than any specific provision in this document.⏎⏎There should at minimum be mandatory disclosure requirements before any further acceleration efforts are considered. Patients should know when AI is involved in their diagnosis or treatment planning. That seems like the floor, not the ceiling, and I can't find a clear commitment to even that in this RFI.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0091♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patricia♔Jones♔♔New York♔♔United States♔University of South Florida Morsani College of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Mercy General in Sacramento in February 2023 with what turned out to be a pulmonary embolism. The ER was using an algorithmic triage tool — I don't know the exact name of it — and her case got flagged as low-to-medium priority based on her vitals and age inputs. The nurse who finally came to check on her did so because she just looked wrong, in the nurse's words. Not because any system told her to. My mother survived. I think about that a lot when I read proposals like this one.⏎⏎What bothers me most about this RFI is that the whole framing assumes the question is how to speed things up, not whether we should. The Department is asking what regulatory barriers are slowing AI adoption, what reimbursement structures would encourage clinicians to use these tools more. But there's almost no weight given to the possibility that the friction is the point. That clinicians are slow to adopt some of these tools because they've seen them fail, or because the tools are built on training data that doesn't reflect their patient populations, or because they don't actually trust a probability score generated by a model they can't examine.⏎⏎There's a real accountability problem here that I don't think HHS has grappled with seriously. When a physician makes a wrong call, there are mechanisms — however imperfect — to understand why, to correct it, to hold someone responsible. When an algorithm contributes to a bad outcome and it's been baked into the workflow as a decision-support tool, who answers for that? The hospital? The vendor? The clinician who technically had override authority but was working a 14-hour shift and deferred to the system recommendation because that's what the workflow incentivized? I've read some of the coverage on deterioration-detection tools like the ones used in a few large health systems and the pattern that comes up over and over is that nurses and physicians start to tune out alerts, not because they're negligent but because the false positive rate is high enough that alert fatigue becomes a genuine clinical risk in itself.⏎⏎I'm not a clinician. I work in healthcare policy research so I'm not a total outsider to this, but I want to be honest that I don't have all the answers on the technical side. What I do feel confident saying is that the Department should not be treating acceleration as a self-evidently good goal. Some of the questions in this RFI feel almost promotional in tone, asking what HHS can do to "remove barriers" without first asking whether those barriers might exist for legitimate reasons.⏎⏎There's also the workforce piece, which I think deserves far more attention than it's getting. If AI tools reduce the time a physician or nurse needs to spend on certain tasks, that sounds good in theory, but in practice what often happens is that staffing gets cut to match the new efficiency expectation, and then when the tool fails or produces a questionable output, there aren't enough experienced humans in the room to catch it. I've seen this pattern described in skilled nursing facilities specifically, where administrative AI tools have been used to justify thinner staffing ratios.⏎⏎I'd ask HHS to seriously consider whether a moratorium on further reimbursement incentives for AI clinical decision tools — at least until there's a more robust post-market surveillance framework in place — might actually serve patient safety better than the current trajectory. The FDA's oversight of software as a medical device is still catching up. Meaningful transparency requirements on training data and model performance by subgroup don't really exist in any enforceable form yet that I can find.⏎⏎I'm genuinely uncertain what the right pace is. But "faster" as a default answer worries me.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0092♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Brown♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔American Physical Therapy Association♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Mercy General in Sacramento in February 2023 with what turned out to be a pulmonary embolism. The first physician who saw her — a resident, overworked, clearly exhausted — almost missed it. But he didn't miss it, because a nurse who had been doing this for twenty-two years looked at my mother and said something was wrong. Not a data point. Not a flag in a system. A human being who looked at another human being and knew.⏎⏎I keep thinking about what would have happened if that nurse had been trusting an AI triage tool instead of her own eyes.⏎⏎HHS is asking how it can accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. My honest answer is that this is the wrong question. The right question is whether AI belongs in these decisions at all, and nobody seems to want to have that conversation seriously.⏎⏎I want to focus specifically on the question of accountability, because I don't think the RFI addresses this honestly. When an AI tool recommends against a course of treatment and a physician follows that recommendation and a patient is harmed — who is responsible? The vendor? The hospital that licensed the software? The doctor who technically still signed off? Right now there is no clear answer, and hospitals are adopting these tools anyway, and patients are not being told when algorithmic recommendations are shaping their care. I find this genuinely alarming. It's not a hypothetical governance gap. It's a liability black hole that everyone is pretending doesn't exist while talking about efficiency gains.⏎⏎There's also the bias problem, which gets mentioned a lot but not seriously engaged with. The training data for most clinical AI tools reflects decades of unequal care. Black patients have historically been undertreated for pain. Women's cardiac symptoms have been systematically underrecognized. If you train a model on that history and then deploy it at scale, you're not building toward better medicine, you're just automating the old mistakes and making them faster and harder to see. A researcher at Stanford published findings in 2021 showing that a widely used algorithm for kidney function estimation was systematically underestimating disease severity in Black patients. That algorithm had been in clinical use for years before anyone looked carefully. That should give everyone pause.⏎⏎What bothers me most is the framing of acceleration itself. The word assumes the direction is correct and we just need to move faster. But the workforce implications alone — nurses, medical coders, radiologists, the entire ecosystem of people whose expertise is being quietly repositioned as redundant — those deserve a real reckoning before we move faster. My sister-in-law is a medical coder and she's been told her position is being eliminated next year because the hospital is rolling out AI documentation tools. She has seventeen years of experience catching errors that affect patient billing and, more importantly, patient records. I don't know what the error rate on those tools is. Nobody told her. Nobody told the patients either.⏎⏎I understand there are legitimate uses. Remote monitoring for patients in rural areas, flagging images for radiologist review, some administrative functions. I'm not saying none of this has value. But "accelerating adoption" as a policy goal, without first establishing mandatory disclosure to patients, without clear liability frameworks, without serious bias auditing requirements, is putting efficiency ahead of safety in a domain where that tradeoff is measured in lives.⏎⏎The comment period for this RFI closes and HHS will compile responses and probably move forward anyway. I just want it on record that some of us think the pace itself is the problem, not a symptom of excessive caution.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0093♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Brown♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at St. Luke's Medical Center in Milwaukee in February 2023 with what turned out to be a pulmonary embolism. The triage nurse almost sent her home. What changed that was a physician who had a bad feeling about her breathing and ordered a CT angiogram against what I later learned was the recommendation of the risk-stratification software the hospital was using at the time. That software, which I was never told existed until I asked months later, had flagged her as low-risk. She would have died in the parking lot.⏎⏎I'm not telling that story to be dramatic. I'm telling it because HHS keeps framing this RFI as though the main question is how to remove barriers to AI adoption, and I think that framing is backward. The question should be whether we have adequate evidence that these tools are safe enough to accelerate in the first place, and right now I don't think we do.⏎⏎What worries me specifically about the reimbursement angle in this RFI is the obvious incentive problem. If CMS starts reimbursing AI-assisted clinical decision support at favorable rates, hospitals will adopt it because of the money, not because they've done rigorous evaluation of whether it works for their patient population. We saw something like this with electronic health records after HITECH — massive adoption driven by financial incentives, followed by years of complaints about interoperability failures and physician burnout, and we're still sorting that out. I don't see why AI would be different. The technology moves faster and the failure modes are less obvious.⏎⏎There's also the question of who gets harmed when these systems fail. The research on racial and ethnic bias in clinical AI is not new at this point. The Obermeyer study from 2019 in Science showed that a widely used algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs, which is not the same thing at all. That was five years ago. I have not seen convincing evidence that the industry has solved this problem. If HHS accelerates adoption without mandatory bias auditing requirements tied to reimbursement, we are going to scale up those disparities rather than address them.⏎⏎And I don't think HHS has really grappled with the accountability question. When an AI system contributes to a bad outcome, who is responsible? The physician who relied on it? The hospital that bought it? The vendor who sold it? Right now that's genuinely unclear, and physicians know it's unclear, and some of them are going to start practicing defensively by just following what the algorithm says rather than exercising independent judgment, because that's the legally safer path. That's not a hypothetical. That's basic institutional behavior.⏎⏎I do want to be honest that I'm not opposed to AI in medicine categorically. I think there are narrow applications, particularly in image analysis and certain screening contexts, where the evidence base is actually decent. But the RFI asks about accelerating adoption broadly across clinical care, and "broadly across clinical care" includes a lot of high-stakes, high-variability situations where the evidence base is thin or nonexistent.⏎⏎The RFI asks what regulatory actions HHS could take to support AI adoption. My answer is that before you take actions to accelerate adoption, you need a mandatory post-market surveillance framework with real teeth, not voluntary reporting. The FDA's current approach to software as a medical device has significant gaps, and without knowing what's actually going wrong in real deployments, any acceleration is essentially a large uncontrolled experiment conducted on patients who have no idea they're in it and never consented to be.⏎⏎I don't know if HHS is in a position to require that kind of surveillance framework given jurisdictional questions, but that seems like the thing to figure out before the reimbursement incentives go into place, not after.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0094♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Jones♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔American Occupational Therapy Association♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Mercy General in Sacramento in February 2023 with what turned out to be a pulmonary embolism, and the thing that caught it was her nurse noticing she looked "off" — not pale exactly, just wrong in a way that prompted extra questions. No algorithm flagged her. The admitting system had actually generated a low-risk score based on her vitals at intake. I think about that a lot when I read materials like this RFI.⏎⏎The question HHS is essentially asking is: what can we do to speed this up? And I understand why that framing is appealing. There are real inefficiencies in healthcare delivery. Nobody is disputing that administrative burden is a problem or that rural access is genuinely terrible in many parts of the country. But speeding up AI adoption as a response to those problems conflates two things that are not actually related. The inefficiency problem is mostly a structural and payment problem. Layering AI on top of a broken system does not fix the system, it just makes the broken parts move faster and harder to audit.⏎⏎What worries me most is the accountability question, and I don't think the RFI takes it seriously enough. When an AI system contributes to a wrong diagnosis or a denied claim and someone is harmed, who is responsible? Right now the answer is genuinely unclear. Vendors disclaim liability through licensing agreements. Physicians who relied on a tool may or may not bear malpractice exposure depending on how the tool was positioned to them. Hospitals are caught in the middle. Patients have essentially no recourse that is specific to the AI failure as distinct from the general medical malpractice framework, which was not designed for this at all.⏎⏎I work in patient advocacy and I've talked with families in the last year where an insurer's prior authorization algorithm denied coverage for treatments that the treating physician considered medically necessary. In several of those cases the denial language was clearly templated and bore no relationship to the specifics of the patient's condition. When families appealed, the process was slow enough that the window for effective treatment had closed before a human reviewer ever looked at the file. I'm not saying AI caused those outcomes definitively. I'm saying AI made it much easier for those denials to happen at scale, with less friction, and with less human accountability at each step.⏎⏎HHS asks specifically about regulatory actions that could accelerate adoption. My answer is that the regulatory priority should be the opposite: mandatory transparency about where AI is being used in clinical and coverage decisions, patient rights to request human review of any AI-influenced decision, and real liability clarity before any further acceleration happens. I'd also want to see independent post-market surveillance requirements with actual teeth, not voluntary reporting. The FDA's approach to software as a medical device has been slow and inconsistent and I don't think ONC has the capacity to fill that gap without significant additional resources and authority.⏎⏎There's also a workforce dimension that this RFI sort of glosses over. If AI tools are positioned as replacing the judgment work that experienced nurses and physicians do, we are going to lose institutional knowledge that took decades to build and cannot be reconstructed from training data. The nurse who caught my mother's PE was a 22-year veteran. That kind of pattern recognition is not in any dataset I'm aware of.⏎⏎I keep coming back to the basic question of what medicine is for. And I don't think the answer is efficiency.⏎⏎I realize that's not a regulatory proposal exactly. But the framing of this RFI bothers me because it treats acceleration as the default desirable outcome and asks only how to get there faster, rather than asking first whether the destination makes sense for patients. The burden of proof should be on demonstrating safety and accountability before scaling, not after.⏎⏎One more thing: the comment period for this RFI closed — or was supposed to close — in a relatively short window given the scope of what's being asked. That timeline itself is a problem. These are not narrow technical questions.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0095♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Rivera♔♔Pennsylvania♔♔United States♔HCA Healthcare♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent 11 days in a cardiac step-down unit at St. Francis Hospital in Hartford in February of this year. During that time, three different nurses mentioned to her that a new "AI monitoring system" had flagged her vitals as stable when she was actually in the early stages of fluid overload. It was a night-shift nurse who noticed — because she looked at my mother and thought something seemed off — who caught it. That nurse had 22 years of experience and she said the monitor had been showing green across the board.⏎⏎I keep thinking about what HHS is actually asking here. The question in this RFI about removing regulatory barriers to accelerate AI adoption — I want to be direct about this — presupposes that the barriers are the problem. What if they're not? What if the barriers exist because we haven't figured out how to make these systems safe yet?⏎⏎The thing that worries me most isn't even the safety failures, though those are serious. It's the accountability question. When an AI system recommends a course of treatment and a physician follows it and the patient is harmed, who is responsible? I've asked this to several people who work in health policy and I have not gotten a satisfying answer from any of them. The physician? The hospital that deployed the system? The vendor who built it? The answer matters enormously and it seems like HHS wants to speed up adoption before anyone has worked that out.⏎⏎There's also something that gets lost in all the talk about efficiency. Medicine is not primarily an efficiency problem. There are parts of it that are, sure — scheduling, billing, prior authorizations — and I'm not opposed to automation in those areas in principle. But clinical judgment involves something that I don't think can be reduced to pattern matching on training data, which is what these systems actually do underneath. A physician who knows a patient over years, who notices that this person always minimizes pain and so when they say "it's a 4" they probably mean a 7 — that's not in any dataset.⏎⏎The bias issue is also not getting enough attention in discussions like this one. We know from published research, including a 2019 study in Science on a widely deployed hospital algorithm, that these systems can encode racial disparities from historical data and then reproduce them at scale. Accelerating deployment before we have mandatory bias auditing requirements in place seems like it would make this worse, not better.⏎⏎I don't think HHS should halt all research or even all deployment. I understand that's probably not realistic and there are specific applications where the evidence is reasonably strong. But "accelerating adoption" as a policy goal, before we have clear liability frameworks, before we have mandatory post-deployment monitoring requirements, before we have genuine transparency about how these systems make decisions — that order of operations seems backward to me.⏎⏎One thing I haven't seen addressed anywhere in this RFI is what happens to the clinical workforce. If AI systems handle an increasing share of diagnostic reasoning, what happens to the training of physicians who will need to exercise that reasoning when the system fails or is unavailable or is simply wrong? There's a deskilling risk here that I think deserves serious study before we go further, and I'm not sure who is looking at it.⏎⏎My mother is fine, by the way. She went home after her fluid overload was treated. But she went home because a human being looked at her.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0096♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Ramirez♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Mercy General in Sacramento in February 2023 with what turned out to be an atypical presentation of pulmonary embolism. The attending physician — a woman who had been practicing for over twenty years — caught it because she noticed something off about the way my mother was describing the fatigue. Not a number, not a scan result. A description. I think about that constantly when I read things like this RFI.⏎⏎What HHS is asking here, essentially, is how can we move faster. And I understand there are real pressures — physician burnout, documentation burden, all of it is real. But "accelerate adoption" as the starting premise of this whole inquiry worries me. That framing assumes the destination is correct and the only question is speed. I'm not sure the destination is correct.⏎⏎The question I kept returning to while reading through the RFI is: who is accountable when an AI-assisted clinical decision goes wrong? Not abstractly, but practically. If a prior authorization algorithm denies coverage for a medication and the patient deteriorates, which specific person at which specific organization is responsible for that outcome? I don't think we have a good answer to that right now, and moving faster doesn't get us closer to one. It actually moves us further from it, because by the time there's enough evidence of harm to act on, the systems will be deeply embedded in workflows that hospitals and insurers have built their operations around.⏎⏎I want to be honest that I'm not a clinician. I work in social services and I've seen a fair amount of how algorithmic tools get used in human services contexts — eligibility determinations, risk scoring for child welfare, things like that. And the pattern I've seen, over and over, is that when an algorithm produces an output, it becomes very hard for a frontline worker to override it even when their instincts say something is wrong. Not because the system won't allow it technically, but because overriding the system creates documentation burdens and sometimes exposes the worker to second-guessing. So in practice, the algorithm's judgment becomes the default judgment. I have no reason to think clinical settings would be different, and some reason to think they'd be worse, because the stakes are higher and the time pressure is greater.⏎⏎There's also the bias question, which I know gets raised a lot, but I don't think it's been taken seriously enough in the framing of this RFI. Pulse oximetry — a technology that's been around for decades — has documented accuracy disparities for patients with darker skin tones, and it took until 2022 for the FDA to even issue updated guidance on that. If we can't address bias in a simple optical sensor over forty years, I am genuinely not confident that we'll catch it in a complex neural network used for diagnostic support before significant harm has accumulated in the population that gets harmed.⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to adoption, but I'd push back on that framing. Some of what looks like a "barrier" is actually appropriate caution. Clinicians who are hesitant about AI tools may be hesitant for good reasons that are hard to articulate in a survey. Slowing down to understand that hesitancy is not an obstacle to good policy, it's part of what good policy requires.⏎⏎I don't think I'm arguing AI has no place in healthcare at all. Probably there are narrow, well-defined applications where it performs reliably and the failure modes are understood and the accountability structures are clear. But I don't think we're close to that being true broadly, and I'd rather HHS spend the next few years building the evidentiary foundation and the accountability infrastructure than spend them trying to accelerate adoption of tools that haven't earned that level of trust yet. The reimbursement incentives in particular concern me because once payment is structured around AI-assisted care, the financial pressure to use these tools regardless of whether they're appropriate for a given patient becomes very hard to♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0097♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Scott♔♔Delaware♔♔United States♔American Academy of Hospice and Palliative Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Mercy General in Sacramento in March 2023 with what turned out to be a fairly complicated presentation of heart failure overlapping with a kidney issue, and the attending physician spent probably forty minutes with her going over her history, her medications, her fears about the prognosis. That conversation mattered enormously. She felt heard. I keep thinking about what that interaction would look like if half the doctor's attention is being directed by an algorithm flagging risk scores, suggesting discharge timelines, nudging toward whatever the AI determined was the statistically optimal care path.⏎⏎I'm not against technology in medicine in some blanket way. That would be silly. But what HHS is asking about here seems different. Accelerating adoption means moving fast, and moving fast in clinical settings has a very specific kind of downside that you don't get in, say, logistics software.⏎⏎The question I want to address is the one about what regulatory actions HHS can take to support AI adoption in clinical care. My answer, honestly, is that the department should be asking the opposite question first. What are the minimum safety thresholds that must be met before these tools get anywhere near a patient? Because right now I don't think those thresholds exist in any meaningful way. The FDA has cleared a number of AI diagnostic tools under pathways that weren't designed for software that updates itself or that performs differently across patient populations. There's a study from 2021 by Obermeyer and colleagues that showed a widely used commercial algorithm was systematically underestimating the health needs of Black patients because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs. That tool was already deployed across hundreds of hospitals before anyone published that finding. That's the timeline we're talking about.⏎⏎The workforce piece bothers me too and I don't think HHS is treating it seriously. If administrative AI reduces billing and coding staff, or diagnostic AI changes how many radiologists a hospital needs, those are real jobs held by real people. And I'm not sure efficiency gains for hospital systems translate into anything good for patients. Hospitals might just pocket the margin.⏎⏎There's also this diffusion of accountability problem that I don't see getting addressed. When an AI system recommends a treatment and the physician follows it and the patient is harmed, who is responsible? The vendor will say the doctor made the final call. The doctor will say they were following validated clinical decision support. The hospital will say they implemented the tool according to specifications. Patients are left with no clear path. That's not a hypothetical, that's already happening in smaller ways with existing tools, and the more we accelerate adoption the worse that problem gets before anyone figures out a legal framework for it.⏎⏎I'd want HHS to at minimum require prospective clinical trials with demographically diverse populations before any AI tool can be used in diagnosis or treatment recommendations, not retrospective validation on whatever data the vendor happened to have. And meaningful informed consent so patients know when AI is involved in their care. Right now most patients have no idea.⏎⏎One more thing and then I'll stop. The framing of this RFI bothers me. "Accelerating adoption" is in the title. The conclusion is already embedded in the question. HHS is asking how to go faster, not whether to go faster, and not asking who gets to decide whether we should be going anywhere at all. I'd expect a little more♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0098♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Jackson♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was hospitalized at Piedmont Atlanta Hospital for six days in November 2023 with a cardiac event, and watching what the nursing staff had to do just to get basic information about her care was exhausting even from a visitor's standpoint. So I'm not against technology in hospitals, I want to be clear about that. What I'm against is the direction this RFI seems to be pointing, which is toward making AI adoption faster, not better.⏎⏎The question I keep coming back to is who is actually responsible when an AI system gets something wrong. And I don't mean responsible in a vague sense, I mean legally, practically, concretely responsible. If a physician misreads a scan, there's a process. It's imperfect and often unfair to patients too, but there is a chain of accountability. When a hospital deploys a sepsis prediction algorithm that flags the wrong patient or misses the right one, the answer always seems to be that the doctor was still technically in the loop. But was she really in the loop if she saw a dashboard recommendation and had 4 minutes to review it before moving to the next room? I don't think that's meaningful oversight and I don't think HHS should pretend it is.⏎⏎The efficiency argument for AI in clinical settings frustrates me because it assumes the bottleneck in American healthcare is physician decision-making speed. It isn't. The bottleneck is that there aren't enough primary care doctors in rural areas, there aren't enough nurses anywhere, and there are people who can't afford to go to the doctor regardless of how fast the AI processes their chart. Speeding up the decisions that do get made doesn't fix any of that.⏎⏎There's also something I think gets glossed over in discussions like this, which is that the training data for most of these clinical AI systems reflects decades of documented disparate treatment of Black patients, women with pain complaints, patients with disabilities. The FDA has cleared quite a few of these tools without requiring any demographic breakdown of how they perform across different populations. I saw a paper from the Lancet Digital Health in 2022 that looked at this directly and found significant performance gaps across racial subgroups for several commercially deployed tools. HHS accelerating adoption before that problem is solved seems backwards to me.⏎⏎I understand there's pressure to do something about physician burnout, about administrative burden, about the genuine inefficiencies in documentation. I'm not dismissing that. But the answer to documentation burden is better documentation systems, not AI that listens to every patient encounter and generates a note. That raises real questions about what patients feel comfortable saying to their doctor when they know a microphone is on. I know some people say patients can opt out, but can they really opt out if their doctor says this is how we do notes now?⏎⏎I guess the specific thing I'd want HHS to address before accelerating anything is mandatory post-market surveillance requirements for clinical AI tools, with public reporting broken down by patient demographics and care setting. Right now that's essentially voluntary and the incentives for vendors to report poor performance just aren't there.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0099♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Scott♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔Boston University School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother had a stroke in February 2023. She was at Mercy Hospital in Des Moines and the nursing staff caught something was wrong with her medication interaction because one of the nurses knew her, had been her nurse before, remembered that she reacted badly to a particular blood thinner. That's the thing nobody wants to say out loud in these discussions about AI in clinical care: that moment of recognition required a person who had built up knowledge of another person over time. No algorithm was going to do that.⏎⏎What bothers me about this whole request for information is the framing. "Accelerating adoption" is the goal before anyone has really answered whether adoption is appropriate at the scale being discussed. The question isn't whether AI can be useful in some narrow imaging analysis task or flagging drug interactions in a database. Maybe it can. The question is whether pushing it into clinical decision-making broadly, and into insurance coverage decisions especially, is something we should be racing toward.⏎⏎On the insurance side I feel pretty strongly that this is where the most immediate harm is happening right now. I've read about UnitedHealth's nH Predict tool being used to deny post-acute care to Medicare Advantage patients, and there was litigation around this, a class action filed in 2023, arguing the algorithm was overriding physician recommendations at very high rates. Whether or not the legal outcome is favorable, the underlying problem is real. When an algorithm denies a claim and the physician appeals, who is responsible? The company points to the algorithm, the algorithm has no face, and the patient is stuck waiting. That diffusion of accountability is not a hypothetical risk. It is already happening.⏎⏎HHS asks about what regulatory actions would help. What I'd actually want to see is the opposite of acceleration. I'd want to see mandatory pre-deployment clinical trials for any AI system used in diagnosis or treatment recommendations, trials with the same rigor we require for drugs. I'd want to see a moratorium on AI-driven insurance denials until there's an actual independent audit process. Not a self-reported one.⏎⏎The workforce piece worries me too, though I admit I'm less certain about exactly how this plays out. I understand there are physician shortages in rural areas and that AI might help with some of that. I don't want to be dismissive of that argument. But the way these tools get deployed in practice, from what I've seen and read, is that they get used to justify reducing staff, not to extend care to people who don't have it. Those are very different things and I don't think HHS's framing accounts for that distinction.⏎⏎I keep coming back to something basic. Medicine is not primarily an information processing problem. It is a relationship between a person who is suffering and a person who has committed to helping them. Some of what makes that work is judgment that can be somewhat systematized. A lot of it can't. And I think when we treat "efficiency" as the primary value, we end up optimizing for the wrong thing entirely, which eventually means patients get worse care even if they get faster care.⏎⏎I don't have a tidy answer for where exactly the line should be drawn on what AI can appropriately assist with. But I am very confident the line should be drawn somewhere, and that HHS drawing it at "let's go faster" is the wrong starting point. The burden of proof should be on demonstrating safety and equity outcomes before scaling, not after. The post-market surveillance systems we have for medical devices are already underfunded and slow and those are for physical products with clearer failure modes than♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0100♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Miller♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔WellCare Health Plans♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother spent eleven days in the ICU at Mercy General in Cincinnati in February 2023. She had a sepsis complication after a routine hip replacement. What I remember most from that experience is not any machine or monitor. It's Dr. Anand Krishnamurthy sitting on the edge of her bed at 11 at night, holding her hand, explaining what her kidneys were doing and why they were scared but not giving up yet. That is not something a model trained on clinical notes can do. I don't think it can even approximate it.⏎⏎So when I see HHS asking how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, my honest reaction is that we are asking the wrong question. The question shouldn't be how fast can we do this. The question is whether we've even agreed on what we're trying to accomplish, and who bears the cost when it goes wrong.⏎⏎I want to respond specifically to the part of this RFI that asks about reimbursement structures for AI-assisted care. The assumption embedded in that question bothers me. It treats reimbursement as the bottleneck, as if the only reason hospitals aren't deploying more AI is that nobody's paying for it yet. But some of the fastest-moving AI deployments in healthcare right now are in prior authorization and claims denial, and those are already getting paid for just fine. Insurance companies don't need CMS to create a billing code to run a denial algorithm. They're already doing it. And patients are already being harmed by it. There was a report out of ProPublica in 2023 documenting how United Healthcare's nH Predict algorithm was systematically denying post-acute care to elderly patients in ways that their own clinicians disagreed with. That's not a hypothetical risk. That happened.⏎⏎The workforce piece also concerns me in ways I haven't seen this RFI address directly. If AI tools are reducing the time physicians spend on documentation, that sounds good on paper. But what happens to that time? In most health systems I've read about, it doesn't go back to the patient. It gets absorbed into higher patient volume expectations. The doctor sees more people per day, has less time per encounter, and the AI note is fine but the relationship is thinner. I'm not sure that's an improvement in care even if it's an improvement in throughput.⏎⏎I also want to say something about accountability that I think gets glossed over in these conversations. When an AI system contributes to a diagnostic error or a harmful treatment decision, who is responsible? The vendor? The hospital that licensed the tool? The physician who clicked accept on the recommendation? Right now there is no clear answer to that and I don't think accelerating deployment before we have an answer is wise. It's not like we can just unwind it once systems are embedded in EHR workflows and clinicians have atrophied the habits the AI is now doing for them.⏎⏎I recognize there are probably genuine benefits in some narrow applications. Radiology image screening for certain conditions, flagging medication interactions, maybe some population health stuff. I'm not saying AI has no place in medicine. But "accelerating adoption" as a policy goal assumes that the current pace is the problem, and I'm not convinced of that at all. The pace of adoption is probably fine. What's missing is the governance, the liability framework, the patient consent infrastructure, and frankly the humility to admit that a lot of these tools have not been tested in populations that look like my mother or Dr. Krishnamurthy's other patients in that ICU.⏎⏎The FDA's current framework for software as a medical device is already struggling to keep up with iterative AI updates. ONC's information blocking rules don't really address what happens when an AI system is the one deciding what information surfaces to a clinician. These are not solved problems and I would ask HHS to spend more time on them before♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
